1 NAME Eugenius the Younger
2 GIVN Eugenius the Younger
2 NICK Eugene III
1 NAME Eugenius II of Toledo
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE 13 NOV 657
1 OCCU Bishop of Toledo; Spanish writer and poet
1 TITL Saint
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eugenius_II_of_Toledo '''Saint Eugenius II'''] (died 13 November 657), sometimes called Eugenius the Younger as the successor of Eugenius I, was Archbishop of Toledo from 647 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is called Eugenius secundus (Eugene the second) in the biography of Archbishop Julian of Toledo by a certain Felix, but in later histories he is sometimes numbered Eugenius III when a legendary martyr and first bishop of Toledo
2 CONC  is included.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Eugenius was the son of a Goth named Evantius, became a cleric in the cathedral of Toledo. Until 646 he was the archdeacon of Braulio of Zaragoza. At the death of Archbishop Eugenius of Toledo in 647, Eugenius the Younger was sele
2 CONC cted as his successor. Braulio petitioned the king to let him retain his archdeacon, but the king refused, saying that his choice of the young Eugenius was inspired by God. The office was so little to his taste that he fled to Zar
2 CONC agoza to lead a monastic life, but was forced to return to Toledo by King Chindaswinth and take up the government of that see.[2] Inexperienced in church government, Eugenius II would consult with Bishop Braulio. He also attended 
2 CONC the eighth synod of Toledo, called by Chindaswinth in December of 653, and a General synod in December 656.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Though of small stature and feeble health he was a zealous prelate. He undertook the reform of the ecclesiastical chant of the Divine Office and achieved distinction as a writer of prose and poetry.[4]
2 CONT Eugenius II died 13 November 657. His feast is kept on 13 November.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Works
2 CONT
2 CONT His poems, though lacking polish and elegance, are full of fire, spirit, and poetic movement. His thought is solid, fertile, and gives evidence of a well-trained mind.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eugenius left two books in prose and verse, containing his poems on religious and secular subjects, his recension of the poem of Dracontius on "The Six days of Creation", to which he added a "Seventh Day", and a letter to King Chi
2 CONC ndaswinth explaining the plan of the entire work. He also edited the metrical "Satisfactio" of Dracontius, an account of the writer's misfortunes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of this work Bardenhewer says that it "underwent a substantial revision at the hands of Eugenius II, Bishop of Toledo, in keeping with the wish of the Visigothic King Chindaswinth; not only were the poetical form and the theology 
2 CONC of the poem affected by this treatment, but probably also its political sentiments. It is this revision that was usually printed as Dracontii Elegia, until the edition of Arévalo (Rome, 1791, 362-402, and 901-32) made known the or
2 CONC iginal text".
2 CONT
2 CONT He also wrote a treatise on the Trinity probably against the Arian Visigoths. Ferrera mentions a letter of Eugenius to the king and one to Protasius, the Metropolitan of Tarragona, promising if possible to write a mass of St. Hipp
2 CONC olytus and some festal sermons, but disclaiming the ability to equal his former productions.[4]
2 CONT Eugenius left a scathing epitaph for King Chindaswinth, who died in 653.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT # Tommaso Stancati, ed.: Julian of Toledo, Prognosticum Futuri Saeculi (Mahwah, NJ: The Newman Press, 2010), p. 478, note 91.
2 CONT # Julianus of Toledo. The Story of Wamba, CUA Press, 2005 ISBN 9780813214122
2 CONT # Von Hefele, Karl Joseph. A History of the Councils of the Church, T. & T. Clark, 1895
2 CONT # McNeal, Mark. "Eugenius II (the Younger)." The Catholic Encyclopedia. Vol. 5. New York: Robert Appleton Company, 1909. 2 Apr. 2015
2 CONT # Constable, Olivia Remie and Zurro, Damian. Medieval Iberia, University of Pennsylvania Press, 2012 ISBN 9780812221688
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT * Jacques Sirmond, Opera (Venice, 1728), II, 610;
2 CONT * Patrologia Latina, LXXXVII, 347-418;
2 CONT * Ferrara, History of Spain, ad ann. 647-658;
2 CONT * Gams, Kirchengesch. Spaniens (1874), II, 2, 132-35;
2 CONT * Michaud, Biog. Univ. (Paris, 1826).
2 CONT * Catholic Encyclopedia article
2 CONT * San Eugenio III" of Toledo," in Enciclopedia Rialp, http://www.mercaba.org/Rialp/E/eugenio_iii_de_toledo_san.htm
2 CONT * Iranzo Abellán, Salvador. "San Eugenio de Toledo". Spanish biographical dictionary of the Royal Academy of History. RAH. < [https://dbe.rah.es/biografias/9131/san-eugenio-de-toledo link] >
