2 CONT Williswint, daughter of Count Adelheim, a widower in 764.  Heiress of lands in the Wormsgau.  Married 730 to Rutpert (Robert) I, duke in the Haspengau, count in the Upper Rhine and Wormsgau, Royal Missus in Italy; b. 689; d. by 76
2 CONC 4.
2 CONT
2 CONT Williswint founded, 12 July 764, with her eldest son, Count Cancor, the Imperial Monastery at Lorsch, near Worms.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/FRANCONIA.htm#Robertdiedbefore764B
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Chapter 7.    GRAFEN im WORMSGAU
2 CONT
2 CONT A.      Die ROTBERTINER
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A geographical description of the pagus Wormatiensis during Carolingian times is found in the Acta Academiæ Theodoro-Palatinæ, compiled principally on the basis of donations made to Lorsch during the 9th and 10th centuries[500]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ROBERT [I], son of LAMBERT [II] comes in Neustria and Austrasia & his wife --- ([700/10]-before 764).  The third continuator of the Gesta Abbatum Trudonensium names "Robertus comes vel dux Hasbanie" in 715, and quotes a charter da
2 CONC ted 7 Apr 742 under which "Robertus comes, filius condam Lamberti" donated property "in villa…Sarcinio…in pago Hasbaniensi…[et] Halon, Scaffnis, Felepa et Marholt" to St Trudon[501].  Comte de Hesbaie.  Comes palatinus 741/42.  Gr
2 CONC af im Oberrheingau und Wormsgau. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([730]) WILLISWINDA, daughter of ADALHELM Grundherr im Wormsgau & his wife ---
2 CONT
2 CONT The primary source which confirms her parentage, and that her husband was named Robert, has not yet been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert [I] & his wife had [three] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT *1.         CANCOR (-771).  m ANGILA, daughter of --- (-after 1 Jun 770).  Cancor & his wife had [five] children:  
2 CONT ** a)         HEIMRICH [Heimo] (-killed in battle Lüne an der Elbe 5 May 795). ... Abbot of Mosbach 784.    
2 CONT ** b)         [CHRODGANG [Hruotgang] (-after [772]).  "Heimericus" names "germano suo domino Ruodgango archiepiscopo" in an undated charter relating to a donation to Kloster Lorsch[519].  "Karolus…rex Francorum", in an undated cha
2 CONC rter (placed in the compilation with charters dated 772) related to Kloster Lorsch, names "avia…Heimerici…Williswinda vel genitor suus Cancor, germano suo domino Ruodgango archiepiscopo"[520].  Archbishop, although his archbishopr
2 CONC ic has not yet been traced.  Other sources name Chrodgang Archbishop of Metz, son of Sigramnus and his wife Landrada (see the document CAROLINGIAN NOBILITY).  It appears beyond coincidence that two archbishops named Chrodgang exis
2 CONC ted at the same time.  It is possible therefore that all the sources refer to the same person and that "germanus" in the Lorsch sources should be interpreted more broadly than "brother".]  
2 CONT ** c)         ERMBERT (-803).  "Rachildis" donated property "in pago Wormatiense in Dinenheimer marcha" to Lorsch, for the souls of "…germani mei Heimerici atque Erimberti episcopi", by charter dated 31 Oct 792[521].  The Chronico
2 CONC n Laureshamense records the donation "in Dinenheim et Sunnincheim" dated "792 prid Kal Nov" by "Rachildis…pro remedio animæ…et germani mei Heimrici atque Ermberti episcopi"[522].  Bishop of Worms 770.  
2 CONT ** d)         RACHILT (-after 1 Nov 792).  "Rachildis" donated property "in pago Wormatiense in Dinenheimer marcha" to Lorsch, for the souls of "…germani mei Heimerici atque Erimberti episcopi", by charter dated 31 Oct 792[523].  
2 CONC The Chronicon Laureshamense records the donation "in Dinenheim et Sunnincheim" dated "792 prid Kal Nov" by "Rachildis…pro remedio animæ…et germani mei Heimrici atque Ermberti episcopi"[524].  Nun at Lorsch.  
2 CONT ** e)         EUPHEMIA .  The primary source which confirms her parentage has not yet been identified.  776.  Nun at Lorsch.  
2 CONT *2.         [ANSELM .  Europäische Stammtafeln[525] shows Anselm (-killed in battle Roncesvalles 15 Aug 788), comes palatii, as the son of Robert [I] but the primary source on which this is based has not yet been identified.   ...
2 CONC   The epitaph of “Aggiardi” records his death “XVIII Kal Sep”[529], which suggests that the three were killed at the first battle of Roncesvalles, dated 15 Aug 778.]    
2 CONT *3.         THURINGBERT (-Jun 770 or after).  "Cancor et Angila conjux mea" donated property "in illa marcha de Bisistat…de dote Angilæ" [Birstatt] to Lorsch by charter dated 1 Jun 770, signed by "Thurincberti fratris ipsius Cancr
2 CONC onis et Heimerici filii sui"[530]. m ---.  The name of Thuringbert's wife is not known.  Thuringbert & his wife had one child:
2 CONT **a)         ROBERT [II] (-12 Jul 807).  "Turincbertus et filius meus Rotbertus" donated property "in pago Rinensi in villa…Birstat" to Lorsch by charter dated Jun 770[534].  Graf im Wormsgau und Oberrheingau 795/807.  Herr zu Die
2 CONC nheim 795.  [same person as…?  RADBERT (-807).  Einhard records that "Radbertus missus imperatoris" died in 807 after returning from the Orient[535].  It is possible that "Radbertus" refers to "Rodbertus".] 
