1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT Merovingian king of Soissons from 511 and of the whole Frankish kingdom from
2 CONT 558, who played an important part in the extension of Frankish hegemony.
2 CONT Arvet Soissons-riket, men la after hvert under seg hele Frankerriket.
2 CONT Chlotar shared in the partition of his father's kingdom in 511, receiving the
2 CONT old heartlands of the Salian Franks in modern northern France and Belgium.
2 CONT After the death of his brother, Clodomir, in 524, he murdered his nephews and
2 CONT shared the kingdom of Orl‚ans with his two remaining brothers, Childebert I
2 CONT and Theodoric I. The deaths without heirs of the latter's grandson,
2 CONT Theodebald, in 555 and of Childebert in 558 brought all the Frankish lands
2 CONT finally under Chlotar's sway. Chlotar's principal campaigns were against the
2 CONT Burgundians in 523 (with Childebert and Clodomir) and 532 to 534 (when he and
2 CONT Childebert finally imposed Frankish rule), against the Visigoths in 532 (with
2 CONT Theodoric) and 542 (with Childebert), and against the Thuringians in circa 531
2 CONT (with Theodoric); he married the Thuringian princess, Radegunda. Chlotar was
2 CONT ruthless and brutal, and the family rivalries and sometimes open hostilities
2 CONT in which he figured prominently were characteristic of Merovingian history; in
2 CONT 560 he even had his rebellious son, Chram, together with Chram's family, put
2 CONT to death.
1 NOTE [FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT    Also spelled Chlodomer, Merovingian king of Orléans from 511.  Theeldest son of Clovis I by Clotilda, Clodomir shared in the fourfoldpartition of his father's kingdom in 511, receiving lands in western andcentral France; his wa
2 CONC s the only one of the four kingdoms to form asingle geographical unit on both sides of the Loire River. In 523, withhis two full brothers, Chlotar I and Childebert I, as allies, he attackedhis eastern neighbours, the Burgundians; 
2 CONC their king, Sigismund, wascaptured and put to death together with his family. In the followingyear, Clodomir resumed the attack, this time with his half-brother,Theodoric I, but was killed at the battle of Vézeronce, where kingGod
2 CONC omer, Sigismund's brother, was his opponent. Two of Clodomir's youngsons were then murdered by Chlotar and Childebert, who shared his landswith Theodoric. A third son, Chlodovald, escaped, to enter the religiouslife and found the 
2 CONC monastery at Paris which bears his name (Saint-Cloud).
2 CONT
2 CONT To cite this page: "Clodomir" Encyclopædia Britannica
2 CONT  <http://www.britannica.com/eb/article?idxref=464437>
1 NOTE Koning van het Frankische deelrijk met als hoofdstad Soissons (november 511). Koning van het Frankische deelrijk met als hoofdstad Orleans (526). Koning van Bourgondië (534). Koning der Franken met als hoofdstad Parijs (23-12-558 
2 CONC tot 10-11-561). Hij heeft zeven echtgenotes gehad en talloze concubines.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chlothar I (-561) (Lotharius I) was een zoon van Clovis I. Na de dood van zijn vader wordt het rijk opgesplitst tussen Chlothar en zijn 3 broers die elk een koningstitel krijgen. Chlothar vestigt zich in Soissons en wordt in 558, 
2 CONC na de dood van zijn 3 broers, alleen koning over het hele Frankische rijk. In 523/524 viel hij met zijn broers Chlodomer en Childebert Bourgondië binnen. Choldomer sneuvelde bij een volgende veldtocht en z'n weduwe Guntheuca huwde
2 CONC  met Chlotharius. Chlotharius liet vervolgens 2 kinderen van Choldomer vermoorden, het derde werd priester. Hij liet ook z'n eigen zoon Chramn wegens opstandigheid executeren. In 541 viel hij met z'n broer Childebert Spanje binnen
2 CONC , een veldtocht die mislukte. In 561 overlijdt hij en wordt het rijk verdeeld tussen zijn 4 zoons, Sigibert I, Chilperik, Charibert I en Guntramnus (Gontram) van Bourgondië
1 NOTE "According to a tradition accepted at the Court of Charlemagne, Saint Arnulf, who had been appointed Bishop of Metz by royal favour, was descended from the family of Ferreolus, one of the great houses of the most Romanised provinc
2 CONC e of Gaul - the Narbonnaise.  His father, Ansbert, who was of senatorial rank, had married Blithilda, one of the daughters of Clotar I.  We have already observed that Saint Begga, the mother of Pepin d'Heristal, was the daughter o
2 CONC f a Gallo-Roman.  Thus the family into which Charlemagne was to be born had as much Roman and Gallo-Roman as German blood in its veins; ..."
2 CONT
2 CONT Clovis II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference No. 2 lists this individual as "Clovis II."
2 CONT
