1 NOTE Source: THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry, chart page 61.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 66:
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.    CHILPèRIC I,  539-584, received the Kingdom of Soisson, by then called Neustria.  Internal dissension began again.  There was civil war, a burning of tax records and killing of tax collectors.  Uprisings were severely punish
2 CONC ed by CHILPEèIC whose men even tortured as ring leaders a number of priests in Limoges.  Yet he wrote Masses, hymns and a treatise on the Trinity.  When he d. by assassination there was further juggling, temporarily abated during 
2 CONC the rule of his son CHLOTAIRE II.   CHILPèRIC  mar. (3) FRèDEGONDE, 543-97, his first wife's maid, after she engineered the death of his second.  FRèDEGONDE was one of the most bloodthirsty women in history.  She murdered all who 
2 CONC stood in her
2 CONT way even after her husband's death.  CHILPèRIC himself was constantly at war with his brother, King Siegbert of adjacent Austrasia; it was FRèDEGONDE who had him assassinated.
2 CONT
2 CONT !Availability: The libraries of Ken, Karen, Kristen, Kevin, Brian, Amy, Adam and FAL.
1 NOTE Source: THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry,  chart: page 61.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 65:
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  CHILPèRIC II, a. 473-killed bef. 493, Burgundian king.  An Arian christian, he mar. a Catholic and had two Catholic daughters.  After gundobad killed him, he had the widow drowned and drove the two daughters in humiliating exi
2 CONC le.  One was CHLOTILDE.
2 CONT
2 CONT !Availability: The libraries of Ken, Karen, Kristen, Kevin, Brian, Amy, Adam and FAL.
