1 NAME Remismund, king of the Suevi
1 NAME rey de los Suevos
2 GIVN
2 SURN
2 NSFX rey de los Suevos
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 440
1 DEAT
2 DATE 469
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Remismund
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Remismund''' (or Rimismund) (died 469) was the Suevic king of Galicia from c. 464 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Isidore of Seville, Remismund was a son of Maldras.[1]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Remismund's early career was spent as an ambassador between Galicia and Gaul, which trip he made several times.[2] After an interregnum of approximately four years (460–464), during which the Sueves who had previously recognised M
2 CONC aldras as king were led by Frumar and those who had recognised Framta followed Rechimund while both their leaders fought for the throne, Remismund, returning from one of his embassies, succeeded in having himself recognised as kin
2 CONC g of a unified Suevic people.[2] This occurred after Frumar's death, but scholars are not certain of the significance of that statement.[2] Had Frumar become sole king? Or did Remismund initially succeed Frumar only over part of t
2 CONC he Suevic nation? Furthermore, Remismund is sometimes identified with Rechimund.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Remismund was confirmed in the kingship when the Visigothic monarch, Theodoric II, sent him gifts, including weapons, and a Gothic princess for a wife.[3] The involvement of Theodoric in the succession of Remismund has, however, b
2 CONC een exaggerated by Jordanes, who claims that after the Gothic king put down the revolt and usurpation of Aioulf, he allowed the Suevi to elect a king of their own, and they chose Remismund.[3] In 466, on the authority of Hydatius,
2 CONC  Theodoric sent an envoy, Salla, to the court of Remismund.[4] Remismund may have sent one Palagorius, a noble Galician, as an envoy to Theodoric, but it is possible that Palagorius went on a private mission.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 465 he sacked Coimbra or Conímbriga and in 468 destroyed it, plundering the goods of a noble family called the Cantabri.[6] In 469 the city of Lisbon was betrayed to the Suevi by a native Roman named Lusidius. Also in 469 Remis
2 CONC mund began negotiations with the Roman Emperor Anthemius through a large embassy of Sueves led by Lusidius.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 466 he requested an Arian missionary from the Gothic court and received Ajax, a Gaul or Galatian, who converted the Suevic nobility and established an Arian church in Galicia.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Genealogy 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/VANDALS,%20SUEVI,%20VISIGOTHS.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT '''REMISMUNDO''' (-469).  He enjoyed closed relations with the Visigoths after his marriage and was recognised as REMISMUNDO King of the Suevi in Spain in 465 by Theoderic II King of the Visigoths.  Isidor's Historia Gothorum, Wan
2 CONC dalorum, Sueborum records that, after the death of Frumario, "Remismundus" succeeded as king of the Suevi in Spain[97].  The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that “Remismundus” united the Suevi in 465 after the death of “Frumar
2 CONC io”[98].  He rejected the Visigoth alliance in order to expand Suevi territories and captured Lisbon in 468[99].  After the death of King Remismundo, there are no contemporary sources which chronicle the succeeding kings of the Su
2 CONC evi until 561, which may indicate that their territory enjoyed a period of peaceful isolation from the rest of the Iberian peninsula[100].  [101]m ([465])''' ---, a Visigoth'''.  The primary source which confirms her origin and ma
2 CONC rriage has not yet been identified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Remismund cites 
2 CONT ** Arias, Jorge C. "Identity and Interactions: The Suevi and the Hispano-Romans." University of Virginia: Spring 2007.< [https://web.archive.org/web/20071025144342/http://people.virginia.edu/~jca4w/Jorge%20Arias%20-%20Identity%20a
2 CONC nd%20Interaction%20The%20Suevi%20and%20the%20Hispano-Romans.pdf PDF] >
2 CONT **Thompson, E. A. Romans and Barbarians: The Decline of the Western Empire. Madison: University of Wisconsin Press, 1982. ISBN 0-299-08700-X.
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_the_Suebi
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Galician_monarchs
2 CONT * Medlands - KINGS of the SUEVI in SPAIN 411-585 https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/VANDALS,%20SUEVI,%20VISIGOTHS.htm 
