1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====Wikitree: [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Bourgogne-423 Bodilon (Bourgogne) de Bourgogne (abt. 595)]'''
2 CONT * '''Biography'''
2 CONT ** "Bodilon (also Bodilo or Bobilo) is the supposed husband of Sigrada and father of St Leodegar, the Bishop of Autun and would have lived in the late sixth and early seventh centuries, in the latter years of the Merovingian Dynas
2 CONC ty.
2 CONT ** "As such he appears in a number of secondary sources about St Leodegar, such as Catholic Encylopedia and various Wikipedia articles. See the French version or English version.
2 CONT ** "However Bodilon is not named as his father in any early versions of the Life of St Leodegar. Neither of the two Passio Leudegarii; Passio Leudagarii A, probably written between 680 and 692, or 680 to before 684, soon after Leu
2 CONC dagar's death, and Passio Leudegarii B, perhaps written by Ursinus in the late seventh or mid eighth centuries[1] mention his father or mother by name."
2 CONT ** "There is a list of biographies of St Leodegar on this website [https://www.stleger.info/bibliographie/culte.htm Eléments pour une étude sur la diffusion du culte de saint Léger] by Roger Gazeau 1971 with links and even some of
2 CONC  the later biographies still state that the name of this father is unknown, although they do start to name Sigrada as his mother."
2 CONT * '''Bodilon in 675'''
2 CONT **"There is a mention of a Merovingian noble named Bodilon or Bodilo in the records for 675 during the reign of Childeric II. He was whipped by Childeric and as a result raised a rebellion with other nobles; Ingobert and Amalbert,
2 CONC  and assassinated Childeric, his wife Bilichilde and their eldest son Dagobert. This is dated to between 18 October and 10 November 675, in the forest of Lorges, near Chelles.[2]
2 CONT ** "This occurred the year after St Leodegar was imprisoned by Childeric II for his objections to Childeric's marriage to his cousin Bilichilde.[3]
2 CONT ** "It is tempting to connect the two events, but we must consider that Bodilon would have been about 80 years old, and it is unsure if he was still alive or capable of leading a revolt."
2 CONT * '''Research Notes'''
2 CONT ** "There are no reliable sources for the current parents of Bodilon or for him being the father of most of his children. More research needs to take place to see if their existence is uncertain, or if they are 'real' but have bee
2 CONC n connected to Bodilon incorrectly."
2 CONT * '''Sources'''
2 CONT ** 1. Jamie Kreiner, "The Social Life of Hagiography in the Merovingian Kingdom", Vol. 96 ''Cambridge Studies in Medieval Life and Thought'', 4th series, Cambridge University Press, 2014. p. 245. Sections available in Google Books
2 CONC .
2 CONT ** 2. Christian Settipani & Van Kerrebrouck, Patrick, « La préhistoire des Capétiens 481-987: Premiere parte Mérovingiens, Carolingiens et Robertiens », Volume 1, of ''Nouvelle histoire généalogique de l'auguste maison de France''
2 CONC , Villaneuve d'Ascq, 1993. p. 131.
2 CONT ** 3. Contributeurs de Wikipédia, "Childéric II," [https://fr.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Child%C3%A9ric_II&oldid=151868931 Wikipédia, l'encyclopédie libre], 
2 CONT *** MacErlean, A. (1910). "St. Leodegar". In ''The Catholic Encyclopedia''. NY: Robert Appleton Company. Retrieved November 19, 2015 from New Advent.
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>————————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
