1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sigrada of Alsace (French: Sigrade d'Alsace; died c. 679 AD) was a Franco-Burgundian countess and mother of Ss. Warin,[4][5] and Leodegar,[6] and grandmother of St. Leudwinus.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Hagiographies tend not to mention where she was born, but given that she is popularly known as Sigrada of Alsace, she was probably Alsatian.[8] She was from the Syagrii family of Gallo-Roman Patricians. Her brother was Bishop Dido
2 CONC  (also called Desiderius) of Poitiers. She married Count Bodilon of Poitiers [it] and gave birth to Warin and Leodegar in Autun, Saône-et-Loire, Burgundy. Through Warin, who inherited the County of Poitiers, she became the ancesto
2 CONC r of the Franco-Lombard dynasty of the Widonids (also called the Lambertiners).
2 CONT
2 CONT She sent Warin to be educated at the court of Chlothar II,[9] while she arranged for Leodegar to be educated under her brother Dido's tutelage.[4] Leodegar quickly rose to prominence as an archdeacon and priest-monk responsible fo
2 CONC r a major Benedictine reform. He caught the attention of the nobility and became embroiled in the complex politics of Merovingian partition. His political stances were used as a pretext by his rival Ebroin to begin persecuting him
2 CONC  and his family, including Sigrada.[9][5] She was shut up in the monastery of Notre-Dame de Soissons by Ebroin.[2] She had all her property taken away and received a letter describing all the tortures her sons were subjected to.[2
2 CONC ] She died shortly after both her sons were martyred.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/FRANKSMaiordomi.htm#BerswindaMAthicus
2 CONT
2 CONT Two siblings, parents not known: 
2 CONT *1.         DIDO (-after 673).  Bishop of Poitou.  … The Passio Leudegarii names "Didone…Pectavi urbe episcopo" as "avunculo" of "Leodegarius urbis Agustedunensis episcopus"[503]. 
2 CONT *2.         [sister] SAINT SIGRADA.  The Chronicon Ebersheimense names "Didone Pictavensi…episcopo" when recording that he was "avunculus" of "sanctum Leudegarium"[504], which would indicate that Leudegar's mother was Dido's siste
2 CONC r, assuming that "avunculus" is used in its strict sense of maternal uncle.  m ---.  Three children: 
2 CONT **a)         LEUDEGAR (-murdered 676).  
2 CONT **b)         GARINUS [Warin] (-murdered 676).  
2 CONT **c)         daughter CHILDESWINTHA.  m ---.  [Two] children: 
2 CONT ***i)          [daughter .  The sources cited below under Berswinda state that she was "sororem…regina", but it is not known to which queen this may refer.  m --- King of the Franks.] 
2 CONT ***ii)         BERSWINDA .  The Cronica Hohenburgensis records the marriage of "Athicum seu Adalricum" and "Berswindam…filiam sororis sancti Leodegarii, sororem videlicet regina"[511].  The Chronicon Ebersheimense names "Berswinda
2 CONC m, filiam sororis Leodegarii episcopi…et Garini comitis Pictavensis, sororem videlicet regina" as the wife of Athicus[512].  m ADALRICUS [Athicus], son of LEUDESIUS maior domus & his wife --
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Léger_d%27Autun French Wikipedia] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Léger d'Autun or Léodegard (in Latin Leodegarius) is the son of Bodilon von Thurgau and Sigarde of Neustrie, daughter of Ansoud of Neustrie known as of Dijon, who becomes Saint Sigarde.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother has as her brother the bishop of Poitiers Dido; and her sister Béreswinthe, wife of Athalric, Duke of Alsace and mother of Saint Odile1.
2 CONT
2 CONT Léger's brother, Warin/Guérin de Vergyn 1 and Poitiers, is also a powerful lord of Burgondie. Victim of the same Ebroin for the same reasons as Léger, he died as a martyr before his brother 2.
2 CONT
