1 NOTE Ferdinand I
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=cf365328-ad72-4328-b540-be07979753d9&tid=261097&pid=-824041224
1 NOTE 867789476. Konge Ferdinand I SANCHOSSON av Castilla-Leon was born in 1017. He was a Konge in 1035 in Kastiljen. (20627) han var en dyktig hersker, som forbedret de gamle vestgotiske lover, reformerte klostertukten og utvidet Kasti
2 CONC ljens makt betydelig. He was a Keiser in 1056.(20628) Over deler av Portugal og det nuværende Ny-Kastiljen utbredte han sin makt og antok 1056 keisertitel for å betegne sin overhøyhet over de andre spanske fyrster. Dermed kom han
2 CONC  i strid med keiser Henrik III av Tyskland. Men han ødela selv Kastiljens førerstilling ved å dele sitt rike mellom sine tre sønner Sancho, Alfons og Garcia, fanske som hans far hadde gjort. Med Sancha fikk han landene mellom Pis
2 CONC egra og Cea. He died on 27 Dec 1065. (20629) He was married to Dronning Sancha (Beata) ALFONSDTR av Leon og Kastiljen in 1032.
1 NOTE Fernando I De Castilla
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=8d3f42c0-bfe5-41b7-9940-d09757cd2c54&tid=10145763&pid=-579037729
1 NOTE Fernando I De Castilla
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=8d3f42c0-bfe5-41b7-9940-d09757cd2c54&tid=10145763&pid=-579037729
1 NOTE "El Grande" REY FERNANDO I DE CASTILLA Murió in 1065 in León, España.. Rey de Castilla de León y de Navarra. Hijo segundo de Sancho III, tuvo que luchar a la muerte de este, contra Bermudo III de León, que fue vencido y muert
2 CONC o en Támara (1037). Confirmó y perfeccionó las leyes de los Godos, conquistó parte de Navarra a su hermano García IV y, aprovechando el desmembramiento del Califato de Córdoba, hizo a los moros una guerra encarnizada, someti
2 CONC endo a su soberanía a los emires de Zaragoza y Toledo
1 NOTE He ruled from 1035 to 1065. He became KIng by conquering Leon and Asturias in 1037. In 1056 he declared himself Emperor of Spain, having disposed of his cousin Garcia in 1054.
1 NOTE Ferdinand I (1017 - 24 June 1065), called the Great (El Magno), was the Count of Castile from his uncle's death 1029 and the King of León, through his wife, after defeating his brother-in-law in 1037. He was the son of Sancho III
2 CONC  of Navarre and Mayor of Castile, and is usually recognised as the first King of Castile. He had himself crowned Emperor of Spain in 1056.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand was barely in his teens when García Sánchez, Count of Castile, was assassinated by a party of exiled Castilian noblemen as he was entering the church of John the Baptist in León, where he had gone to marry Sancha, sis
2 CONC ter of Bermudo III. In his role as feudal overlord, Sancho III of Navarre nominated his younger son Ferdinand, born to the deceased count's sister Mayor, as successor, and further arranged for Ferdinand to marry García'a intended
2 CONC  bride, Sancha of León.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his father's death, Ferdinand continued as count of Castile, now recognizing the suzerainty of his brother-in-law Bermudo III, but they fell out and on 4 September 1037 Bermudo was killed in battle with Fernando at Tamarón. Fe
2 CONC rdinand took possession of León by right of his wife, who was the heiress presumptive, and the next year had himself formally crowned king of León and Castile. He overran the Moorish section of Galicia, and set up his vassal as 
2 CONC count in what is now northern Portugal. With northern Iberia consolidated, Ferdinand, in 1039, proclaimed himself emperor of Hispania. The use of the title was resented by the Emperor Henry III and Pope Victor II in 1055 as implyi
2 CONC ng a claim to the headship of Christendom and as a usurpation of the Roman Empire. It did not, however, mean more than that the sovereign of León was the chief of the princes of the Iberian peninsula. Ferdinand's brothers García
2 CONC  V of Navarre and Ramiro I of Aragón opposed his power, but were both killed in ensuing battles, leaving Ferdinand preeminent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand died on the feast of Saint John the Baptist, 24 June 1065, in León, with many manifestations of ardent piety, having laid aside his crown and royal mantle, dressed in the robe of a monk and lying on a bier covered with 
2 CONC ashes, which was placed before the altar of the Basilica of San Isidoro. At his death, Ferdinand divided up his kingdom between his three sons: Sancho, who received Castile; Alfonso, who received León; and Garcia, who received Ga
2 CONC licia. His two daughters each received cities: Elvira received Toro and Urraca received Zamora. By giving them his dominion, he wanted them to abide by the split in the kingdom and respect his wishes. However, Sancho (born 1032), 
2 CONC being the oldest, believed that he deserved more of the kingdom, and therefore sought to gain possession of the divided parts of the kingdom that had been given to his siblings.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica, Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
1 NOTE Conde de Castilla (1029-1035)
2 CONT I Rey de Castilla (1035-1065)
2 CONT Rey de León (1037-1065)
2 CONT A la muerte de su cuñado Bermudo (o Vermudo) III de León, quedaron reunida lascoronas de León y Castilla, a las que se agregó Asturias. 
2 CONT Una vez rey de Castilla, introdujo notables modificaciones en la legislación goda, que culminaron en el Concilio de Coyanza (1050), donde se reguló también la administración judicial y la vida de los eclesiásticos.  Realizó tambié
2 CONC n fructíferas campañas para extender sus dominios.  Conquistó las tierras del sur del Ebro pertenecientes a su hermano García IV, de Navarra; aprovechando las disensiones que minaba el califato de Córdoba, combatió a os moros y co
2 CONC nquistó Badajoz y otras comarcas vecinas y sometió a su vasallaje a los emires de Zaragoza y de Toledo en 1057.  En 1064 se rindió Coimbra, llegando su dominio hasta el Mondego, y poco después marchó con sus ejércitos hasta Valenc
2 CONC ia, siendo ésta la primera vez que los castellanos pudieron llegar hasta el Mediterráneo.  Sintiendo seriamente lesionada su salud, decidió regresa a León, donde murió crisitanamente, en medio de la consideración de sus súbditos, 
2 CONC que le dieron elcalificativo de Magno.  Sus estados fueron repartidos entre sus hijos Sancho, Alfonso y García.
