1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the [https://www.geni.com/people/of-the-Thuringians/6000000190987627850 daughter] of https://www.geni.com/people/Hermanfried-king-of-the-Th%C3%BCringians/6000000013363233248 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wikipedia page on Rodelinda:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rodelinda_(6th_century)
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodelinda was a Lombard queen who lived in the 6th century. She was the wife of king Audoin and mother of king Alboin.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the first wife of Audoin, regent for the infant king of the Lombards Walthari from 540 to 546/547 and king in his own right from 546/547 to an uncertain date after 552, and gave him a son, his successor Alboin.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT When the marriage took place is unclear. the contemporary Procopius speaks of a marriage arrangement between Audoin and an unnamed sister of Amalafrid, a prince of mixed royal Ostrogothic and Thuringian stock. The bethrothment had
2 CONC  been organized by the Byzantine Emperor Justinian at a date spanning from c.540 to 552, and this unnamed female may be the Rodelinda named by Paul the Deacon; but it has been objected by the Prosopography of the Later Roman Empir
2 CONC e that it is not certain that the marriage Procopius speaks of eventually took place. A major difficulty added to this is that in 552 Alboin was already a warrior. The PLRE believes the marriage between Audoin and Rodelinda took p
2 CONC lace in the 530s.[2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Other scholars instead tend to accept the identification, observing its importance in linking the Lombard kings with the bloodline of the Amali, the royal house of the Ostrogoths. Such a marriage would have made Audoin a legitimat
2 CONC e heir to both the Ostrogothic and Thuringian thrones, the latter in particular as Audoin was already half-brother of the last king of the Thuringians, thus putting him in opposition to the Franks who had took over most of the Thu
2 CONC ringian lands.[4][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Another explanation of Rodelinda's origins is given by István Boná: according to him she was probably a Bavarian princess, to be considered distinct from the Thuringian princess Audoin was engaged to later on.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Hungary Kings:
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/HUNGARY.htm#Audoin
2 CONT
2 CONT AUDOIN 547-560, ALBOIN 560-572
2 CONT
2 CONT [---] . (Unknown)  King of the Pissa. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m MENIA ---.  The Historia Langobardorum names "mater…Audoin…Menia uxor fuit Pissæ regis"[149].  This wording suggests that "Pissæ regis" was not the father of Audoin, presumably Menia's second husband.  It is assumed that "Pissæ"
2 CONC  indicates that he was king of a tribe of that name. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [King of the Pissa] & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.   AUDOIN (-in Pannonia 560).  
2 CONT *The Historia Langobardorum names "Audoin ex genere…Gausus" and his mother "Menia uxor…Pissæ regis"[150].  
2 CONT *He was installed as AUDOIN King of the Lombards in Hungary in [547] in succession to King Walthari.  The Origo Gentis Langobardorum records that "Auduin" reigned after Walthari, specifying that he brought the Lombards into Pannon
2 CONC ia and, in a later passage, stating that they remained in Pannonia for 43 years[151].  Byzantium encouraged the Lombards to consolidate their position in Pannonia by granting them the city of Noricum and other strongholds, though 
2 CONC it is reported that they celebrated by raiding Dalmatia and Illyricum[152].  
2 CONT *The war with the Gepids, which started in [547], was settled by a peace treaty imposed by Emperor Justinian in 552, under which the Lombards sent troops to Italy to help Narses rout the Ostrogoths[153].  
2 CONT *The Historia Langobardorum records that Audoin died in Pannonia[154].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly RODELINDA [Roddenda], daughter of ---.  
2 CONT *The Origo Gentis Langobardorum names "Roddenda" as mother of "Albuin filius [Auduini]"[155].  The Historia Langobardorum names "Rodelenda" as mother of Alboin[156].  Paulus Diaconus names "Rodelindam" as wife of Audoin and mother
2 CONC  of Alboin[157].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly --- of the Thuringians, daughter of HERMINAFRID King of the Thuringians & his wife Amalaberga the Ostrogoth.  
2 CONT *Procopius records that "Amalafridus, vir Gotthus, ex filia nepos Amalafridæ sororis Theoderici Gotthorum regis et filius Hermenefridi regis Thoringorum…sororem eius” married "Anduino Langobardorum regi"[158].  The Codex Theodosia
2 CONC nus records that the daughter of Amalaberga became the second wife of King Audoin[159].  
2 CONT
2 CONT King Audoin & his first wife had [two] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)  ALBOIN (-murdered 28 Jun 572).  
2 CONT *The Origo Gentis Langobardorum names "Albuin" as son of "Auduin"[160].  Paulus Diaconus names "Alboin, filius Audoin" when recording his succession[161].  He succeeded in 560 as ALBOIN King of the Lombards in Pannonia.  He was cr
2 CONC owned ALBOIN King of the Lombards in Italy at Milan in [570]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         [---.  m ---.]  (or father of...) GISULF .  
2 CONT *Shield-bearer of Alboin King of the Lombards, who installed him as duke in the region of Friuli after the Longobard migration into Italy in [569][162].  Paulus Diaconus records that King Alboin installed "Gisulfum…suum nepotem" a
2 CONC s "ducem…[in] Foroiulanæ civitati"[163].  The Chronicle of Andreas Bergomatis records that Alboin conceded Friuli to "nepoti sui Gisolfi"[164].  The precise relationship between Gisulf and King Alboin is unknown and may have been 
2 CONC more remote than implied by "nephew" if the word nepos if translated strictly in these passages. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT *[149] Historia Langobardorum Codicis Gothani 5, MGH SS rer Lang I, p. 9. 
2 CONT *[150] Historia Langobardorum Codicis Gothani 5, MGH SS rer Lang I, p. 9. 
2 CONT *[151] Origo Gentis Langobardorum 5, MGH SS rer Lang I, p. 4. 
2 CONT *[152] Procopius, III 33, cited in Christie, N. (1998) The Lombards (Blackwell, Oxford), p. 35. 
2 CONT *[153] Christie (1998), p. 36. 
2 CONT *[154] Historia Langobardorum Codicis Gothani 5, MGH SS rer Lang I, p. 9. 
2 CONT *[155] Origo Gentis Langobardorum 5, MGH SS rer Lang I, p. 4. 
2 CONT *[156] Historia Langobardorum Codicis Gothani 5, MGH SS rer Lang I, p. 9. 
2 CONT *[157] Pauli Historia Langobardorum I.27, MGH SS rer Lang I, p. 68. 
2 CONT *[158] Procopius, Vol. II, De Bello Gothico IV.25, p. 593. 
2 CONT *[159] Mommsen, T. (ed) (1954) Codex Theodisianus Vol 1 (2nd edn. reprint, Berlin), VII 8.5, p. 328, cited in Wolfram (1998), pp. 320 and 470. 
2 CONT *[160] Origo Gentis Langobardorum 5, MGH SS rer Lang I, p. 4. 
2 CONT *[161] Pauli Historia Langobardorum I.23, MGH SS rer Lang I, p. 61. 
2 CONT *[162] Christie (1998), pp. 76-7. 
2 CONT *[163] Pauli Historia Langobardorum II.9, MGH SS rer Lang I, p. 77. 
2 CONT *[164] Andreæ Bergomatis Chronicon 1, MGH SS III, p. 232. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rodelinda_(6th_century) cites 
2 CONT
