1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfonso_I_de_Arag%C3%B3n
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso I de Aragón, el Batallador (c. 1073 – Poleñino, Huesca, 7 de septiembre de 1134)[1] fue rey de Aragón y de Pamplona entre 1104 y 1134.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hijo de Sancho Ramírez (rey de Aragón y de Pamplona entre 1063 y 1094) y de Felicia de Roucy, ascendió al trono tras la muerte de su hermanastro Pedro I. Destacó en la lucha con los musulmanes, llegando a duplicar la extensión del
2 CONC  reino de Aragón tras obtener la conquista clave de Zaragoza. Temporalmente, y gracias a su matrimonio con doña Urraca gobernó sobre Castilla, haciéndose llamar entre 1109–1114 «Rey y Emperador de Castilla, Toledo, Aragón, Pamplon
2 CONC a, Sobrarbe y Ribagorza», lo que duró hasta que la oposición nobiliaria forzó la anulación del matrimonio. Los ecos de sus victorias traspasaron fronteras; en la Crónica de San Juan de la Peña, del siglo XIV, podemos leer: «clamab
2 CONC anlo don Alfonso batallador porque en Espayna non ovo tan buen caballero que veynte nueve batallas venció». Sus campañas lo llevaron hasta las mismísimas Córdoba, Granada y Valencia y a infligir a los musulmanes severas derrotas e
2 CONC n Valtierra, Cutanda, Cullera y otros sitios.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso the Battler
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso I (1073/1074[1] – 8 September 1134), called el Batallador, the Battler or the Warrior, was the king of Aragón and Navarre from 1104 until his death in 1134. In 1109, he took up the title of his father-in-law: Imperator tot
2 CONC ius Hispaniae. He was the second son of King Sancho Ramírez and successor of his brother Peter I. Alfonso the Battler won his greatest successes in the middle Ebro, where he expelled the Moors from Zaragoza in 1118 and took Egea, 
2 CONC Tudela, Calatayud, Borja, Tarazona, Daroca, and Monreal del Campo. He died in September 1134 after an unsuccessful battle with the Moors at the siege of Fraga.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT His earliest years were passed in the monastery of Siresa, learning to read and write and to practise the military arts until the tuition of Lope Garcés the Pilgrim, who was repaid for his services by his former charge with the co
2 CONC unty of Pedrola when Alphonso came to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his brother's reign, he participated in the taking of Huesca (the Battle of Alcoraz, 1096), which became the largest city in the kingdom and the new capital. He also joined El Cid's expeditions in Valencia. His father gave 
2 CONC him the lordships of Biel, Luna, Ardenes, y Bailo.
2 CONT
2 CONT A series of fortunate deaths put Alfonso directly in line for the throne. His brother's children, Isabel and Peter (who married María Rodríguez, daughter of El Cid), died in 1103 and 1104 respectively.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Matrimonial conflicts
2 CONT
2 CONT A passionate fighting-man (he fought twenty-nine battles against Christian or Moor), he was married (when well over 30 years and a habitual bachelor) in 1109 to the ambitious and Urraca of Castile, widow of Raymond of Burgundy, a 
2 CONC passionate woman unsuited for a subordinate role. The marriage had been arranged by her father Alfonso VI of Castile in 1106 to unite the two chief Christian states against the Almoravides, and to supply them with a capable milita
2 CONC ry leader. But Urraca was tenacious of her right as proprietary queen and had not learnt chastity in the polygamous household of her father. Husband and wife quarrelled with the brutality of the age and came to open war. Alfonso h
2 CONC ad the support of one section of the nobles who found their account in the confusion. Being a much better soldier than any of his opponents he gained victories at Sepulveda and Fuente de la Culebra, but his only trustworthy suppor
2 CONC ters were his Aragonese, who were not numerous enough to keep Castile and León subjugated. The marriage of Alfonso and Urraca was declared null by the pope, as they were second cousins, in 1110, but he ignored the papal nuncio and
2 CONC  clung to his liaison with Urraca until 1114. During his marriage, he had called himself "King and Emperor of Castile, Toledo, Aragón, Pamplona, Sobrarbe, and Ribagorza" in recognition of his rights as Urraca's husband; of his inh
2 CONC eritance of the lands of his father, including the kingdom of his great-uncle Gonzalo; and his prerogative to conquer Andalusia from the Moor. He inserted the title of imperator on the basis that he had three kingdoms under his ru
2 CONC le.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso's late marriage and his failure to remarry and produce the essential legitimate heir that should have been a dynastic linchpin of his aggressive territorial policies have been adduced as a lack of interest in women. Ibn al
2 CONC -Athir (1166-1234) describes Alfonso as a tireless soldier who would sleep in his armor without benefit of cover, who responded when asked why he did not take his pleasure from one of the captives of Muslim chiefs, responded that 
2 CONC the man devoted to war needs the companionship of men not women.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Church relations
2 CONT
2 CONT The king quarrelled with the church, and particularly the Cistercians, almost as violently as with his wife. As he beat her, so he drove Archbishop Bernard into exile and expelled the monks of Sahagún. He was finally compelled to 
2 CONC give way in Castile and Leon to his stepson Alfonso Raimúndez, son of Urraca and her first husband. The intervention of Pope Calixtus II brought about an arrangement between the old man and his young namesake.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1122 in Belchite, he founded a confraternity of knights to fight against the Almoravids. It was the start of the military orders in Aragón. Years later, he organised a branch of the Militia Christi of the Holy Land at Monreal d
2 CONC el Campo.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Reconquista
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso spent his first four years in near-constant war with the Moor. In 1105, he conquered Ejea and Tauste and refortified Castellar and Juslibol. In 1106, he defeated Ahmad II al-Musta'in of Zaragoza at Valtierra. In 1107, he t
2 CONC ook Tamarite de Litera and Esteban de la Litera. Then followed a period dominated by his relations with Castile and León through his wife, Urraca. He resumed his Reconquista in 1117 by conquering Fitero, Corella, Cintruénigo, Murc
2 CONC hante, Monteagudo, and Cascante.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1118, the Council of Toulouse declared it a crusade to assist in the reconquest of Zaragoza. Many Frenchmen consequently joined Alfonso at Ayerbe. They took Almudévar, Gurrea de Gállego, and Zuera, besieging Zaragoza itself by 
2 CONC the end of May. On 18 December, it fell and the forces of Alfonso occupied the Azuda, the government tower. The great palace of the city was given to the monks of Bernard. Promptly, the city was made Alfonso's capital. Two years l
2 CONC ater, in 1120, he defeated a Muslim army intent on reconquering his new capital at Cutanda. He promulgated the fuero of tortum per tortum, facilitating taking the law into one's own hands, and forced the Muslim population of the c
2 CONC ity (greater than 20,000) to move to the suburbs.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1119, he retook Cervera, Tudejen, Castellón, Tarazona, Ágreda, Magallón, Borja, Alagón, Novillas, Mallén, Rueda, Épila and repopulated the region of Soria. He began the siege of Calatayud, but left to defeat the army at Cutanda
2 CONC  trying to retake Zaragoza. When Calatayud fell, he took Bubierca, Alhama de Aragón, Ariza, and Daroca (1120). In 1123, he besieged and took Lérida, which was in the hands of the count of Barcelona. From the winter of 1124 to Sept
2 CONC ember 1125, he was on a risky expedition to Peña Cadiella deep in Andalusia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the great raid of 1125, he carried away a large part of the subject Christians from Granada, and in the south-west of France, he had claims as usurper-king of Navarre. From 1125 to 1126, he was on campaign against Granada, wher
2 CONC e he was trying to install a Christian prince, and Córdoba, where got only as far as Motril. In 1127, he reconquered Longares, but simultaneously lost all his Castilian possessions to Alfonso VII. He confirmed a treaty with Castil
2 CONC e the next year (1128) at Támara which fixed the boundaries of the two realms.
2 CONT
2 CONT He conquered Molina de Aragón and repopulated Monzón in 1129, before besieging Valencia, which had fallen again upon the Cid's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He went north of the Pyrenees in October 1130 to protect the Val d'Aran. Early in 1131, he besieged Bayonne. It is said he ruled "from Belorado to Pallars and from Bayonne to Monreal."
2 CONT
2 CONT At the siege of Bayonne in October 1131, three years before his death, he published a will leaving his kingdom to three autonomous religious orders based in Palestine and politically largely independent on the pope, the Knights Te
2 CONC mplars, the Hospitallers, and the Knights of the Holy Sepulchre, whose influences might have been expected to cancel one another out. The will has greatly puzzled historians, who have read it as a bizarre gesture of extreme piety 
2 CONC uncharacteristic of Alphonso's character, one that effectively undid his life's work. Elena Lourie (1975) suggested instead that it was Alphonso's attempt to neutralize the papacy's interest in a disputed succession— Aragon had be
2 CONC en a fief of the Papacy since 1068— and to fend off Urraca's son from her first marriage, Alphonso VII of Castile, for the Papacy would be bound to press the terms of such a pious testament.[3] Generous bequests to important churc
2 CONC hes and abbeys in Castile had the effect of making the noble churchmen there beneficiaries who would be encouraged by the will to act as a brake on Alphonso VII's ambitions to break it— and yet among the magnates witnessing the wi
2 CONC ll in 1131 there is not a single cleric. In the event it was a will that his nobles refused to carry out— instead bringing his brother Ramiro from the monastery to assume royal powers— an eventuality that Lourie suggests was Alpho
2 CONC nso's hidden intent.
2 CONT
2 CONT His final campaigns were against Mequinenza (1133) and Fraga (1134), where García Ramírez, the future king of Navarre, and a mere 500 other knights fought with him. It fell on 17 July. He was dead by September. Alfonso was a fierc
2 CONC e, violent man, a soldier and nothing else, whose piety was wholly militant. He has a great role in the Spanish reconquest.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Death
2 CONT
2 CONT His testament was not honored: Aragon took his aged brother abbot-bishop Ramiro out of a monastery and made him king, marrying him without papal dispensation to Agnes, sister of the Duke of Aquitaine; the Navarrese lords, perhaps 
2 CONC irked at the personal union of Aragon and Navarre signalled their independence by putting García Ramírez Lord of Monzón, descendant of an illlegitimate son of García Sánchez III, to the throne in Pamplona. "The result of the crisi
2 CONC s produced by the result of Alfonso I's will was a major reorientation of the peninsula's kingdoms: the separation of Aragon and Navarre, the union of Aragon and Catalonia and— a moot point but stressed particularly by some Castil
2 CONC ian historians— the affirmation of 'Castilian hegemony' in Spain"[4] by the rendering of homage for Zaragoza by Alfonso's eventual heir, Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ According to the fourteenth-century Crónica di San Juan Peña he died in his sixty-first year (Lourie 1975:639 note).
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Quoted in Lourie 1975:639 note. No bastards are recorded, though they would have cut prominent figures under the circumstances. No inference of homosexuality need be drawn: the chastity that supports fitness for the hunt or for 
2 CONC battle is a cultural topos as old as the myth of Actaeon.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Innocent II indeed did write Alfonso VII to just this effect, 10 June 1135 or 36 (Lourie 1995:645).
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Lourie 1975:636.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lourie, Elena. "The Will of Alfonso I, 'El Batallador,' King of Aragon and Navarre: A Reassessment." Speculum, 50.4 (October 1975), pp. 635-651.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Alfonso the Battler
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia  (Redirected from Alfonso I of Aragon)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search 
2 CONT
2 CONT Statue of Alfonso, dressed as a battler, in the Parque Primo de Rivera in Zaragoza, the city he recovered from the Moors and made his capital.Alfonso I (1073/1074[1] – 8 September 1134), called the Battler or the Warrior (Spanish:
2 CONC  el Batallador), was the king of Aragon and Navarre from 1104 until his death in 1134. He was the second son of King Sancho Ramírez and successor of his brother Peter I. With his marriage to Urraca, queen regnant of Castile and Le
2 CONC ón, in 1109, he began to use, with some justification, the grandiose title Emperor of Spain, formerly employed by his father-in-law, Alfonso VI. Alfonso the Battler earned his sobriquet in the Reconquista. He won his greatest mili
2 CONC tary successes in the middle Ebro, where he expelled the Moors from Zaragoza in 1118 and took Egea, Tudela, Calatayud, Borja, Tarazona, Daroca, and Monreal del Campo. He died in September 1134 after an unsuccessful battle with the
2 CONC  Moors at the Battle of Fraga.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Matrimonial conflicts
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Church relations
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Reconquista
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Death 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.1 Will and testament
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.2 Pseudo-Alfonso the Battler
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT His earliest years were passed in the monastery of Siresa, learning to read and write and to practise the military arts until the tuition of Lope Garcés the Pilgrim, who was repaid for his services by his former charge with the co
2 CONC unty of Pedrola when Alfonso came to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his brother's reign, he participated in the taking of Huesca (the Battle of Alcoraz, 1096), which became the largest city in the kingdom and the new capital. He also joined El Cid's expeditions in Valencia. His father gave 
2 CONC him the lordships of Biel, Luna, Ardenes, and Bailo.
2 CONT
2 CONT A series of deaths put Alfonso directly in line for the throne. His brother's children, Isabella and Peter (who married María Rodríguez, daughter of El Cid), died in 1103 and 1104 respectively.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Matrimonial conflicts
2 CONT
2 CONT A passionate fighting-man (he fought twenty-nine battles against Christian or Moor), he was married (when well over 30 years and a habitual bachelor) in 1109 to the ambitious Queen Urraca of León, widow of Raymond of Burgundy, a p
2 CONC assionate woman unsuited for a subordinate role. The marriage had been arranged by her father Alfonso VI of León in 1106 to unite the two chief Christian states against the Almoravides, and to supply them with a capable military l
2 CONC eader. But Urraca was tenacious of her right as queen regnant and had not learnt chastity in the polygamous household of her father. Husband and wife quarrelled with the brutality of the age and came to open war. Alfonso had the s
2 CONC upport of one section of the nobles who found their account in the confusion. Being a much better soldier than any of his opponents he won the Battle of Candespina and the Battle of Viadangos, but his only trustworthy supporters w
2 CONC ere his Aragonese, who were not numerous enough to keep Castile and León subjugated. The marriage of Alfonso and Urraca was declared null by the Pope, as they were second cousins, in 1110, but he ignored the papal nuncio and clung
2 CONC  to his liaison with Urraca until 1114. During his marriage, he had called himself "King and Emperor of Castile, Toledo, Aragón, Pamplona, Sobrarbe, and Ribagorza" in recognition of his rights as Urraca's husband; of his inheritan
2 CONC ce of the lands of his father, including the kingdom of his great-uncle Gonzalo; and his prerogative to conquer Andalusia from the Moor. He inserted the title of imperator on the basis that he had three kingdoms under his rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso's late marriage and his failure to remarry and produce the essential legitimate heir that should have been a dynastic linchpin of his aggressive territorial policies have been adduced as a lack of interest in women. Ibn al
2 CONC -Athir (1166–1234) describes Alfonso as a tireless soldier who would sleep in his armor without benefit of cover, who responded when asked why he did not take his pleasure from one of the captives of Muslim chiefs, responded that 
2 CONC the man devoted to war needs the companionship of men not women.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Church relations
2 CONT
2 CONT The king quarrelled with the church, and particularly the Cistercians, almost as violently as with his wife. As he beat her,[citation needed] so he drove Archbishop Bernard into exile and expelled the monks of Sahagún[citation nee
2 CONC ded]. He was finally compelled to give way in Castile and Leon to his stepson, Alfonso VII of Castile, son of Urraca and her first husband. The intervention of Pope Calixtus II brought about an arrangement between the old man and 
2 CONC his young namesake.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1122 in Belchite, he founded a confraternity of knights to fight against the Almoravids. It was the start of the military orders in Aragon. Years later, he organised a branch of the Militia Christi of the Holy Land at Monreal d
2 CONC el Campo.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Reconquista
2 CONT
2 CONT A denarius of Alfonso's, minted at Jaca, bearing his effigy and the inscription ANFUS-REX ARA-GON (Anfusus rex Aragonensium, King Alfonso of Aragon).Alfonso spent his first four years in near-constant war with the Moor. In 1105, h
2 CONC e conquered Ejea and Tauste and refortified Castellar and Juslibol. In 1106, he defeated Ahmad II al-Musta'in of Zaragoza at Valtierra. In 1107, he took Tamarite de Litera and Esteban de la Litera. Then followed a period dominated
2 CONC  by his relations with Castile and León through his wife, Urraca. He resumed his Reconquista in 1117 by conquering Fitero, Corella, Cintruénigo, Murchante, Monteagudo, and Cascante.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1118, the Council of Toulouse declared a crusade to assist in the reconquest of Zaragoza. Many Frenchmen consequently joined Alfonso at Ayerbe. They took Almudévar, Gurrea de Gállego, and Zuera, besieging Zaragoza itself by the
2 CONC  end of May. The city fell on 18 December, and the forces of Alfonso occupied the Azuda, the government tower. The great palace of the city was given to the monks of Bernard. Promptly, the city was made Alfonso's capital. Two year
2 CONC s later, in 1120, he defeated a Muslim army intent on reconquering his new capital at the Battle of Cutanda. He promulgated the fuero of tortum per tortum, facilitating taking the law into one's own hands, which among others reass
2 CONC umed the Muslim right to dwell in the city and their right to keep their properties and practice their religion under their own jurisdiction as long as they maintained tax payment and relocated to the suburbs.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1119, he retook Cervera, Tudejen, Castellón, Tarazona, Ágreda, Magallón, Borja, Alagón, Novillas, Mallén, Rueda, Épila and repopulated the region of Soria. He began the siege of Calatayud, but left to defeat the army at Cutanda
2 CONC  trying to retake Zaragoza. When Calatayud fell, he took Bubierca, Alhama de Aragón, Ariza, and Daroca (1120). In 1123, he besieged and took Lleida, which was in the hands of the count of Barcelona. From the winter of 1124 to Sept
2 CONC ember 1125, he was on a risky expedition to Peña Cadiella deep in Andalusia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the great raid of 1125, he carried away a large part of the subject Christians from Granada, and in the south-west of France, he had claims as usurper-king of Navarre. From 1125 to 1126, he was on campaign against Granada, wher
2 CONC e he was trying to install a Christian prince, and Córdoba, where got only as far as Motril. In 1127, he reconquered Longares, but simultaneously lost all his Castilian possessions to Alfonso VII. He confirmed a treaty with Castil
2 CONC e the next year (1128) with the Peace of Támara, which fixed the boundaries of the two realms.
2 CONT
2 CONT He conquered Molina de Aragón and repopulated Monzón in 1129, before besieging Valencia, which had fallen again upon the Cid's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He went north of the Pyrenees in October 1130 to protect the Val d'Aran. Early in 1131, he besieged Bayonne. It is said he ruled "from Belorado to Pallars and from Bayonne to Monreal."
2 CONT
2 CONT At the Siege of Bayonne in October 1131, three years before his death, he published a will leaving his kingdom to three autonomous religious orders based in Palestine and politically largely independent on the pope, the Knights Te
2 CONC mplars, the Hospitallers, and the Knights of the Holy Sepulchre, whose influences might have been expected to cancel one another out. The will has greatly puzzled historians, who have read it as a bizarre gesture of extreme piety 
2 CONC uncharacteristic of Alphonso's character, one that effectively undid his life's work. Elena Lourie (1975) suggested instead that it was Alphonso's attempt to neutralize the papacy's interest in a disputed succession— Aragon had be
2 CONC en a fief of the Papacy since 1068— and to fend off Urraca's son from her first marriage, Alphonso VII of Castile, for the Papacy would be bound to press the terms of such a pious testament.[3] Generous bequests to important churc
2 CONC hes and abbeys in Castile had the effect of making the noble churchmen there beneficiaries who would be encouraged by the will to act as a brake on Alphonso VII's ambitions to break it— and yet among the magnates witnessing the wi
2 CONC ll in 1131 there is not a single cleric. In the event it was a will that his nobles refused to carry out— instead bringing his brother Ramiro from the monastery to assume royal powers— an eventuality that Lourie suggests was Alpho
2 CONC nso's hidden intent.
2 CONT
2 CONT His final campaigns were against Mequinenza (1133) and Fraga (1134), where García Ramírez, the future king of Navarre, and a mere 500 other knights fought with him. It fell on 17 July. He was dead by September. Alfonso was a fierc
2 CONC e, violent man, a soldier and nothing else, whose piety was wholly militant. He had a great role in the Spanish reconquest.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Death
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Will and testament
2 CONT
2 CONT His testament was not honored: Aragon took his aged brother abbot-bishop Ramiro out of a monastery and made him king, marrying him without papal dispensation to Agnes, sister of the Duke of Aquitaine; the Navarrese lords, perhaps 
2 CONC irked at the personal union of Aragon and Navarre signalled their independence by putting García Ramírez, Lord of Monzón, descendant of an illegitimate son of García Sánchez III, to the throne in Pamplona. "The result of the crisi
2 CONC s produced by the result of Alfonso I's will was a major reorientation of the peninsula's kingdoms: the separation of Aragon and Navarre, the union of Aragon and Catalonia and— a moot point but stressed particularly by some Castil
2 CONC ian historians— the affirmation of 'Castilian hegemony' in Spain"[4] by the rendering of homage for Zaragoza by Alfonso's eventual heir, Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Pseudo-Alfonso the Battler
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime during the reign of Alfonso II of Aragon, the Battler's grandnephew, a man came forward claiming to be Alfonso the Battler. The only contemporary references to this event are two letters of Alfonso II addressed to Louis V
2 CONC II of France; they were carried to Louis by Berengar, the Bishop of Lleida, but are not dated.[5] According to the second of these, the pretender was then living in Louis's domains, meaning the Principality of Catalonia, which was
2 CONC  ruled by Alfonso under Louis's suzerainty. This pretender was an old man (appropriately, since the Battler had died some decades earlier) and Alfonso II expressed confidence that Louis would arrest him at the earliest possible mo
2 CONC ment and bring him to justice. The first letter supplies sufficient information to date it approximately, since the Bishop sojourned at the court of Louis on his way to Rome. It is known from other sources that Berengar attended t
2 CONC he Third Lateran Council in March 1179. The letters were probably written towards the end of 1178 or in January 1179 at the latest.[6] According to an annalist source for the years 1089–1196, the pretender was received with honour
2 CONC  and pomp in Zaragoza, Calatayud, and Daroca, which the Battler had conquered, but after it was found out that he was false he was executed before the city of Barcelona in 1181.[7] Modern historian Antonio Ubieto Arteta, has hypot
2 CONC hesised that the Aragonese lords of the tenencies of Zaragoza, Calatayud, and Daroca—Pedro de Luesia, Loferrench de Luna, Pedro de Castillazuelo (lord of Calatayud), Pedro Cornel (lord of Murillo de Gállego), and the majordomo Jim
2 CONC eno de Artusilla, all of whom disappear between 1177 and 1181 in the documentation of their tenencies—perhaps support, at least inititally, the pretender.[8] These lords also appear in the later legend of the Bell of Huesca, which
2 CONC  has no historical basis, as the victims Ramiro II (1136). Since, historically, they were not active in the 1130s, it is possible that the historically-based legend of the pseudo-Alfonso had some influence on the genesis of the Be
2 CONC ll of Huesca.
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest chronicle source for the imposture is Rodrigo Jiménez de Rada, writing in the middle of the thirteenth century, who records that there were several legends then current about the death of Alfonso the Battler: some bel
2 CONC ieved he perished in the battle of Fraga, some that his body had never been recovered, others that he was buried in the monastery of Montearagón, and still others that he had fled from Fraga in shame after his defeat and became a 
2 CONC pilgrim as an act of penance. Some years later, Rodrigo writes, though he does not give a year, an impostor arose and was received by many as the Battler, though Alfonso II had him arrested and hanged. This is the earliest referen
2 CONC ce to the impostor's end.[9] The legend was amplified in later years. According to the fourteenth-century Crónica de los Estados Peninsulares, the Battler went on a pilgrimage to Jerusalem, where he lived for many years.[10] The C
2 CONC rónica de San Juan de la Peña also recounts the incident, but it depends entirely on Rodrigo and the Estados Peninsulares. It is not until the seventeenth-century historian Jerónimo Zurita penned his Anales de la Corona de Aragón 
2 CONC that new details were added to the legend.[11] Zurita's dates the impostor's appearance to the death of Raymond Berengar IV of Barcelona, who had been exercising power in Aragon, and the succession of the child Alfonso II in 1162.
2 CONC  The death of the impostor, by hanging, must have occurred in 1163.
2 CONT
