2 CONT ''    [https://www.geni.com/people/Guglielmo-I-il-Grande-conte-di-Borgogna/6000000000716998561 Guillaume I] & his wife had [thirteen] children: ''
2 CONT 13.     ''' [''' '''BERTHE de Bourgogne''' (-early Jan 1100, bur Sahagún, León, royal monastery of Santos Facundo y Primitivo).  <br/>
2 CONT -The Chronicon Regum Legionensium names "Berta, who was of Tuscan descent" ("Bertam Tuscia oriundam") as the third of the "five legitimate wives" of King Alfonso[124].  
2 CONT -Las crónicas anónimas de Sahagún refer to her as "otra mugger de la nacion de Lombardia llamada Berta". [ another woman from the nation of Lombardy named Berta - SHARON 2025]
2 CONT The precise origin of Berthe is not known.  
2 CONT -According to Europäische Stammtafeln[125], she was the daughter of [http://www.geni.com/people/William-the-Great-Count-of-Burgundy/6000000000716998561 Guillaume I] Comte [Palatin] de Bourgogne, Comte de Vienne et de Macon, which 
2 CONC is inconsistent with the "Tuscan descent" reported in the Chronicon Regum Legionensium.  
2 CONT -Szabolcs de Vajay suggests that she was the daughter of Guillaume Comte de Bourgogne[126].  
2 CONT -Reilly does not mention this possible Burgundian origin of Berthe, implying that the Castilian king chose his third wife from outside the Burgundian circle in order to diminish the influence of the Burgundians at court.  As Berth
2 CONC e de Bourgogne would have been the sister of Raymond de Bourgogne who married Infanta doña Urraca, oldest legitimate daughter of King Alfonso, around the same time that King Alfonso married Queen Berta, it is surprising that the c
2 CONC hronicles do not refer to this relationship if it is correct.
2 CONT -The references to "Tuscia" and "Lombardia" in the chronicles could be consistent with the family of Bourgogne [Comte] having originated in northern Italy, their ancestors being Marchesi of Ivrea until 968, although this was nearl
2 CONC y 130 years before the date of Queen Berta's marriage.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Reilly dates this marriage to "during the Christmas season of 1094", but does not state his source[127].  In a later passage, Reilly states that the first reference to Berta as queen is dated 28 Apr 1095 [128]. According to Reilly
2 CONC , Queen Berta died shortly after the new year 1100, probably before 16 Jan[129].  In another passage, he notes that the last notice of her is dated 17 Nov 1099 [130].  She was dead in 25 Jan 1100, the date of the charter under whi
2 CONC ch "Adefonsus…Toletani imperii rex" donated the churches of "Sancti Facundi et Sancti Primitivi…cum sua villa…Villaverde", ceded by "comitis Monini Fernandis…in vita sua dederam uxori mee Berte regine", to Cluny, confirmed by "Rai
2 CONC mundus totius Gallecie comes et gener regis, Urraca soror regis, Urraca regis filia et Raimundi comitis uxor, Enricus Portugalensis comes, uxor ipsius Tarasia filia regis…"[131].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ([Dec] 1094) as his fourth wife, ALFONSO VI King of Castile and Leon, son of FERNANDO I "el Magno" King of Castile and León & his wife doña Sancha de León (Compostela [1037]-Toledo 30 Jun 1109, bur Sahagún, León, San Manci
2 CONC o chapel in the royal monastery of Santos Facundo y Primitivo).''']''' 
2 CONT
