2 CONT Douce I (also Dulcia or Dolça, called "of Rouergue" or "of Gévaudan") (c. 1090–1127) was the daughter of Gilbert I of Gévaudan and Gerberga II of Provence and wife of Ramon Berenguer III, Count of Barcelona. In 1112, she inherited
2 CONC  the county of Provence through her mother. She married Ramon Berenguer at Arles on 3 February that year. In 1113, Douce ceded her rights in Provence, Gévaudan, and the viscounty of Millau to her husband. By her marriage, she had 
2 CONC already linked the history of Provence with that of Catalonia. Her death inaugurated a period of instability in Provence, which did not terminate until the Baussenque Wars (1144-1162), in which the Provençals defeated the Catalans
2 CONC . Her children with Ramon Berenguer were: Almodis, married Ponce de Cervera Berenguela or Berengaria (1116–1149), married Alfonso VII of Castile Ramon Berenguer (1113–1162), Count of Barcelona Berenguer Ramon (c. 1115–1144), Count
2 CONC  of Provence Bernard, died young
