1 NOTE Bernard Plantapilosa (22 March 841 - 20 June 885[1]), or Plantevelue, meaning Hairyfeet or Hairypaws, son of Bernard of Septimania and Dhuoda, was the Count of Auvergne (as Bernard II) from 872 to his death. The Emperor Charles th
2 CONC e Fat granted him the title of Margrave of Aquitaine in 885.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother's Liber Manualis mentions that he was born at Uzès in the year following the death of Louis the Pious. He was appointed Margrave of Septimania (or Gothia) sometime before 868. He was the lay abbot of Brioude between 85
2 CONC 7 and 868 and Count of Autun and from 864 to 869. He was deposed sometime before 876 and replaced by Bernard of Gothia in that year. He returned to favour under Charles the Fat. At sometime during the war against Boso of Provence,
2 CONC  he obtained the county of Mâcon.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Ermengard, daughter of Bernard I of Auvergne (or perhaps of Guerin I). Their son was William I of Aquitaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT ^ Also possibly 6 January or 16 August 886 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT MacLean, Simon. Kingship and Politics in the Late Ninth Century: Charles the Fat and the end of the Carolingian Empire. Cambridge University Press: 2003. 
2 CONT Hummer, Hans J. Politics and Power in Early Medieval Europe: Alsace and the Frankish Realm 600 - 1000. Cambridge University Press: 2005. 
