2 CONT "William I (22 March 875 – 6 July 918), called the Pious, was the Count of Auvergne from 886 and Duke of Aquitaine from 893, succeeding the Poitevin ruler Ebalus Manser. He made numerous monastic foundations, most important among 
2 CONC them the foundation of Cluny Abbey on 11 September 910.
2 CONT
2 CONT "William was son of Bernard Plantapilosa and Ermengard. Sometime before 898, William married the Bosonid Angilberga, daughter of Boso of Provence and Ermengard of Italy.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT "By inheritance, William was the ruler of Auvergne and the Limousin. He conquered Poitou and Aquitaine in 893 on behalf of Ebalus Manser. He kept the latter for himself and was proclaimed duke. His possessions extended from Austra
2 CONC sia to Toulouse and included the Autunois and Mâconnais.
2 CONT
2 CONT "In 909, William founded the Benedictine abbey of Cluny that would become an important political and religious centre.[2] William required no control over the abbey, which he arranged should be responsible directly to the pope (se
2 CONC e Clunian reforms). This was especially striking since most monasteries were privately owned and the appointment of abbots and officials was left to that family or individual, leading to the appointment of untrained and unordained
2 CONC  abbots and officials. William also nominated Cluny's first abbot, Berno of Baume.
2 CONT
2 CONT "A sign of William's independence of rule in Aquitaine is that he had a deniers minted in his own name at Brioude.[3] He was buried in the monastery of Saint-Julien. William had no sons of his own [clarification needed] and was su
2 CONC cceeded by a nephew, William the Younger, son of his sister Adelinda by Acfred I of Carcassonne.
2 CONT
2 CONT William married Engelberga, daughter of Boso of Provence and Ermengard they had:
2 CONT Engelberga
2 CONT Possibly another daughter
