1 NOTE [FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Killed by agents of his kinsman, Clovis I.
1 NOTE [FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT    king of the Franks and ruler of much of Gaul from 481 to 511, a keyperiod during the
2 CONT transformation of the Roman Empire into Europe. His dynasty, theMerovingians, survived more than 200 years, until the rise of theCarolingians in the 8th century. While he was not the first Frankishking, he was the kingdom's politi
2 CONC cal and religious founder.
2 CONT    Clovis was the son of the pagan Frankish king Childeric and theThuringian queen Basina. He succeeded his father in 481 as the ruler ofthe Salian Franks and other Frankish groups around Tournai (now inBelgium). Although the chro
2 CONC nology of his reign is imprecise, it iscertain that by the time of his death in 511 he had consolidated theFranks and expanded his influence and rule to include the Roman provinceof Belgica Secunda in 486 and the territories of th
2 CONC e Alemanni (in 496),the Burgundians (in 500),
2 CONT and the Visigoths (in 507). Clovis's kingdom began in the regionencompassing modern Belgium and northeastern France, expanded south andwest, and became the most powerful in Gaul. He was the most importantWestern ally of the Byzant
2 CONC ine emperor Anastasius I. The Pactus LegisSalicae (Law of the Salian Franks), a written code combining customarylaw, Roman written law, Christian ideals, and royal edicts, likelyoriginated during Clovis's reign and had a long hist
2 CONC ory of emendation andinfluence.
2 CONT Clovis married the Catholic Burgundian princess Clotilda and had fivechildren with her. A son, Theuderic, was born prior to the marriage; hismother is unknown.
2 CONT    Clovis, like his father, dealt politically and diplomatically with theCatholic bishops of Gaul. These powerful figures had no qualms aboutworking with Germanic kings, as a letter to Clovis from
2 CONT Bishop Remigius of Reims, written early in the king's reign, makes clear.The bishops saw themselves as the king's natural advisers, and, evenbefore his conversion to Catholic Christianity and his baptism at Reims(now in France) by
2 CONC  Remigius, Clovis apparently recognized their rightsand protected their property. In a letter written to Clovis at the timeof his baptism, Avitus of Vienne (now in France) praises his faith,humility, and mercy. Significantly, in t
2 CONC he year of his death, Clovissummoned the bishops to a church council at Orléans.
2 CONT    Much was written about Clovis by Gregory of Tours in his Histories(often called the History of the Franks), which appeared more than 50years after Clovis's death. Interpreting him from a Christianperspective, Gregory tells stir
2 CONC ring stories about Clovis and portrays himas a single-minded warrior. He uses florid rhetoric to describe thearguments with which Clotilda attempted to persuade her husband toabandon paganism. When Clovis finally converted, he bec
2 CONC omes for Gregory a“new Constantine,” the emperor who Christianized the Roman Empire in theearly 4th century. In both cases, an unexpected victory in battle led aking to trust the power of the Christian God and to submit to baptism
2 CONC .Gregory places Clovis's baptism in 496 and characterizes his subsequentbattles as Christian victories, particularly the engagement with theVisigoths in 507 that has long been identified with Vouillé but now isbelieved to have occ
2 CONC urred at Voulon near Poitiers, France. Gregoryportrays the Visigothic war as a
2 CONT campaign against Arian heresy. His account indicates that prior to thebattle, Clovis gave gifts to the church and made appeals to St. Martin ofTours , for which he was rewarded with victory, blessed with miracles,and honoured with
2 CONC  an imperial consulship by Anastasius I .
2 CONT    Recent scholarship has revealed flaws in Gregory's account of Clovisand raised questions about the ultimate purpose of the Histories. Gregoryelevated the Franks to equivalency with the ancient Hebrews, the chosenpeople, and Clo
2 CONC vis to the stature of their great king David. Even moreimportant, he held Clovis up as a model for his own contemporary Frankishkings, Clovis's grandsons. In Gregory's estimation, unlike theirgrandfather, they did not maintain uni
2 CONC ty and peace within the kingdom noradequately respect the advice of bishops. While the Histories providesbroad background and engaging stories about the early Frankish world, theClovis of the Histories is more a literary fiction t
2 CONC han a historicalreality.
2 CONT    However, Gregory and other contemporary authors were not wholly wrongin describing
2 CONT Clovis, a warrior king, as a religious figure. His life illustrates acrucial series of ideological and cultural transformations that tookplace throughout the Western Roman Empire as it gave way to Germanickingdoms. Clovis's father
2 CONC , Childeric, died a pagan and was buried inTournai in a tomb surrounded by barbarian horse burials. Thirty yearslater Clovis was buried next to his contemporary St. Geneviève in theChurch of the Holy Apostles that he built in Pari
2 CONC s, and he was joinedyears later by his wife, St. Clotilda.
2 CONT    Over the centuries much has been made of Clovis's conversion toCatholicism. One of the first Germanic kings to do so, he did, in fact,convert to Catholicism, but recent analysis of the contemporary sourcesthat describe his reig
2 CONC n, especially of a letter written by Avitus ofVienne congratulating him on his baptism, suggests that Clovis did notconvert to Catholicism directly from paganism. Prior to acceptingCatholicism, he was interested in the Christian h
2 CONC eresy Arianism,sympathetic with it, and perhaps even leaning toward adopting it.According to Avitus, it is also likely that Clovis was baptized
2 CONT rather late in life, possibly at Christmas in 508, only three yearsbefore his death.
2 CONT    If this sequence of events is correct, it reflects the intellectualand religious climate of late 5th- and early 6th-century Gaul. The Arianheresy was the form of Christianity to which most Germanic peoplesinitially converted. I
2 CONC t understood the godhead in hierarchical terms.Jesus Christ, the Son of God, was a created being who did not share theeternal nature of God the Father but who was superior to God the HolySpirit. Orthodox Catholicism understood the
2 CONC  godhead as comprising three(coequal,/
2 CONT coeternal) members. These two Christian belief systems represent atheological power struggle within the Christian community during thetransformation period. The Catholics won by ecclesiastical and imperialdecree in the 4th century
2 CONC , making Arianism a heresy, but Arianismremained an important force in parts of Europe as late as the 6th century.
2 CONT    Pagans, Arians, and Catholics shared the Gaul of Clovis and theFranks. Clovis personally illustrates the juxtaposition of these threebelief systems. He was born into paganism, two of his sisters were Arians(one married the Aria
2 CONC n Ostrogothic king Theodoric the Great), and hiswife, Clotilda, like her sister, was Catholic but from a Burgundian royalfamily that included Arians. His conversion to Catholicism was that ofone man and not of his kingdom, but it 
2 CONC can be seen as pivotal in Frankishhistory.
2 CONT    Clovis's life as a religious man illustrates the challenges then facedby the Catholic bishops and illuminates their concerns with evangelism.They combatted paganism and the ancient traditions that it embodied,stamped out heresy
2 CONC , and attempted to convert Gaul's Jewish communities.The powerful advocacy of Catholicism that resonates in Gregory'sHistories is, perhaps, a response to the difficulty of conversions ofthose like Clovis, who was not baptized unti
2 CONC l at least 15 years into hisreign. This advocacy may also reflect a deep-seated communal memory of areligiously diverse kingdom and the daunting task of converting it.
2 CONT    Upon Clovis's death, he divided his kingdom among his four survivingsons. Only Chlotar, who outlived his brothers, ruled a united kingdom,but he in turn divided it among his sons. It was not until the reign ofClovis's great-gra
2 CONC ndson Chlotar II in the early 7th century that theMerovingians experienced long-lasting political unity. The kingdom whichClovis established, however, superseded its occasional individual partsand remained intact for centuries.
2 CONT    The historical Clovis remains a shadowy figure: a warrior whosolidified a kingdom, corresponded with bishops, and converted toCatholic Christianity. Within decades of his death, he had become a heroand was held up as a model ki
2 CONC ng. A millennium and a half later he remainssignificant. For the French, he was the founder of France, and aderivation of his name, Louis, became the principal name of its kings.His baptism is considered one of the formative dates
2 CONC  in French history.For Catholics, he was the first major Germanic Catholic king, and PopeJohn Paul II celebrated a mass in Reims in 1996 in honour of the 15thcentenary of his baptism.
2 CONT
2 CONT To cite this page: "Clovis I" Encyclopædia Britannica
2 CONT  <http://www.britannica.com/eb/article?idxref=176581>
2 CONT 481King of France
2 CONT King of Cologne
2 CONT
2 CONT MEROVINGIAN KINGS OF THE FRANKS
2 CONT AD 476 - 751
2 CONT
2 CONT The Frankish leader, Clovis, united the Salians with the Ripuarian(Eastern) Franks and they were converted to Christianity in 497. Theirkingdom expanded under Clovis and his sons to include Neustria (northernFrance), Austrasia (Ne
2 CONC therlands, Austria, northern Germany), Burgundy andProvence by 714.The Franks quickly became the dominant Germanic tribe innot only Gaul but throughout Central and Western Europe. The territorybetween modern-day France and Germany
2 CONC , and south to Central Italy, becameknown as Francia. The kingdoms this eventually encompassed included WestFrancia (France), East Francia (eventually to emerge as Germany),Burgundy, Lombardy, Lotharingia / Lorraine (only briefly 
2 CONC an independentkingdom before it splintered into various interrelated principalities,known as the Stem Duchies), and the Papal States (which started life asthe Byzantine Exarchate of Ravenna), which were centered on Rome. All ofthi
2 CONC s territory was part of the Empire of Charlemagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT 456 - 511
2 CONT  Clovis I (Chlodwig) Son of Childeric. Founded kingdom 476. MarriedChlothild.
2 CONT 486
2 CONT  Clovis occupies Northern Gaul.
2 CONT 496
2 CONT  The Franks conquer the Alemanni.
2 CONT 505
2 CONT  The Franks absorb the Alemanni (who later emerge as the Swabians).
2 CONT 507
2 CONT  The Franks defeat the Visigoths, forcing them out of Gaul.
2 CONT 511
2 CONT  The kingdom is divided between Clovis' four sons, ruling Austrasia,Orléans, Paris, and Soissons. Clotaire, king of Soissons was also the newking of the Franks. Soissons was incorporated directly under that rule.
2 CONT 511 - 560
2 CONT  Clotaire / Chlothar I Son. Established the basis of early Frankishmonarchy in Gaul.
2 CONT 531
2 CONT  The Thuringians are conquered.
2 CONT 534
2 CONT  The Burgundians are conquered.
2 CONT 555
2 CONT  The Bavarians are conquered.
2 CONT 561 - 567
2 CONT  Charibert I King of Franks.
2 CONT 561 - 584
2 CONT  Chilperic I Son. King of Neustria.
2 CONT 558
2 CONT  Orléans and Paris are drawn back under direct control of the king of theFranks.
2 CONT 584 - 629
2 CONT  Clotaire / Chlothar II Son. King of Neustria.
2 CONT 613
2 CONT  The Frankish Empire is reunified under Chlothar II’s rule, with Neustria(and the attached Swiss territories) forming its heartland. Austrasiabecomes semi-independent again in 622.
2 CONT 629 - 632
2 CONT  Charibert II King of Acquitaine.
2 CONT 630 - 638
2 CONT  Dagobert I King of Austrasia. Defeated in battle by Carinthian Slavs.
2 CONT 633 - 656
2 CONT  Clovis II 633 could be year of birth.
2 CONT 656
2 CONT  Dagobert II
2 CONT [
2 CONT  Grimoald Son of Pepin I. Carolingian Mayor of the Palace of Austrasia.]
2 CONT 656 - 661
2 CONT  Childebert Adoptivus Son. Adopted by Sigibert III of Austrasia.
2 CONT 662 - 675
2 CONT  Childeric II Son of Clovis II. King of Austrasia.
2 CONT 675 - 676
2 CONT  Clovis III
2 CONT 673 - 691
2 CONT  Theuderich III Son of Clovis II. King of Neustria, Burgundy, & Austrasia.
2 CONT 689 - 719
2 CONT  The Franks conquer the kingdom of Friesland.
2 CONT 691 - 695
2 CONT  Clovis IV Reunited Neustria & Austrasia under central government.
2 CONT 695 - 711
2 CONT  Childebert III Son of Theuderich III. King of Neustria, Burgundy, &Austrasia.
2 CONT 711 - 715
2 CONT  Dagobert III Son. King of Neustria, Burgundy, & Austrasia.
2 CONT 715 - 721
2 CONT  Daniel Chilperich II Son of Childerich II. King of Neustria.
2 CONT 717 - 720
2 CONT  Chlothar / Lothair IV Son of Theuderich III. King of Austrasia.
2 CONT 721 - 737
2 CONT  Theuderich IV Son of Dagobert III. King of Neustria, Burgundy, &Austrasia.
2 CONT 737 - 743
2 CONT  Interregnum, Carolingian mayors rule.
2 CONT 743 - 751
2 CONT  Childeric III Grandson of Childeric II. King of Neustria, Burgundy, &Austrasia. Deposed. Died 755.
2 CONT 751
2 CONT  With the Pope's blessing, the Carolingian Mayors of the Palace deposethe Merovingians and take control of the empire. Neustria, Austrasia &Burgundy are controlled directly, and the former two names fade fromcommon use.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT CAROLINGIAN (FRANKISH) EMPIRE
2 CONT AD 751 - 888
2 CONT
2 CONT [628 - 639
2 CONT  Pepin I Mayor. His dau m. Mayor of the Palace of Austrasia.]
2 CONT [687 - 714
2 CONT  Pepin II of Herstal (the Fat) Grandson. Mayor]
2 CONT [714 - 741
2 CONT  Charles Martel (the Hammer) Son. Mayor. Defeated Islamic Spain atPotiers in 732.]
2 CONT [741 - 747
2 CONT  Carloman Son. Mayor]
2 CONT [741 - 751
2 CONT  Pepin III the Short Brother. Mayor]
2 CONT 751
2 CONT  Pepin III deposes the last Merovingian king in 751 and, with the Pope'sblessing, assumes the title.
2 CONT
2 CONT 751 - 768
2 CONT  Pepin III
2 CONT 768 - 814
2 CONT  Charles the Great / Charlemagne Son. King of France. French HRE(800-814).
2 CONT 768 - 771
2 CONT  Carloman I Brother. Joint King of France.
2 CONT 814 - 840
2 CONT  Louis I the Pious I of France, Italy, Germany, Burgundy, & Empire. LostCroatia.
2 CONT 840
2 CONT  Louis wills the Frankish Empire to his sons, but tries to ensure thatthe eldest gains the biggest share, in order to avoid the fragmentationof territory that so weakened the Merovingians. Lothar receives MiddleFrancia (Rhine corr
2 CONC idor and Italy); Charles the Bald receives WesternFrancia (France); Louis the German receives Eastern Francia (Germany).
2 CONT 840 - 855
2 CONT  Lothar I of Middle (Italian) Franks rules the Empire.
2 CONT 855 - 875
2 CONT  Louis II of Middle (Italian) Franks rules the Empire.
2 CONT 875 - 877
2 CONT  Charles II of Western Franks rules the Empire.
2 CONT 881 - 888
2 CONT  Charles III of Eastern Franks rules the Empire.
2 CONT 888
2 CONT  The rule of the Holy Roman Empire falls to non-Frankish emperors.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT CAROLINGIAN KINGS OF WESTERN FRANKS (FRANCE)
2 CONT AD 843 - 987
2 CONT
2 CONT 843 - 877
2 CONT  Charles II the Bald II of France & Empire. Emperor (875-877).
2 CONT 877 - 879
2 CONT  Louis II the Stammerer II of France.
2 CONT 879 - 882
2 CONT  Louis III III of France.
2 CONT 879 - 884
2 CONT  Carloman II II of France.
2 CONT 884 - 888
2 CONT  Charles III the Fat of Germany.
2 CONT 888
2 CONT  Charles III (his numbering is not strictly counted within the list ofFrench monarchs) is deposed by the Germans and the Frankish Empire isofficially divided between East and West. The western section becomesmodern France, the eas
2 CONC tern section, modern Germany.
2 CONT 888 - 898
2 CONT  Odo / Eudes Count of Paris. Elected by the nobility. Capetian ancestor.
2 CONT 898 - 922
2 CONT  Charles III the Simple Son of Louis II. Granted land to the Normans.
2 CONT 922 - 923
2 CONT  Robert I Brother of Odo. Count of Paris.
2 CONT 923 - 936
2 CONT  Rudolf / Raoul Son-in-law of Odo. Duke of Burgundy (921-936).
2 CONT 936 - 954
2 CONT  Louis IV d'Outremer Son of Charles III. Had been exiled in England(=outre mer).
2 CONT 954 - 986
2 CONT  Lothair V Son.
2 CONT 986 - 987
2 CONT  Louis V Son.
2 CONT 987
2 CONT  By this time the Carolingian kings and those drawn from the House ofParis had so weakened the effectiveness of the monarchy in France that itowned little land outside Paris. The Carolingian son of Louis V, Charlesof Lorraine, was
2 CONC  ignored in favour of passing the crown permanently tothe House of Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT CAPETIAN DYNASTY
2 CONT AD 987 - 1328
2 CONT
2 CONT 987 - 996
2 CONT  Hugh Capet
2 CONT 996 - 1031
2 CONT  Robert II the Pious
2 CONT 1031 - 1060
2 CONT  Henry I
2 CONT 1060 - 1108
2 CONT  Philip I
2 CONT 1108 - 1137
2 CONT  Louis VI
2 CONT 1137 - 1180
2 CONT  Louis VII
2 CONT 1180 - 1223
2 CONT  Philip II Augustus
2 CONT 1223 - 1226
2 CONT  Louis VIII
2 CONT 1226 - 1270
2 CONT  St. Louis IX
2 CONT 1270 - 1285
2 CONT  Philip III
2 CONT 1285 - 1314
2 CONT  Philip IV the Fair M. Jeanne I of Navarre.
2 CONT 1314 - 1316
2 CONT  Louis X Also Luis the Stubborn of Navarre.
2 CONT 1316
2 CONT  John I Reigned in France for eight days.
2 CONT 1316 - 1322
2 CONT  Philip V Also Philip of Navarre.
2 CONT 1322 - 1328
2 CONT  Charles IV Also Charles I of Navarre.
2 CONT 1328
2 CONT  Charles' daughter, Jeanne, is disqualified from occupying the Frenchthrone by Salic Law. Instead, she accedes the throne of Navarre, whilethe Valois succeed to France.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT VALOIS DYNASTY
2 CONT AD 1328 - 1589
2 CONT
2 CONT 1328 - 1350
2 CONT  Philip VI of Valois
2 CONT 1337 - 1453
2 CONT  Edward III of England invades France to press his own claim to thethrone. The Hundred Years War begins.
2 CONT 1350 - 1364
2 CONT  John II
2 CONT 1364 - 1380
2 CONT  Charles V
2 CONT 1380 - 1422
2 CONT  Charles VI
2 CONT 1422 - 1461
2 CONT  Charles VII
2 CONT 1461 - 1483
2 CONT  Louis XI
2 CONT 1483 - 1498
2 CONT  Charles VIII
2 CONT 1498 - 1515
2 CONT  Louis XII of Orléans
2 CONT 1515 - 1547
2 CONT  Francis I of Angloulême
