1 OCCU Roi des Francs Ripuaires de Cologne 289, Duc des Francs de l'Est, Duke of the East Franks, King of East Franks, Roi de Cologne, Duc des Francs, , Duke of East Franks, L8T2-DXG, Frankisch hoofdman
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Marcomir De Cologne==
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.genealogieonline.nl/en/richard-and-charlotte-allen-cosby-ancestry/P39807.php
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marcomer''' (or '''Marcomir''', '''Marcomeres''', '''Marchomer''', '''Marchomir''') (late 4th century), Frankish chief.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''In later legend he was the son of [http://www.geni.com/people/Chlodio-King-of-the-Franks-at-Cologne/5586743481520107438 Chlodio IV], ... from the kings of Troy. ''
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcomer English] | [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcomir French]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marcomer''' (Marcomeres, Marchomer, Marchomir) was a Frankish leader (dux) in the late 4th century who invaded the Roman Empire in the year 388, when the usurper and leader of the whole of Roman Gaul, Magnus Maximus was surroun
2 CONC ded in Aquileia by Theodosius I.
2 CONT
2 CONT The invasion is documented by Gregory of Tours who cited the now lost work of Sulpicius Alexander. According to this account Marcomer, Sunno and Genobaud invaded the Roman provinces Germania and Belgia in Gaul. They broke through 
2 CONC the lines, killed many people, destroyed the most fruitful lands and made the city Köln panic. After this raid the main body of the Franks moved back over the Rhine with their booty. Some of the Franks remained in the Belgian wood
2 CONC s. When the Roman generals Magnus Maximus, Nanninus and Quintinus heard the news in Trier, they attacked those remaining Frankish forces and killed many of them. After this engagement Quintinus crossed the Rhine to punish the Fran
2 CONC ks in their own country, however his army was surrounded and beaten. Some Roman soldiers drowned in the marshes, others were killed by Franks, few made it back to their Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nanninus and Quintinus were replaced by Charietto and Syrus, who were again confronted by an attack of unindentified Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, after the fall of Magnus Maximus, Marcomer and Sunno held a short meeting about the recent attacks with the Frank Arbogastes, who was a general (magister militum) in the Roman army. The Franks delivered hostages as usual, a
2 CONC nd Arbogastes returned to his winter quarters in Trier.
2 CONT
2 CONT A couple of years later when Arbogastes had seized power and the West Roman army was nearly completely in the hands of Frankish mercenaries, he crossed the Rhine with a Roman army into Germania, because he hated his own kin. Marco
2 CONC mer was seen as leader with Chatti and Ampsivarii but the two did not engage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later we hear from the poet Claudian that Marcomer was arrested by Romans and banned to a villa in Tuscany. His brother Sunno crossed the Rhine and tried to settle himself as leader of the band of Marchomir, however he was killed 
2 CONC by his own people.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the later ''Liber Historiae Francorum'', Marcomer tried to unite the Franks after the death of Sunno. He proposed that the Franks should live under one king and proposed his own son Pharamond (whose earliest mention i
2 CONC s in this work, and who is considered mythological by scholars) for the kingship. This source does not relate whether Marcomer succeeded, but from other later sources that recall the account of Liber Historiae Francorum, the impre
2 CONC ssion may be gained that Pharamond was regarded as the first king of the Franks. However, modern scholars, such as Edward James, do not accept this account in the ''Liber Historiae Francorum'' as historical, because Marcomer is ca
2 CONC lled the son of the Trojan king Priam, which is an obvious impossibility. Another difficulty with this account is that earlier sources such as Gregory of Tours make it crystal clear that a century after Marcomer there were still m
2 CONC any Frankish kings, ruling over portions or separate tribes - indeed, it has been proposed that the word "ruler" may be more appropriate than "king", as there was at that time no one ruler over all the Frankish people. Clovis I, a
2 CONC ccording to Gregory of Tours, had several other rulers or kings killed in order to manipulate control and increase his territory, and through his machinations dethroned other leaders such as the Frankish counts of Triër, but even 
2 CONC he was not the single Frankish king, for tribes as the Thuringii, Chamavi and Bructeri continued their own structures. After Clovis' death, his empire was divided again amongst his sons who ruled simultaneously over different area
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Sources
2 CONT * Gregory of Tours, Historia Francorum, Book II, paragraph 9.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Claudian, Loeb classical Library, On Stilicho's Consulship (translation Platnauer)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Edward James, The Franks
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
