1 NOTE A Learned Discussion
2 CONT
2 CONT Series of e-mail with Chairman and Webmaster Michael DiMaio of the Historical Board for 'De Imperatoribus Romanis: An Online Encyclopedia of Roman Emperors' World Wide Web site: Available: [Online]: (http://www.roman-emperors.org/
2 CONC ) [29 August 2001].
2 CONT
2 CONT Amy Forliti, Associated Press wrote: "Ancient Rome has entered the digital age. De Imperatoribus Romanis: An Online Encyclopedia of Roman Emperors, found at www.roman-emperors.org, contains original essays on many of the emperors 
2 CONC who ruled from BC 27 to 1453 AD. Complete with family trees, maps and hundreds of footnotes, the Web site is attracting users from the serious scholar to the curious moviegoer.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Web site was started in June 1996 by Michael DiMaio, a professor who teaches philosophy and Latin at Salve Regina University in Newport who wanted to compile biographies of all the Roman emperors in one high-quality resource.
2 CONT
2 CONT Collegues from around the world got on board, and later that year the Web site began. It has continued to grow ever since, all on volunteer efforts... And critics are paying attention. De Imperatoribus Romanis has received awards 
2 CONC and commendations from organizations as varied as the U.S. History Channel, Encyclopedia Britannica and Library Journal..."All of the essays are peer-reviewed before they're put on the Web site, and the authors have to have academ
2 CONC ic credentials." ...for full article see The Globe & Mail. Toronto 29 August 2001
2 CONT
2 CONT (http://www.roman-emperors.org/)
2 CONT
2 CONT On Wed, 29 Aug 2001, John H.M. Young wrote:
2 CONT
2 CONT What happened to Charlemagne and the Holy Roman Empire?
2 CONT
2 CONT
