2 CONT Encyclopedia.com writes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Holy Roman Empire
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT The Holy Roman Empire was a successor state to the empire founded in 800 by Charlemagne (see also Carolingians), who revived the title of Roman emperor in the West. According to Carolingian theory, the Roman Empire had merely been
2 CONC  suspended, not ended, by the abdication of the last Roman emperor in 476. In 800, Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne Roman emperor, probably perceived more as a personal title than as a reference to a particular territorial rule. F
2 CONC rom the death of Arnulf (899), the last Carolingian to hold the imperial title, until Otto's coronation in Rome by Pope John XII, various rulers bore the imperial title but exercised no authority; among them were Louis III, king o
2 CONC f Provence, and Berengar I, king of Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT On Sat, 1 Sep 2001, Michael DiMaio at dimaiom@salve.edu wrote:
2 CONT
2 CONT You have hit the nail on the head!
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John:
2 CONT
2 CONT I am in full agreement with the argument you received earlier:
2 CONT
2 CONT Repeat
2 CONT John, we define Roman empire as that founded by Augustus and connected to Augustus by Roman law. Hence, the Latin speaking empire fell in 476 in Italy and the eastern empire, connected to the west by law and through Constantine I,
2 CONC  perished with the fall of Constantinople in 1453. I am
2 CONT making a distinction that all professional historians make. Perhaps the best way to put it is that the byzantines considered themselves Romans and called themselves that and never thought of the Franks as such.
2 CONT
2 CONT New
2 CONT The Byzantine Empire is a direct continuation of the eastern Roman empire and merits inclusion in a site of Roman emperors. The Carolingian empire was a Frankish creation. The main thing "Roman" about the Holy Roman Empire was tha
2 CONC t several emperors had to go to Rome to be crowned. It was strictly a Germanic state, especially from Otto I on, and was only an imitation, not a lineal continuation, of the Roman empire. I have a doctoral field in Carolingian, so
2 CONC  I am certainly not opposed to spreading solid information on the internet about that field . Although the Carolingian/H.R.E. is more closely connected than are the empires of Tsars, Kaisers, and Napoleon, each of these areas coul
2 CONC d make a similar case to be included in DIR.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Weigel, Praeses Senior, DIR
2 CONT
