1 NOTE  Charlemagne, or Charles the Great, was the son of a Frankish King. After inheriting his father's throne, Charlemagne was converted to Christianity. Encouraged by the Pope to spread his religion, he began a series of military conq
2 CONC uests which were to lead to the formation the Holy Roman Empire. § Charlemagne's father was Pepin III, a King ofthe Franks, who was also known as Pepin the Short. When Peppin died in 768, his kingdom was inherited jointly by his t
2 CONC wo sons. However, Charlemagne's brother, Carloman, died just three years later, leaving Charlemagne the sole ruler. § Shortly afterward, Charlemagne began an extended military campaign designed to conquer neighboring territories a
2 CONC nd to convert them to Christianity. These were waged against the Saxons to the north (from 772 to 777) and the Lombards to the south (in 773). In 778 , Charlemagne began a drive into Spain to conquer the Moors, but he withdrew whe
2 CONC n some of the German tribes hehad recently overcome revolted. § Charlemagne spent the next seven years quelling various uprisings which finally ended with the surrender and baptism of the Saxon's Chief. Charlemagne there upon resu
2 CONC med his efforts at conquest and was able to subdue Bohemia, portions of northern Spain, and the Kingdom of the Avars (now Hungary). It was at this point that Pope Leo III asked Charlemagne to subdue an uprising of the Roman tribes
2 CONC . To reward Charlemagne for his success, the Pope crowned him Emperor Carolus Augustus in 800. § To consolidate his rule, Charlemagne established a capital at Aachen (now Aix-la-Chapelle in France). In addition to a palace, he bui
2 CONC lt a large university, and spent the remainder of his reign promoting education and the arts. He also established a stable government and initiated good relations with other countries. However, his efforts to establish a governmen
2 CONC t based on Christian principles did not survive much beyond his death since his sons were unable to maintain his authority, and the empire soon disintegrated. §
2 CONT
