1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==[https://www.geni.com/people/Pippin-the-Hunchback/6000000010526480839 Pépin / Pépin le Bossu / Pippin the Hunchback], (c. 769 – 811), 
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Charlemagne/6000000002457013227 CHARLEMAGNE] & his mistress [http://www.geni.com/people/Himiltrude-concubine-of-Charlemagne/6000000008187409439   HIMILTRUD], (daughter of ---.  "Himiltrude nobili puell
2 CONC a" is named mother of "Pippinum" in the Gesta Mettensium) had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''[http://www.geni.com/people/Pippin-the-Hunchback-Carolingian-son-of-Charlemagne-Himiltrude/6000000010526480839   PEPIN “le Bossu”] ([770]-Abbey of Prüm 811)'''. He is named, and his parentage recorded, in the Gesta Mettensium,
2 CONC  which specifies that he was born before his father married Queen Hildegard. He rebelled against his father in 792, allegedly due to the cruelty of Queen Fastrada, was judged by an assembly at Regensburg and imprisoned in the Abbe
2 CONC y of St-Gallen. He was transferred to the Abbey of Prüm in 794. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CAROLINGIANS.htm#_ftnref163
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Accounts describe Pépin as normally proportioned with attractive features. However, his looks were marred by a spinal deformity from which his nickname is derived.
2 CONT
2 CONT Due to his disability, and possible illegitimacy, Pépin was never likely to inherit much of the Frankish domains. Nevertheless, Charles treated his son well, giving him precedence over his younger brothers as was appropriate for h
2 CONC is age. Pépin was an amiable fellow, and he grew to be a well-liked member of Charles' court. The hunchbacked prince probably held some hope for succession from his father. In addition, Pépin was an easy target for discontented no
2 CONC bles, who lavished sympathies on him and lamented the treatment his mother had received when Charles had put her aside in order to marry a Lombard princess, Desiderata.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 780, Charles formally disinherited Pépin and had the pope baptize his third son, Carloman, who now received the name Pépin. The name had a special significance as Pépin had been a recurring name in the Carolingian dynasty. This
2 CONC  move may have been prompted by Hildegard, Charles' wife and Carloman's mother, who felt her son's inheritance expectations were threatened by the hunchbacked prince.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pépin was allowed to remain at court, and Charles continued to give the boy precedence over his younger brothers. Pépin also remained a popular "friend" of discontented nobles, and in 792, several counts played upon Pépin's dislik
2 CONC e for his brothers to convince the deformed prince to play the figurehead in their rebellion. The conspirators planned to kill Charles, his wife Hildegarde, and his three sons by her. Pépin the Hunchback would then be set upon the
2 CONC  throne as a more sympathetic (and more easily manipulated) king. The day of the assassination, Pépin pretended to be ill in order to meet with the plotters. The scheme nearly succeeded, but a Lombard deacon named Fardulf ultimate
2 CONC ly exposed it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charlemagne held an assembly at Regensburg to try the conspirators, and all were found guilty of high treason and ordered executed. Charles seemed still to have held fond feelings for his first son, however, for Pépin's sentence w
2 CONC as commuted. Instead, Pépin was forced to enter the monastery of Prüm to live out the rest of his life as a monk. Pépin died there some twenty years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~greenefamily/lape/pafg72.htm#13198
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pepin_the_Hunchback
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''SO MANY PEPINS ! - DISAMBIGUATION TEMPLATE for discussion & editing''' from [http://www.geni.com/projects/Charlemagne-Emperor-of-the-West/1550 Charlemagne Project]=
2 CONT
2 CONT From youngest/ most chronologically recent:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[http://www.geni.com/people/P%C3%A9pin-Carloman-roi-d-Italie/6000000003962328571 Pepin d810]''', King of Italy, originally called Carloman, son of [http://www.geni.com/people/Charlemagne/6000000002457013227  Charlemagne] & [htt
2 CONC p://www.geni.com/people/Hildegard-von-Vinzgau/6000000003715297906?through=6000000002457013227    Hildegarde]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[http://www.geni.com/people/Pippin-the-Hunchback-Carolingian-son-of-Charlemagne-Himiltrude/6000000010526480839   Pepin The Hunchback d811], eldest, but  'illegitimate' son of [http://www.geni.com/people/Charlemagne/600000000245
2 CONC 7013227  Charlemagne]  &  [http://www.geni.com/people/Himiltrude-concubine-of-Charlemagne/6000000008187409439?through=6000000002457013227  Himiltrude]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[http://www.geni.com/people/P%C3%A9pin-III-le-Bref-Roi-des-Francs/5475363828490111452  Pepin III d768]''', '”le Bref” The Short', Mayor of the Palace of Neustria, King of the Franks, father [http://www.geni.com/people/Charlemag
2 CONC ne/6000000002457013227  Charlemagne]  - son of [http://www.geni.com/people/Charles-Martel-Duke-of-the-Franks/288390320120001964  Charles Martel ] & [http://www.geni.com/people/Rotrude-de-Tr%C3%A8ves/6000000002154297114?through=547
2 CONC 5363828490111452 Rotrude]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[http://www.geni.com/people/Pepin-II-d-H%C3%A9ristal-Mayor-of-the-Palace-of-Austrasia/5535353244920040751   Pepin II   d714]''', 'The Younger' or 'The Middle' 'of Heristal', Mayor of the Palace of Austrasia, Neustria & Burgundy
2 CONC , paternal grandfather of [http://www.geni.com/people/P%C3%A9pin-III-le-Bref-Roi-des-Francs/5475363828490111452  Pepin III d768] through [http://www.geni.com/people/Charles-Martel-Duke-of-the-Franks/288390320120001964  Charles Mar
2 CONC tel ] - son of  [http://www.geni.com/people/St-Beggue-of-Austrasia/6000000000860259173?through=5535353244920040751 Begga] & [http://www.geni.com/people/Ansigisel-de-Metz-Mayor-of-the-Palace-of-Austrasia/5535400391550125499?through
2 CONC =5535353244920040751 Ansegis]
