2 CONT Sancho I (Portuguese pronunciation: [ˈsɐʃu]), nicknamed the Populator (Portuguese o Povoador), second monarch of Portugal, was born on 11 November 1154 in Coimbra and died on 26 March 1212 in the same city. He was the second but o
2 CONC nly surviving legitimate son and fourth child of Afonso I Henriques of Portugal by his wife, Maud of Savoy. Sancho succeeded his father in 1185. He used the title King of the Algarve and/or King of Silves between 1189 and 1191
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1170, Sancho was knighted by his father, King Afonso I, and from then on he became his second in command, both administratively and militarily. At this time, the independence of Portugal (declared in 1139) was not firmly establ
2 CONC ished. The kings of León and Castile were trying to re-annex the country and the Roman Catholic Church was late in giving its blessing and approval. Due to this situation Afonso I had to search for allies within the Iberian Penins
2 CONC ula. Portugal made an alliance with the Kingdom of Aragon and together they fought Castile and León. To secure the agreement, Infante Sancho of Portugal married, in 1174, Infanta Dulce of Aragon, younger sister of King Alfonso II 
2 CONC of Aragon. Aragon was thus the first Iberian kingdom to recognize the independence of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the death of Afonso I in 1185, Sancho I became the second king of Portugal. Coimbra was the centre of his kingdom; Sancho terminated the exhausting and generally pointless wars against his neighbours for control of the Galici
2 CONC an borderlands. Instead, he turned all his attentions to the south, towards the Moorish small kingdoms (called taifas) that still thrived. With Crusader help he took Silves in 1191. Silves was an important city of the South, an ad
2 CONC ministrative and commercial town with population estimates around 20,000 people. Sancho ordered the fortification of the city and built a castle which is today an important monument of Portuguese heritage. However, military attent
2 CONC ion soon had to be turned again to the North, where León and Castile threatened again the Portuguese borders. Silves was again lost to the Moors. It should be noted that the global Muslim population had climbed to about 6 per cent
2 CONC  as against the Christian population of 12 per cent by 1200.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho I dedicated much of his reign to political and administrative organization of the new kingdom. He accumulated a national treasure, supported new industries and the middle class of merchants. Moreover, he created several new
2 CONC  towns and villages (like Guarda in 1199) and took great care in populating remote areas in the northern Christian regions of Portugal, notably with Flemings and Burgundians – hence the nickname "the Populator". The king was also 
2 CONC known for his love of knowledge and literature. Sancho I wrote several books of poems and used the royal treasure to send Portuguese students to European universities. [edit] Ancestors Sancho's ancestors in three generations Sanch
2 CONC o I of Portugal 	Father: Afonso I of Portugal 	Father's father: Henry, Count of Portugal 	Father's father's father: Henry of Burgundy Father's father's mother: unknown Father's mother: Theresa, Countess of Portugal 	Father's mothe
2 CONC r's father: Alfonso VI of León and Castile Father's mother's mother: Jimena Muñoz Mother: Maud of Savoy 	Mother's father: Amadeus III of Savoy 	Mother's father's father: Humbert II of Savoy Mother's father's mother: Gisela of Burg
2 CONC undy Mother's mother: Adelaide 	Mother's mother's father: unknown Mother's mother's mother: unknown [edit] Marriage and descendants
