2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: b. 1152, Barcelona
2 CONT
2 CONT d. 1196, Perpignan, Roussillon 
2 CONT
2 CONT count of Barcelona from 1162 and king of Aragon from 1164.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Ramón Berenguer IV, Alfonso succeeded his father as count of Barcelona and his mother as ruler of Aragon, thus associating the two countries under the house of Barcelona--a union that was destined to be permanent. Arago
2 CONC nese involvement in France became steadily greater during Alfonso's reign. Nevertheless, the conquest of Teruel (1171) opened the way for the conquest of Valencia; and, in 1179, the pact of Cazorla with his ally, Alfonso VIII of C
2 CONC astile, fixed the future zones of reconquest for the two countries. In his will Alfonso followed the Spanish custom of dividing his kingdom; Provence was thus lost to the Aragonese crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Copyright © 1994-2001 Encyclopædia Britannica, Inc.
2 CONT
2 CONT History: Aragon, history of 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Romans defeated the Carthaginians during the Punic Wars, Aragón became part of the Roman province of Hispania Tarraconensis. The Visigoths conquered the region late in the 5th century, the Moors in the 8th century. Subse
2 CONC quently the region was incorporated with the kingdom of Navarre. In 1035 Ramiro I , a son of the Navarrese ruler Sancho III , established Aragón as an independent kingdom. Navarre was annexed in 1076, and during the next 100 years
2 CONC  additional territory was added by successful wars against the Moors. In 1137 Aragón was united with Catalonia and Barcelona. Aragón grew into a leading Mediterranean naval power around the port of Barcelona. The kings of Aragón g
2 CONC ained possession of the Balearic Islands, Sicily, Sardinia, and Naples during the next two centuries. In 1238 the important city of Valencia was captured by Aragón from the Moors. The marriage of Ferdinand II of Aragón (later Ferd
2 CONC inand V  of Castile) to Isabella I of Castile united those two regions. Formal merger of the two kingdoms took place on the accession of Charles I in 1516, but Aragón retained its own administration and representative institutions
2 CONC  until the end of the 17th century. Area, 47,669 sq km (18,405 sq mi).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso II Raimond, Rey de Aragón also went by the nick-name of Alfonso 'the Chaste' (?).3 He succeeded to the title of Conde de Barcelona in 1162.4 He gained the title of Rey Alfonso II de Aragón in 1162.2 He succeeded to the tit
2 CONC le of Comte de Provence in 1166.5
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
