1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''DHUODA [Doda] (->2 Feb 843)''' 
2 CONT d/o NN 
2 CONT x (Aix-la-Chapelle 24 Jun 824) BERNARD ([795]-executed Toulouse Saint-Sernin [Jan/Jun] 844) Comte d'Autun; Marquis de Septimanie; Count of Barcelona, s/o  GUILLAUME Comte de Toulouse, Marquis de Septimanie & Cunigundis 
2 CONT * GUILLAUME (29 Nov 826-killed in battle Barcelona 850)
2 CONT *BERNARD "Plantevelue" (Uzès 22 Mar 841-[20 Jun 885/Aug 886]) Comte d'Autun 864/69; Comte de Rodez; Comte d'Auvergne
2 CONT *NN ([843/44]-) x VULGRIN [I] Comte d'Angoulême, s/o VULFARD [d'Angoulême] & Susanna de Paris (-3 May 886, bur Angoulême Saint-Cybard).] https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/FRANKISH%20NOBILITY.htm#Bernarddiedafter844B
2 CONT ----
2 CONT She was a scholar and wrote a Manual which is piously Christian. According to later romances she was a pagan. There  is nothing to exclude this possibility. She might have been daughter of [http://www.geni.com/people/Sanche-I-duc-
2 CONC de-Gascogne/6000000000424646987 Sanche I Loup de Gascogne]. She has also been called sister, or sister-in-law, of [http://www.geni.com/people/Louis-I-le-Pieux-roi-des-Francs/6000000001266578142 Louis I of France]. Zuckerman dismis
2 CONC ses the idea that she might have been a daughter of Charlemagne because of the problem of consanguinity, namely that she would have been a half 2nd cousin of her husband. Moriarty also questions her parentage.
2 CONT
2 CONT DHUODA DE GASCUÑA, UNA MUJER EJEMPLAR DEL SIGLO IX
2 CONT
2 CONT Desde los primeros tiempos, las mujeres recibían un ajuar de bienes domésticos y personales que, para desgracia de Tácito, ahora también incluye una gran cantidad de joyas y vestidos costosos.
2 CONT
2 CONT En la aristocracia, el servicio real y de guerra absorbían las energías de los hombres, de modo que la supervisión de las propiedades de la familia se dejaba en manos de las mujeres. Dhuoda, la esposa de Bernardo de Septimania, pe
2 CONC rmaneció en su casa, en Uzes, y dirigió las posesiones rurales, mientras él pasaba el tiempo en la corte como chambelán Imperial.
2 CONT
2 CONT Los padres de Dhuoda fueron Sancho I López, duque de Gascuña (775-816) y Aznárez de Aragón (hija de Aznar Galindo I, conde de Aragón). Nacida en una familia de la alta nobleza a principios del siglo IX (c.810), la casaron el año 8
2 CONC 24 con Bernardo, Duque de Septimania y primo de Carlomagno. El hijo de ambos, Guillermo, nació en noviembre del 826. Poco después -exactamente cuándo y por qué no se sabe- Bernardo envió a su mujer a Uzes, en el sudoeste de Franci
2 CONC a, donde parece haber pasado el resto de su vida, separada de su marido. Aprendió a vivir sola, a gobernar los campos, a pedir préstamos a cristianos y judíos para armar a su marido (otra costumbre que conservan). En el 841 nació 
2 CONC Bernardo, a quien el padre se llevó a la Corte al niño a toda prisa, sin bautizar. Guillermo estaba en la corte de Carlos el Calvo, como prueba de la lealtad de Bernardo hacia el rey. Dhuoda, sola en su castillo, le escribió un ma
2 CONC nual de educación a su primogénito. En el tratado le explica sus ideales religiosos y mundanos, "... se trata de un notable retrato de una dama digna y culta, golpeada, pero no abatida por las dificultades de la vida".
2 CONT
2 CONT El manual expone muy claramente el doble sistema de valores que Dhuoda deseaba presentar a su hijo: el servicio a Dios, por supuesto, pero también la adecuada defensa del ideal de una existencia noble en esta vida. Dhuoda insiste 
2 CONC en que debe actuar noblemente, respetando los rangos y haciendo dádiva, pero mostrando también cortesía con todos, no sólo con sus iguales. Dhuoda está convencida de que esta conducta, cuando se combina con la devoción cristiana, 
2 CONC le traerá tanto felicidad terrenal como la salvación eterna. Su libro es un notable retrato de la propia Dhuoda con todo su anhelo humanos de una vida normal con sus hijos, pero con una auténtica devoción religiosa y la dignidad y
2 CONC  el autocontrol que se podía esperar de una mujer de su alcurnia.
2 CONT
2 CONT La reina carolingia supervisaba el palacio, los estados reales, y representaba a su marido en ausencia de éste. La posición la adquiría cuando era ungida y coronada, las concubinas no llegaron a tener este poder. En su Capitulare 
2 CONC de Villis, Carlomagno declaró que lo que la reina ordenara a los jueces, ministros, senescales y escanciadores, debía ejecutarse al pie de la letra. En una época en que no se distinguía entre el poder privado y público de un gober
2 CONC nante, era éste un poder enorme. Hincmaro de Reims explicó, dos generaciones después, que la reina, con ayuda del chambelán, también estaba a cargo del tesoro real. Agregó que el rey no podía verse implicado en tales trivialidades
2 CONC  domésticas. Las reinas merovingias también tenían acceso al palacio y al tesoro, pero el chambelán ejercía las funciones administrativas, que luego pasarán a las reinas carolingias.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Dhuoda of Gascony, Countess of Agen & Septimania 1
2 CONT
2 CONT Alias: Duodene (Dhoude) Liegarde
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: ABT 804 in Gascony, France 1
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: AFT 2 FEB 842/43 1
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Sancho Loupez Duke\Prince of Gascony b: ABT 772 in Gascony, France 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: __________ Aznarez b: ABT 788 in Aragon, Spain
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Bernard I Count of Autun, Margrave Septimania b: BEF 804 in Autun, Saone-et-Loire, Bourgogne, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: 29 JUN 824 in Aix-la-Chapelle, Aachen, Germany 1
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT William b: 826 d: 849
2 CONT
2 CONT Rosalinda (Sancha) of Toulouse b: ABT 825 in Toulouse, Haute-Garonne, Midi-Pyrenees, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Aton Trencavel, Vicomte de Rouergue b: ABT 830 in Sauveterre de Rouergue, Languedoc, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernard II Plantevelue Comte d'Auvergne b: 22 MAR 840/41 in Uzes, Languedoc, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes [PJ]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dhuoda was the wife of Bernhard, a military and political figure in the early medieval French court. In 843, she finished writing a manual for her 16-year-old son, William. Bernhard had put Dhuoda away in a castle and handed their
2 CONC  son over to Charles the Bald as a hostage. Dhuoda, confined like Rapunzel, reached out to her exiled firstborn by writing an instruction manual for him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Bernhard was vying for favor among Charlemagne's squabbling grandsons, of whom Charles was one. He was also evolving into a monster as he gained power. Cruel, lecherous, and political, he tortured and maimed his enemies
2 CONC . He seduced the previous king's wife. He also removed a second son from Dhuoda even before the baby was baptized. Bernhard's enemies were no better. One by one, they had the rest of his family blinded or murdered. And, hostage or
2 CONC  no, Charles the Bald finally beheaded Bernhard, only a year after Dhuoda wrote her book.
2 CONT
2 CONT This was an age when few men, and almost no women, could write. Yet the manual shows a fine grasp of theology, philology, philosophy, and mathematics. Translator James Marchand judges that it's written in fairly good, but certainl
2 CONC y not fluent, Latin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dhuoda speaks in a unique voice, slipping from poetry to prose and back again so deftly it's hard to find the seams. She has a first-class knowledge of the classics. She loves words, word games, arithmetic, and the mystic power of
2 CONC  numbers. Her religious conviction is absolute, and she's fervently committed to William as his loving mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT She begins with a poem praising God and asking for William's well-being. She also spells her name out in an acrostic. Later, she calls up numbers to direct her meditations. In her thinking, four has a special perfection. Four is t
2 CONC he number of letters in the Latin word Deus, for God. And the first letter of Deus, D, is the fourth letter of the alphabet. All that is typical medieval thinking rendered with a fluency that suggests a mind chafing for somewhere 
2 CONC to go. But her playfulness is gone by the time she finishes the book with her own epitaph,
2 CONT
2 CONT Dhuoda's body, formed of earth, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lies Buried in this tomb. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT O King, forgive her sins ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Great Hagios, unlock her chains ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Almus, give her rest ...
2 CONT
2 CONT When he was 23, young William had proven to be, in the words of one old chronicle, "too much the son of [his father] in flesh and in habits." So Charles the Bald had him beheaded as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dhuoda's manual on how to grow up in the Grace of God had done scant good without her presence behind it. What she did accomplish was to leave us a glimpse into the heart and mind of a woman living in the worst of times -- one rar
2 CONC e woman who escaped the veil of anonymity shrouding all women -- twelve centuries ago. Engines of Our Ingenuity
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes [JW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Turton has Bernard's wife as Duodene, a daughter of Charlemagne, the marriage date being 824.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: Newsgroup: soc.genealogy.medieval, at groups - google.com
2 CONT
2 CONT Page: Alan B Wilson, 12 Jun 1998
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://home.infionline.net/~ddisse/dhuoda.html
2 CONT
2 CONT The first we know of Dhuoda is that she was married in 824 in the imperial palace at Aachen to Bernard, Duke of Septimania (c.802-844). Her own family was noble, but she tell us nothing specific about them. She learned to write fl
2 CONC uently in Latin at a time when relatively few lay persons did. In 826 she and Bernard had a son, William; in 841 they had another, who would be named Bernard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Until the 840 death of Charlemagne's son Louis I, Bernard of Septimania was a powerful figure: he was an important military leader who briefly acted as Louis' chancellor, and he was godfather to Louis' youngest son, Charles. There
2 CONC  are contemporary accounts accusing Bernard of financial malpractice and of adultery with Louis' queen, but these were written by his political opponents who, like Bernard, were seeking to gain and hold positions of power.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Louis' death, the Carolingian empire began to collapse. His eldest son held the title of emperor, but there was fierce conflict about what son would rule what portion of land. For three years, there was a state of war. Bernard 
2 CONC was officially on the side of his godson, Charles the Bald, but at one battle in 841, Bernard either was late sending his troops to Charles' aid or actually betrayed him (depending on which sources you read). At any rate, Charles 
2 CONC was furious, and Bernard sent his 15-year-old son William to his court, both to serve the 20-year-old Charles and to act as a hostage to his father's future loyalty.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT BERNARD, son of GUILLAUME Comte de Toulouse, Marquis de Septimanie & his first wife Cunigundis --- ([795]-executed Toulouse Saint-Sernin [Jan/Jun] 844).  The Vita S. Willelmi names “Bernardo…et Gaucelino” as sons of Guillaume[472]
2 CONC .  "Willelmus…comes" names "filiabus meis et filiis Barnardo, Witchario, Gotcelmo, Helimbruch" (version two: "filios meos et filias Witcario, Hildehelmo et Helinbruch") in his charter dated 14 Dec 804 (version two: dated 15 Dec 80
2 CONC 4) for the foundation of the monastery of Gellone, Bernard being named in only one of the versions[473].  Flodoard refers to "Bernardo comiti Tolosano, propinquo suo [Teodulfo comite]"[474].  Thegan's Vita Hludowici Imperatoris re
2 CONC cords that "quondam duce Bernhardo, qui erat de stirpe regali" was accused of violating "Iudith reginam" but comments that this was all lies[475].  Comte d'Autun until 830.  Marquis de Septimanie until 831.  He was installed as Co
2 CONC unt of Barcelona in 827 or before: Einhard's Annales name "Bernhardus…Barcinonæ comes" in 827[476].  It is not certain whether this means that the "March of Spain" was at that time part of the marquisate of Septimania.  It is poss
2 CONC ible that Bernard was appointed to Barcelona after the disgrace of Bero in 820, but no primary source has been identified which confirms that this is correct.  Einhard's Annales name "Bernhardus…Barcinonæ comes" in 827[477].  The 
2 CONC Annales Fuldenses record that "Barnhardus comes Barcinonensis" was made camerarius in the palace in 829[478].  Emperor Louis I appointed "Bernard Duke of Septimania" as his chamberlain and entrusted his son Charles to him, but he 
2 CONC "recklessly abused the imperial power…and undermined it entirely".  He was banished to Septimania in [Apr 830] by the emperor's sons who rebelled against their father[479].  The Annales Bertiniani record that "præfatus Bernardus" 
2 CONC fled to Barcelona in 830[480].  The Vita Hludowici Imperatoris records that "Bernhardus" fled into exile in Spain and was deprived of his honours [in 831][481].  The same source records that "Werinus et Bernhardus comites" gathere
2 CONC d a force in Burgundy and reached "Matronam fluvium" [in 834][482].  The Annales Bertiniani record that "Bernardus comes marcæ Hispanicæ" was sentenced to death in 844[483].  The Annales Xantenses record that "Bernhardus comes" wa
2 CONC s killed "a Karolo" in 844, after which "filio Bernhardi" and "Pippinus rex Aquitainiæ, filius Pippini" defeated the king's army[484].  The Annales Fuldenses record that "Karolus" killed "Bernhardum Barcenonensium ducem" in 844 "i
2 CONC ncautem et nihil ab eo suspicantem"[485]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m (Aix-la-Chapelle 24 Jun 824) DHUODA [Doda], daughter of --- (-after 2 Feb 843).  The Manual of Dhuoda records the marriage "anno XI domino nostro Ludoico VIII Kal Iul" at "Aquisgrani palatio" of Doda and Bernard[486].  The Ma
2 CONC nual was written in 843 and consists of her instructions to her son. 
2 CONT '''
2 CONT Bernard & his wife had [three] children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         GUILLAUME (29 Nov 826-killed in battle Barcelona 850).  The Manual of Dhuoda records the birth "in XIII anno regni eius III Kal Dec" of "fili primogenite [Willelme]" of Doda and Bernard[487].  [Comte de [Bordeaux?].  Du
2 CONC ke of Gascony [845/46].  The Chronicon Fontanellensis records in 848 that "Nortmanni" captured “Budegalim urbem...et Ducem eiusdem Guilhelmum noctu” in 848[488].  The editor of the Recueil des Historiens des Gaules et de la France
2 CONC  says that "Willelmus iste diversus esse videtur à Willelmo Bernardi Septimaniæ Ducis filio", noting that he had succeeded “Siguino Duci Wasconiæ et Comiti Burdegalens” [Seguin [II], see the document GASCONY], who had been killed 
2 CONC two years earlier “à Nortmannis”, as “Dux...Burdegalensis” (no sources cited)[489].  The primary source which confirms this information has not been identified, but once the possibility is excluded that Guillaume Duke of Gascony w
2 CONC as the son of “Bertrand comte de Bazas”, as discussed in GASCONY, it seems a reasonable assumption that it is correct.  This hypothesis assumes that Guillaume was rehabilitated quickly by the Carolingian rulers after the death of 
2 CONC his father, whose execution was ordered in 844 by Charles II “le Chauve” King of the West Franks as noted above.]  The Annales Bertiniani record that "Guilhelmus filius Bernardi" captured "Impurium et Barcinonam" in 848, and captu
2 CONC red "Aledramnum et Isembardum comites" in the march of Spain in 850 but was himself captured and killed at Barcelona[490].  The Chronicon Fontanellensis records that "Wilhelmus filius Bernardi Ducis" captured Barcelona and expelle
2 CONC d "Aledranno custode illius urbis et limitis Hispanici", dated to [849/50], in a later passage recording that "Wilhelmo invasore urbis Barcinonæ" captured "Isembardus filius Warini et Aledrannus" but that Guillaume was later defea
2 CONC ted and killed by "factione Aledranni et quorundam Gothorum"[491].  These sources do not apply the title dux/comes to Guillaume, nor do they prove that Guillaume in 850 was the same person as Guillaume in 848, but it appears to be
2 CONC  a reasonable assumption that that co-identity is correct. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         BERNARD "Plantevelue" (Uzès 22 Mar 841-[20 Jun 885/Aug 886]).  The Manual of Dhuoda records the birth "XI Kal Apr", in the year following the death of Emperor Louis, at "Uzecia urbes" of the second child [Bernard] of Do
2 CONC da and Bernard[492].  The Annales Bertiniani name "rex markiones Bernardum scilicet Tolosæ et iterum Bernardum Gothiæ, itemque Bernardum alium" in 868[493].  Lay Abbot of Brioude 857/68.  Comte d'Autun 864/69, deposed.  Comte de R
2 CONC odez 864/74.  Comte d'Auvergne after 872. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.         [--- ([843/44]-).  The Chronicle of Adémar de Chabannes records that "Vulgrimnus" inherited "Aginnum...urbem" through "sororem Willelmi Tolosani" whom he had married[494].  The identity of "Willelmi Tolosani" is unclear
2 CONC .  The Histoire Générale de Languedoc speculates that he was Guillaume Comte de Bordeaux (whom it calls comte de Toulouse), son of Bernard Marquis de Septimanie and his wife Dhuoda[495].  If Vulgrin’s wife was Bernard and Dhouda’s
2 CONC  daughter, she is assumed to have been born in [842/44] as the Manual of Dhuoda states that the couple’s second son Bernard was his mother's second child [assuming that female children were not simply ignored by the Manual][496]. 
2 CONC  The editors of the third edition of the Histoire Générale de Languedoc suggest that the passage in Adhémar de Chabannes refers "probablement [à] Guillaume comte de Bordeaux" [Duke of Gascony][497].  Jaurgain suggests that this su
2 CONC ggestion is correct, on the basis that Guillaume was the son of “Bertrand comte de Bazas et d’Agen” and therefore entitled to pass Agen to his brother-in-law[498].  Unfortunately, he cites no source which confirms that Bertrand wa
2 CONC s “comte d’Agen”, nor any source which confirms beyond doubt the co-identity between Bertrand’s son Guillaume and Guillaume Comte [de Bordeaux [Duke of Gascony].  As discussed in the document GASCONY (see the section recording Duk
2 CONC e Guillaume who died in 850), the existence of this “Bertrand” in fact seems based on a misinterpretation by Marca of an undated later charter under which Vulgrin [I]’s grandson Bernard Comte de Périgord restored the abbey of Sain
2 CONC t-Sour de Genouillac (see the section Comtes de Périgord in the document ANGOULÊME, LA MARCHE, PERIGORD).  “Guillaume comte de Bordeaux” was therefore probably the same person as Guillaume, son of Bernard Comte d’Autun, Marquis de
2 CONC  Septimanie, in which case the supposedly revised suggestion in the third edition of the Histoire Générale de Languedoc was in fact the same as the suggestion made in the earlier edition.  m VULGRIN [I] Comte d'Angoulême, son of V
2 CONC ULFARD [d'Angoulême] & his wife Susanna de Paris (-3 May 886, bur Angoulême Saint-Cybard).] 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/FRANKISH%20NOBILITY.htm#Bernarddiedafter844B
2 CONT ----
