1 NOTE story
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=3c4248e5-7519-4a0f-ad0b-4f5ab35bd7f6&tid=6268114&pid=-1059262664
1 NOTE story
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=3c4248e5-7519-4a0f-ad0b-4f5ab35bd7f6&tid=6268114&pid=-1059262664
1 NOTE Fue hija de:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho I López, duque de Gascuña (775-816) y Aznárez de Aragón (hija de Aznar Galindo I, conde de Aragón).
2 CONT
2 CONT Nacida en una familia de la alta nobleza a principios del siglo IX (c.810), la casaron el año 824 con Bernardo, Duque de Septimania y primo de Carlomagno. El hijo de ambos, Guillermo, nació en noviembre del 826.
2 CONT
2 CONT Poco después -exactamente cuándo y por qué no se sabe- Bernardo envió a su mujer a Uzes, en el sudoeste de Francia, donde parece haber pasado el resto de su vida, separada de su marido. Aprendió a vivir sola, a gobernar los campos
2 CONC , a pedir présta
2 CONT mos a cristianos y judíos para armar a su marido (otra costumbre que conservan).
2 CONT
2 CONT En el 841 nació Bernardo, a quien el padre se llevó a la Corte al niño a toda prisa, sin bautizar. Guillermo estaba en la corte de Carlos el Calvo, como prueba de la lealtad de Bernardo hacia el rey. Dhuoda, sola en su castillo, l
2 CONC e escribió un ma
2 CONT nual de educación a su primogénito. En el tratado le explica sus ideales religiosos y mundanos, "... se trata de un notable retrato de una dama digna y culta, golpeada, pero no abatida por las dificultades de la vida".
2 CONT
2 CONT El manual expone muy claramente el doble sistema de valores que Dhuoda deseaba presentar a su hijo: el servicio a Dios, por supuesto, pero también la adecuada defensa del ideal de una existencia noble en esta vida. Dhuoda insiste 
2 CONC en que debe actu
2 CONT ar noblemente, respetando los rangos y haciendo dádiva, pero mostrando también cortesía con todos, no sólo con sus iguales. Dhuoda está convencida de que esta conducta, cuando se combina con la devoción cristiana, le traerá tanto 
2 CONC felicidad terren
2 CONT al como la salvación eterna. Su libro es un notable retrato de la propia Dhuoda con todo su anhelo humanos de una vida normal con sus hijos, pero con una auténtica devoción religiosa y ladignidad y el autocontrol que se podía espe
2 CONC rar de una muje
2 CONT r de su alcurnia.
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-20792
1 NOTE Dhuoda was the wife of Bernard of Septimania and the author of the Liber Manualis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Dhuoda's parentage is unknown, but her education and her connections indicate that her family was wealthy. She married Bernard, Duke of Septimania, at Aachen on the 24th of June, 824. Bernard was the son of William of Gellone, Cha
2 CONC rlemagne's cousin, who was later named the patron saint of knights.[1] Bernard was the godson of King Louis the Pious.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their first son, William, was born on the 29 November, 826, and the second, Bernard, on 22 March, 841. In the interim, the couple probably lived apart most of the time: she in Uzes in Southern France, and he at court in Aachen. On
2 CONC e scholar has suggested that a daughter was born in 844, as one chronicler reports the marriage of William's sister.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT What little we know of her life comes from her book, the Liber Manualis, or Manual, which Dhuoda wrote for her elder son, William, between 841 and 843. It was a work written when Dhuoda had been separated from both her husband and
2 CONC  her two sons, the victim of the conflicting ambitions of Charlemagne's descendants. William had been sent as a hostage to the court of Charles the Bald in order to secure the loyalty of his father; Bernard was taken from her befo
2 CONC re his baptism and was sent to Aquitaine in order to keep him safe. We have no evidence that Dhuoda ever saw either of her children again; indeed, because of references she makes to her ill-health, it is quite possible that she di
2 CONC ed shortly after she completed her work.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband, Bernard, was condemned for rebellion and executed in 844. Of her sons, William was killed in 850, Bernard in 885.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Liber Manualis
2 CONT The Liber Manualis consists of seventy-three chapters as well as an introduction, invocation, and prologue. The book is full of practical moral directives aimed to help guide her sons through life. It is an invaluable document bot
2 CONC h for the general history of the Frankish era, but also for the history of education and the standards of education which could be attained by women even within the prescriptive bounds of early medieval society. It contains numero
2 CONC us quotations from and allusions to the Bible, and some references to secular writers, though some of the references are incorrect and the Latin is not overly-polished.
2 CONT
2 CONT The work is known from a manuscript of the seventeenth century in the Bibliothèque Nationale, Paris, and from fragments of a manuscript of the Carolingian epoch, found in the library of Nîmes.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT ^ McKitterick, Rosamond. 1989. The Carolingians and the Written Word. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. 
2 CONT ^ Gies, Frances and Joseph. Marriage and the Family in the Middle Ages, p. 80 (Harper & Row, 1987) ISBN 0-06-015791-7 
2 CONT This article incorporates text from the public-domain Catholic Encyclopedia of 1913. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT The Liber manualis (full title: Liber manualis Dhuodane quem ad filium suum transmisit Wilhelmum) has been edited and translated:
2 CONT
2 CONT Thiebaux, Marcelle (ed. and tr.). Dhuoda. Handbook for her warrior son. Cambridge Medieval Classics 8. Cambridge, 1998. (English translation) 
2 CONT Riché, Pierre (ed.), Bernard de Vregille and Claude Mondésert (trs.), Dhuoda: Manuel pour mon Fils. Sources Chrétiennes 225. Paris, 1975.(French translation) 
2 CONT Bondurand, Édouard (ed. and tr.). Le Manuel de Dhuoda. Paris: Picard, 1887. French translation. PDF of reprint available from Gallica 
2 CONT Mabillon, Jacques (ed.). PL 106.109-118. Partial edition, available from Documenta Catholica Omnia 
2 CONT Neel, Carol (tr.). Handbook for William. A Carolingian woman's counsel for her son. Regents Studies in Medieval Culture. Lincoln: University of Nebraska Press, 1991. 
2 CONT Thiebaux, Marcelle (tr.). The Writings of Medieval Women. New York, 1987. 65-79. Selective translation into English. 
2 CONT A selection of secondary literature in English and French:
2 CONT
2 CONT Cherewatuk, Karen. "Speculum Matris: Duoda's Manual." Florilegium 10 (1988-91): 49-64. Available online 
2 CONT Claussen, Martin A. "God and Man in Dhuoda's Liber Manualis." SCH 27 (1990): 43-52. 
2 CONT Claussen, Martin A. "Fathers of Power and Mothers of Authority: Dhuoda and the Liber Manualis. French Historical Studies 19 (1996): 785-809. 
2 CONT Dronke, Peter. Women Writers of the Middle Ages. Cambridge, 1984. 
2 CONT Durrens, Janine. Dhuoda, duchesse de Septimanie. Clairsud, 2003. 
2 CONT Godard, Jocelyne. Dhuoda. La Carolingienne. Le Sémaphore, 1997. 
2 CONT Marchand, James. "The Frankish Mother: Dhuoda." In Medieval Woman Writers, ed. Katharina M. Wilson. Athens, 1984. 1-29. Includes selective translation of the Liber Manualis. 
2 CONT Nelson, Janet L. "Dhuoda." In Lay intellectuals in the Carolingian world, ed. Patrick Wormald and Janet L. Nelson. Cambridge, 2007. 106-20. 
2 CONT Stofferahn, Steven A. "The many faces in Dhuoda's mirror: The Liber Manualis and a century of scholarship." Magistra. A journal of women's spirituality in history 4.2 (Winter 1998): 89-134. 
