2 CONT
2 CONT Richard, Duke of Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT By his wife Adelaide (married 888), daughter of Conrad II, Count of Auxerre, and Ermentrude of Alsace, he had several sons and daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Rudolph, successor and later King of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh the Black, later Duke of Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT Ermengard, married Gilbert, Duke of Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT Willa, married firstly Hugh, Count of Vienne, and secondly Boso, Count of Arles
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide, married Reginar II, Count of Hainaut
2 CONT
2 CONT Richilda, married Litaud I, Count of Mâcon
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Bouchard, Constance B. "The Bosonids or Rising to Power in the Late Carolingian Age." French Historical Studies, Vol. 15, No. 3. (Spring, 1988), pp 407–431.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Charles the Fat in 888, Richard supported the claim of Duke Rudolph to be King of Upper Burgundy and married his sister Adelaide, daughter of Conrad II of Auxerre. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT By his wife Adelaide (married 888), daughter of Conrad II, Count of Auxerre, and Ermentrude of Alsace, he had several sons and daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Rudolph, successor and later King of France
2 CONT
2 CONT * Hugh the Black, later Duke of Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT * Ermengard, married Gilbert, Duke of Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT * Adelaide, married Reginar II, Count of Hainaut
2 CONT
2 CONT * Richilda, married Litaud I, Count of Mâcon
2 CONT
