1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''GEOFFROY (-[15 Feb/21 Jul] 1060) [I] Marquis et Comte de Provence'''
2 CONT s/o GUILLAUME [III] de Provence ([986/87]-1018 <30 May) & GERBERGE de Mâcon ([985]-[1020/23])    
2 CONT x (< 27 Jan 1040) ETIENNETTE [Douce] (->28 Jul 1094, maybe >11 Sep 1095)
2 CONT * BERTRAND [II] (-[29 Apr 1090/28 Jul 1094]) x  (<Feb 1061) MATHILDE
2 CONT * NN x (1066, repudiated <1080) RAYMOND de Toulouse
2 CONT *?ETIENNETTE (-[1085]) x (>054) GUILLEM [II] "Trunus" Comte de Besalú i Ripoll
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/PROVENCE.htm#GuillaumeIIIProvencedied1018B
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT GUILLAUME [III] de Provence, son of GUILLAUME [II] Comte de Provence et d'Arles & his second wife Adelais [Blanche] d´Anjou ([986/87]-1018 before 30 May, bur Abbaye de Montmajour)...m ([1002]) GERBERGE de Mâcon, daughter of OTHON 
2 CONC GUILLAUME Comte de Mâcon [Bourgogne-Comté] & his first wife Ermentrude de Roucy ([985]-[1020/23]).  
2 CONT Comte Guillaume [III] & his wife had three children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''3.         GEOFFROY (-[15 Feb/21 Jul] 1060).  "Geiriberga comitissima et filii mei Vuilelmus et Fulco sive Josfredus" donated property to Saint-Victor de Marseille by charter dated 1018[296], which suggests that Geoffroy was he
2 CONC r third son.  This appears confirmed by "Wilelmus filius Vilelmi comitis, Fulco frater eius, Gosfredus" subscribing the charter dated 1018 under which "Adalax comitissa mater Villelmi quondam Provintie comitis et Geriberga eque co
2 CONC mitissa…eiusdem principis olim uxor" donated property to Saint-Victor de Marseille for the soul of their late son and husband respectively[297], and by the charter dated 1019 under which "Geriberga comitissa" donated property to S
2 CONC aint-Victor de Marseille for the soul of "senioris mei Guilelmi comitis Provincie" and for "filiorumque nostrorum…Wilelmo, Fulcho, Jozfredus"[298].  "Gisberga comitissa…cum filiis meis Willelmo, Fulcone Bertranno, Gaufredo" donate
2 CONC d property to Saint-André-lès-Avignon by charter dated 1019[299].  His ancestry is confirmed by the charter dated 1057 under which "Gausfredus marchyo sive comes Provincie et uxor mea Stefania et filius meus Bertrannus" donated pr
2 CONC operty to Saint-Victor de Marseille naming "avus meus Wuilelmus marchio sive comes Provincie et avia mea Adalax comitissa"[300].  "Duo fratres, comites Provincie, Gausfredus atque Bertrannus" made a joint donation to Cluny dated 2
2 CONC 6 May 1037[301].  GEOFFROY [I] Marquis et Comte de Provence.  "Gauzfredus marchio sive comes Provincie" consented to the donation by "Guillelmus vicecomes Massiliensis…" to Saint-Victor de Marseille by charter dated 1045[302].  "G
2 CONC osfredus et Bertrannus frater meus, utrique Provinciales marchiones sive comites" donated property to Saint-Victor, Marseille by charter dated 1048[303].  “Godfredus comes et uxor mea Stephania et filius meus Bertrannus” donated p
2 CONC roperty by charter dated 15 Feb 1060[304].  m (before 27 Jan 1040) [as her first husband,] ETIENNETTE [Douce], daughter of --- (-after 28 Jul 1094, maybe after 11 Sep 1095).  "Gaufredus comes" donated property to Monmajour by char
2 CONC ter dated 1040, signed by "Bertrannus comes…Stephania comitissa"[305].  The date of her marriage is set by the charter dated 27 Jan 1040 under which "Josfredus comes et uxor mea" confirmed a donation of property to Saint-Victor de
2 CONC  Marseille, although it is not clear from the document that the confirmation (which appears after the text which recites the original grant) was contemporaneous with the donation[306].  Raimbaud Archbishop of Arles transferred pro
2 CONC perty in the presence of "Jauffredi comitis et Stephanie uxor sue" by charter dated Mar 1048[307].  "Godfredus comes Provincie et uxor mea Stephania et filius meus Bertrannus" donated property to Saint-Victor de Marseille by chart
2 CONC er dated 1 Jul 1055[308].  "Gausfredus marchyo sive comes Provincie et uxor mea Stefania et filius meus Bertrannus" donated property to Saint-Victor de Marseille by charter dated 1057[309].  “Godfredus comes et uxor mea Stephania 
2 CONC et filius meus Bertrannus” donated property by charter dated 15 Feb 1060[310].  A charter dated 28 Jul 1094 records that "Raimundus...comes et Provincie marchio" and “Dulcis comitissa” both donated tolls on the Durance and Rhône r
2 CONC ivers to Saint-Victor de Marseille “atque domno Ricardo”, confirming each other’s donations[311].  A charter dated 11 Sep 1095 confirmed a donation to Saint-Victor de Marseille by “Stephania comitissa cognomento Dulcis” for the so
2 CONC ul of “filii sui Bertranni comitis”[312].  The wording of this document is silent on whether the donor was deceased at the time.  Szabolcs de Vajay indicates that the two documents dated 1094 and 1095 are the only ones which speci
2 CONC fy her alternative name “Douce” and suggests that Etiennette could have adopted the name in old age, maybe on taking the veil[313].  Szabolcs de Vajay suggests that the wife of Geoffroy [I] Comte de Provence was Etiennette [Douce]
2 CONC  de Marseille, daughter of Guillaume [II] Vicomte de Marseille & his second wife Etiennette ---[314].  He relies on the numerous common connections between the Provence and Marseille families in the charters of Marseille Saint-Vic
2 CONC tor, which indicate the likelihood of a family connection, although he points to no specific document which supports his argument.  He also highlights that the supposed mother of Etiennette [Douce] was referred to in sources as “S
2 CONC tephana”, in contrast to the diminutive “Stephanetta” used by her supposed daughter, which he says is consistent with a mother/daughter relationship.  However, as can be seen from the extracts quoted in this section, Etiennette [D
2 CONC ouce] was not consistently referred to in charters by the diminutive form of her name.  "Bertrannus comes et mater mea Stephania…[et] conjux mea Matildis" donated property to Monmajour by charter dated to Feb [1061/62][315].  [Sza
2 CONC bolcs de Vajay suggests that Etiennette married secondly ([1062/63]) as his second wife, Bernard [II] Comte de Bigorre[316].  He bases this suggestion on the charter dated 1 Apr 1080 under which "Centullus comes et uxor mea Beatri
2 CONC x et mater eius Stephania" donated the monastery of Saint-Savin de Lavedan "in comitatu Bigorritano" to Marseille Saint-Victor “et abbati Ricardo”[317], pointing out that the Bigorre family made no earlier donations to Marseille S
2 CONC aint-Victor.  He also points out that this second marriage would explain the absence of Etiennette [Douce] from any charters in Provence for more than 30 years after the death of Comte Geoffroy [I].  It should be noted that Etienn
2 CONC ette, widow of Bermard [II] Comte de Bigorre, was absent from the similar donation made to Marseille Saint-Victor by her daughter and son-in-law dated 12 May 1087[318].  This could indicate that she was deceased by then or (as sug
2 CONC gested by Szabolcs de Vajay) that she had returned to Provence.  Szabolcs de Vajay’s argument for this second marriage certainly has some appeal, but in the absence of other indications cannot be taken as conclusive.]  Comte Geoff
2 CONC roy [I] & his wife had [three] children: 
2 CONT '''
2 CONT >a)         BERTRAND [II] (-[29 Apr 1090/28 Jul 1094]).  His parentage is confirmed by the charter dated 1057 under which "Gausfredus marchyo sive comes Provincie et uxor mea Stefania et filius meus Bertrannus" donated property to
2 CONC  Saint-Victor de Marseille naming "avus meus Wuilelmus marchio sive comes Provincie et avia mea Adalax comitissa"[319].  “Godfredus comes et uxor mea Stephania et filius meus Bertrannus” donated property by charter dated 15 Feb 10
2 CONC 60[320].  Comte de Provence.  A charter dated 21 Jul 1060 records that "B. comes Provincie" was received into the fraternity of Marseille Saint-Victor, just as his parents had been ("sicut et parentes eius antea fuerant")[321].  T
2 CONC he editor of the cartulary of Marseille Saint-Victor doubted the accuracy of the date of this charter.  However, it is consistent with the last mention of Bertrand’s father on 15 Feb 1060 (assuming that date was N.S.) and, if corr
2 CONC ect, may help narrow the estimated date when his father died as shown above.  "Bertrannus comes et mater mea Stephania…[et] conjux mea Matildis" donated property to Monmajour by charter dated to Feb [1061/62][322].  Marquis de Pro
2 CONC vence.  Bernard Comte et Marquis de Provence renounced allegiance to the empire by act dated 1081 and swore fealty to the papacy[323].  The testament of Armengol [IV] Comte de Urgell dated 29 Apr 1090 named “Bertran Conde de Arles
2 CONC ” among the guardians of his infant son[324].  After his death the marquisate of Provence passed to Raymond IV Comte de Toulouse, as shown by the bull of Pope Urban II dated 22 Jul 1096 which confirmed the rights of the monastery 
2 CONC of Saint-André near Avignon after its abandonment by "comes Nimirum Tholosanorum ac Ruthenensium et marchio Provintie Raimundus"[325], but the precise process by which this occurred has not yet been ascertained.  m (before Feb 106
2 CONC 1) MATHILDE, daughter of ---.  "Bertrannus comes et mater mea Stephania…[et] conjux mea Matildis" donated property to Monmajour by charter dated to Feb [1061/62][326].  Comte Bertrand [II] & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>i)          CECILE (-1150).  
2 CONT
2 CONT >b)         [--- de Provence.  Her parentage and marriage are shown in Europäische Stammtafeln but the primary source on which this is based has not yet been identified.  It is likely that the connection is speculative, presumably
2 CONC  in an effort to explain how Raymond Comte de Toulouse later claimed the marquisate of Provence.  However, Raymond´s right could have been hereditary through his paternal grandmother, who was the sister of Guillaume [V] Comte et M
2 CONC arquis de Provence (see above).  The fact of Raymond´s first marriage is confirmed by the charter dated to [1066] under which "Raimundum comitem de Rutenis, filium Almodis" and "Guifredum archiepiscopum de Narbona, filium Guille c
2 CONC omitissæ" confirmed an agreement, which was confirmed by "uxorem suam comitissam"[333].   The Histoire Générale de Languedoc suggests that she was Raymond´s first cousin, the daughter of his paternal uncle Bertrand, suggesting tha
2 CONC t Raymond naming his first son Bertrand would then have been consistent with the contemporary convention of using the name of one of the child´s grandfathers for the first-born son[334].   It is assumed that the marriage was termi
2 CONC nated, maybe for consanguinity, which could explain the doubts expressed in some primary sources about the legitimacy of Raymond´s son Bertrand.  m (1066, repudiated before 1080) as his first wife, RAYMOND de Toulouse Comte de Sai
2 CONC nt-Gilles, son of PONS Comte de Toulouse & his third wife Almodis de La Marche (-castle of Mount Pèlerin near Tripoli, Palestine 28 Feb 1105).  He succeeded his brother in 1094 as RAYMOND IV Comte de Toulouse.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT >c)         [ETIENNETTE (-[1085]).  Szabolcs de Vajay suggested that the wife of Guillem [II] Comte de Besalú was the daughter of Comte Geoffroy [I][335].  Edouard de Saint-Phalle highlighted in 2004 that the theory was never supp
2 CONC orted by any documentary evidence[336].  Her marriage (but not her parentage) is confirmed by the charter dated to [1084] under which her son "Bernardus Besaldunensis comes filius qui fui Stephaniæ" swore homage to "Aymerico vicec
2 CONC omiti Narbonæ filio qui fuisti Fidis"[337].  m (after 1054) GUILLEM [II] "Trunus" Comte de Besalú i Ripoll, son of GUILLEM [I] Comte de Besalú & his wife Adelaide --- (-murdered [1066/70]).] 
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/PROVENCE.htm#GuillaumeIIIProvencedied1018B
