0 @N6000000002187918239@ NOTE Ferdinand II of Castile
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand II González (930–970) was the first independent count of Castile, son of Gonzalo Fernández de Lara, who had been named count of Arlanza and the Duero around the year 900, a descendent of Nuño Rasura, one of the two judge
1 CONC s from Castile, and of Rodrigo, the first of the counts from Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand González was a colourful character of legendary status in Iberia and a member of the influential Lara family. In the year 930, Ferdinand's name appears with the title of count inside the administrative organization of ea
1 CONC stern the Kingdom of León.
1 CONT
1 CONT He grew up in the castle of Lara and inherited his father's title after the capture and death of his brother, Nuño Fernández.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 931, Ferdinand gathered under his control a strong military force composed of troops from the counties of Burgos, Asturias, Santillana, Lantaron, Álava, Castile, and Lara. His military prowess came to prominence in the Battle o
1 CONC f Simancas in 939 and then at Sepulveda, where he wrested the region from the Moors and repopulated it. As his power increased, so did his independence from León. During this period he married Sancha, the sister of the king of Nav
1 CONC arre, García III.
1 CONT
1 CONT After having fought with Ramiro II of León against the Arabs, and after the Battle of Simancas and the retreat of the Muslims, Ferdinand was dissatisfied because the king of León distributed his troops in the frontier towns and he
1 CONC  rose in rebellion against him. He was, however, defeated and made prisoner in 944, which lasted for 3 years until he became reconciled with his sovereign, giving his daughter Urraca in marriage to the king's son, Ordoño, who afte
1 CONC rwards became King Ordoño III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Notwithstanding this alliance, Ferdinand continued to foment trouble and discord in León, aiming to secure his independence. He successively aided Sancho I against his brother Ordoño III, and Ordoño IV, son of Alfonso IV, against 
1 CONC Sancho himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon the death of Ramiro II of Leon in 951, the kingdom of León experienced a dynastic crisis that Ferdinand played out to his advantage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Initially he supported the demands of Sancho I against his brother Ordoño III, but when he failed, he was forced to recognize Ordoño as king. Ordoño III's early death allowed Ferdinand to recover his maneuvering capacity, although
1 CONC  in this occasion he didn't support his old ally, Sancho's, pretenses, but rather allied himself with the son of Alfonso IV, Ordoño IV. Defeated in 960 through Navarrese intervention, he was captured by King García of Navarre, but
1 CONC  he recovered his freedom after making various territorial concessions. With the kingdom of León weakened and in disorder, Ferdinand slowly assured his position as legitimate independent count of Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his death the county was left to his son García Fernáandez. His remains were buried in the monastery of San Pedro of Arlanza.
1 CONT
1 CONT His life and feats are recorded in an anonymous poem, The Poem of Fernán González, written between 1250 and 1271 and conserved as an incomplete copy from the fifteenth century.
1 CONT Wikimedia Commons has media related to:
1 CONT Fernán González
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ferdinand Aznárez count of Castile
1 CONT 923–970 Succeeded by:
1 CONT García I Fernáandez
0 @N6000000002188182495@ NOTE Fernán González of Castile
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand González (930–970) was the first independent count of Castile, son of Gonzalo Fernández de Lara, who had been named count of Arlanza and the Duero around the year 900, a descendant of Nuño Rasura, one of the two judges f
1 CONC rom Castile, and perhaps of Rodrigo, the first of the counts from Castile. His mother Muniadona Ramírez was so well remebered that the later Counts of Castile would sometimes be recorded by Iberian Muslim scholars as Ibn Mama Duna
1 CONC  (descendant of Muniadona).
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand González was a colourful character of legendary status in Iberia and a member of the influential Lara family. In the year 930, Ferdinand's name appears with the title of count inside the administrative organization of ea
1 CONC stern the Kingdom of León.
1 CONT
1 CONT He grew up in the castle of Lara and inherited his father's title after the capture and death of his uncle, Nuño Fernández.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 931, Ferdinand gathered under his control a strong military force composed of troops from the counties of Burgos, Asturias, Santillana, Lantaron, Álava, Castile, and Lara. His military prowess came to prominence in the Battle o
1 CONC f Simancas in 939 and then at Sepulveda, where he wrested the region from the Moors and repopulated it. As his power increased, so did his independence from León. During this period he married Sancha, the sister of the king of Nav
1 CONC arre, García Sánchez I. Sancha was a daughter of Sancho I of Pamplona, and Toda of Navarre.
1 CONT
1 CONT After having fought with Ramiro II of León against the Arabs, and after the Battle of Simancas and the retreat of the Muslims, Ferdinand was dissatisfied because the king of León distributed his troops in the frontier towns and he
1 CONC  rose in rebellion against him. He was, however, defeated and made prisoner in 944, which lasted for 3 years until he became reconciled with his sovereign, giving his daughter Urraca in marriage to the king's son, Ordoño, who afte
1 CONC rwards became King Ordoño III.
1 CONT Sepulchre in Covarrubias, the lid is from the nineteenth century and the casket from the fifth.
1 CONT Sepulchre in Covarrubias, the lid is from the nineteenth century and the casket from the fifth.
1 CONT Statue in Madrid (J. Villanueva, 1750-53).
1 CONT Statue in Madrid (J. Villanueva, 1750-53).
1 CONT
1 CONT Notwithstanding this alliance, Ferdinand continued to foment trouble and discord in León, aiming to secure his independence. He successively aided Sancho I against his brother Ordoño III, and Ordoño IV, son of Alfonso IV, against 
1 CONC Sancho himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon the death of Ramiro II of Leon in 951, the kingdom of León experienced a dynastic crisis that Ferdinand played out to his advantage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Initially he supported the demands of Sancho I against his brother Ordoño III, but when he failed, he was forced to recognize Ordoño as king. Ordoño III's early death allowed Ferdinand to recover his maneuvering capacity, although
1 CONC  in this occasion he didn't support his old ally, Sancho's, pretenses, but rather allied himself with the son of Alfonso IV, Ordoño IV. Defeated in 960 through Navarrese intervention, he was captured by King García of Navarre, but
1 CONC  he recovered his freedom after making various territorial concessions. With the kingdom of León weakened and in disorder, Ferdinand slowly assured his position as legitimate independent count of Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his death the county was left to his son García Fernández. His remains were buried in the monastery of San Pedro of Arlanza.
1 CONT
1 CONT His life and feats are recorded in an anonymous poem, The Poem of Fernán González, written between 1250 and 1271 and conserved as an incomplete copy from the fifteenth century.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Ferdinand Ansúrez count of Castile
1 CONT 923–970 Succeeded by
1 CONT García Fernández
