0 @N6000000000337167872@ NOTE (Research):Clotaire I Clotaire I Pronounced As: klotâr , d. 561, Frankish king, son of Clovis I. On his father's death (511) he and his brothers received equal shares of the Frankish kingdom. His capital was at Soissons. In 524 he
1 CONC  and his brother Childebert I divided the kingdom of their deceased brother Clodomir, whose children they murdered. With his brother Theodoric he conquered Thuringia. In 534 Clotaire and Childebert seized and divided the First Kin
1 CONC gdom of Burgundy, and in 542 they attacked the Visigoths of Spain but were repulsed before Zaragoza. The deaths of Theodebald, Theodoric's grandson (555), and of Childebert (558) made Clotaire sole king of the Franks. His sons Chi
1 CONC lperic I and Sigebert I inherited Neustria and Austrasia respectively; his sons Charibert and Guntram divided the remainder of the kingdom.  Chlotar I Encyclopædia Britannica Article    born c. 500? died , late 561, Compiègne, Fr.
1 CONC    Merovingian king of Soissons from 511 and of the whole Frankish kingdom from 558, who played an important part in the extension of Frankish hegemony.   The youngest of Clovis I's sons, Chlotar shared in the partition of his fat
1 CONC her's kingdom in 511, receiving the old heartlands of the Salian Franks in modern northern France and Belgium. After the death of his brother, Clodomir, in 524, he murdered his nephews and shared the kingdom of Orléans with his tw
1 CONC o remaining brothers, Childebert I and Theodoric I. The deaths without heirs of the latter's grandson, Theodebald, in 555 and of Childebert in 558 brought all the Frankish lands finally under Chlotar's sway.   Chlotar's principal 
1 CONC campaigns were against the Burgundians in 523 (with Childebert and Clodomir) and 532-534 (when he and Childebert finally imposed Frankish rule), against the Visigoths in 532 (with Theodoric) and 542 (with Childebert), and against 
1 CONC the Thuringians in c. 531 (with Theodoric); he married the Thuringian princess, Radegunda. Chlotar was ruthless and brutal, and the family rivalries and sometimes open hostilities in which he figured prominently were characteristi
1 CONC c of Merovingian history; in 560 he even had his rebellious son, Chram, together with Chram's family, put to death. Gregory of Tours describes Chlotar, wracked with fever on his deathbed, asking_whether in outrage or in admiration
1 CONC _what manner of heavenly king it was who would bring great rulers to their deaths in such a fashion.
0 @N6000000000437062487@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> I, King Of The Franks "The Old" ", d. 561, Frankish king, son of Clovis I. On his father’s death (511) he and his brothers received equalshares of the Frankish kingdom. His capital was at Soissons. In 524 he and
1 CONC  hisbrother Childebert I divided the kingdom of their deceased brother Clodomir, whose children they murdered. With his brother Theodoric he conquered Thuringia.In 534 Clotaire and Childebert seized and divided the First Kingdom o
1 CONC f Burgundy, and in 542 they attacked the Visigoths of Spain but were repulsed before Zaragoza. The deaths of Theodebald, Theodoric’s grandson (555), and of Childebert (558) made Clotaire sole king of the Franks. His sons Chilperic
1 CONC  I and Sigebert Iinherited Neustria and Austrasia respectively; his sons Charibert and Guntramdivided the remainder of the kingdom." _________________________ Nickname:<NICK> The Old 1  NAME Clotair I "The Old" /Meroving/ 1  BIRT 
1 CONC 2  DATE 499 2PLAC Rheims, Marne, Loire-Atlantique, France 1  DEAT 2  DATE 561 1  DEAT 2DATE 23 NOV 561 2  PLAC Braines, Loire-Atlantique, France
1 CONT
