1 CONT
1 CONT Chlotar I King of the Franks 1
1 CONT Born: Abt 500 1
1 CONT Marriage (1): Ingund on an unknown date 1
1 CONT Marriage (2): Aregund on an unknown date
1 CONT Died: 561, Compiègne, France about age 61 1
1 CONT
1 CONT General Notes:
1 CONT
1 CONT Other names for Chlotar were Chloderic, Chlothar and Clotaire I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Merovingian king of Soissons from 511 and of the whole Frankish kingdom from 558, who played an important part in the extension of Frankish hegemony.
1 CONT
1 CONT The youngest of Clovis I's sons, Chlotar shared in the partition of his father's kingdom in 511, receiving the old heartlands of the Salian Franks in modern northern France and Belgium. After the death of his brother, Clodomir, in
1 CONC  524, he murdered his nephews and shared the kingdom of Orléans with his two remaining brothers, Childebert I and Theodoric I. The deaths without heirs of the latter's grandson, Theodebald, in 555 and of Childebert in 558 brought 
1 CONC all the Frankish lands finally under Chlotar's sway.
1 CONT
1 CONT Chlotar's principal campaigns were against the Burgundians in 523 (with Childebert and Clodomir) and 532-534 (when he and Childebert finally imposed Frankish rule), against the Visigoths in 532 (with Theodoric) and 542 (with Child
1 CONC ebert), and against the Thuringians in c. 531 (with Theodoric); he married the Thuringian princess, Radegunda. Chlotar was ruthless and brutal, and the family rivalries and sometimes open hostilities in which he figured prominentl
1 CONC y were characteristic of Merovingian history; in 560 he even had his rebellious son, Chram, together with Chram's family, put to death. Gregory of Tours describes Chlotar, wracked with fever on his deathbed, asking--whether in out
1 CONC rage or in admiration--what manner of heavenly king it was who would bring great rulers to their deaths in such a fashion.
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Clovis I, King of the Franks, he inherited Soissons on his death in 511. He, with his four brothers, attacked and defeated Burgundy under the kings Sigisbert and Godomar early in his reign. With his oldest brother Theuderic
1 CONC  I, King of Metz, he attacked the Thuringian Franks under King Hermanfrid, took the kingdom, and took his daughter Radegund. Next, with his brother Childebert I, King of Paris, Chlotar murdered his nephews who were under the care 
1 CONC of Queen Clotilda his mother. When Theuderic died, the kingdom was up for grabs. Chlotar and Childebert each received only a small part, the most of it going to Theudebert, his son. In 555, Theudebald, who had succeeded his father
1 CONC  Theudebert in Austrasia, died, and Austrasia passed to Chlotar. When Childebert died in 558, Paris fell to Chlotar as well, thus making him sole ruler of the Franks. When Chlotar died in 561, the kingdom was divided among his 4 l
1 CONC iving sons: Charibert (Paris), Guntram (Burgundy), Chilperic (Soissons), and Sigebert (Metz).
1 CONT
1 CONT Acceded: 511. Merovingian king of Soissons
1 CONT He was King of Austrasia 555 - 558
1 CONT Acceded: 558. King of the Franks
1 CONT
1 CONT [source: Ketil Ken Nygaard's Genealogy, http://nygaard.howards.net/]
1 CONT
1 CONT Chlotar married Ingund on an unknown date.1 (Ingund was born about 499 in Thuringia 1.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Chlotar next married Aregund on an unknown date. (Aregund was born about 500-510 and was buried in 580-590 1.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT 1 Nygaard, Ken, "Ketil Ken Nygaard's Genealogy" (http://nygaard.howards.net/).
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0 @N6000000002187572278@ NOTE Chlothar I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT The division of Gaul upon Chlothar's death (561).Chlothar I (or Chlothachar, Chlotar, Clothar, Clotaire, Chlotochar, or Hlothar, giving rise to Lothair; 497 – 561), called the Old (le Vieux), King of the Franks, was one of the fou
1 CONC r sons of Clovis. He was born about 497 in Soissons (now in Aisne département, Picardie, France).
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of his father in 511, he received, as his share of the kingdom, the town of Soissons, which he made his capital; the cities of Laon, Noyon, Cambrai, and Maastricht; and the lower course of the Meuse River. But he was 
1 CONC very ambitious, and sought to extend his domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the chief instigator of the murder of his brother Chlodomer's children in 524, and his share of the spoils consisted of the cities of Tours and Poitiers. He took part in various expeditions against Burgundy and, after the d
1 CONC estruction of that kingdom in 534, obtained Grenoble, Die, and some of the neighbouring cities.
1 CONT
1 CONT When the Ostrogoths ceded Provence to the Franks, he received the cities of Orange, Carpentras, and Gap. In 531, he marched against the Thuringii with his nephew Theudebert I and in 542, with his brother Childebert I against the V
1 CONC isigoths of Spain. On the death of his great-nephew Theodebald in 555, Clotaire annexed his territories. On Childebert's death in 558 he became sole king of the Franks.
1 CONT
1 CONT He also ruled over the greater part of Germany, made expeditions into Saxony, and for some time exacted from the Saxons an annual tribute of 500 cows. The end of his reign was troubled by internal dissensions, his son Chram rising
1 CONC  against him on several occasions. Following Chram into Brittany, where the rebel had taken refuge, Clotaire shut him up with his wife and children in a cottage, which he set on fire. Overwhelmed with remorse, he went to Tours to 
1 CONC implore forgiveness at the tomb of St Martin, and died shortly afterwards.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Clotaire's first marriage was to Guntheuc, widow of his own brother Chlodomer, sometime around 524. They had no children.
1 CONT
1 CONT His second marriage, which occurred around 532, was to Radegund, daughter of Bertachar, King of Thuringia, whom he and his brother Theuderic defeated. She was later canonized. They had no children.
1 CONT
1 CONT His third and most successful marriage was to Ingund, by whom he had five sons and two daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Gunthar, predeceased father 
1 CONT Childeric, predeceased father 
1 CONT Charibert, King of Paris 
1 CONT Guntram, King of Burgundy 
1 CONT Sigebert, King of Austrasia 
1 CONT Chlothsind, married Alboin, King of the Lombards 
1 CONT Blitildis, married Senator Ansbertus of Rome, Duke of Moselle 
1 CONT His next marriage was to a sister of Ingund, Aregund, with whom he had a son:
1 CONT
1 CONT Chilperic, King of Soissons 
1 CONT His last wife was Chunsina (or Chunsine), with whom he had one son:
1 CONT
1 CONT Chram, who became his father's enemy and predeceased him 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT Franks (main history of Frankish kingdoms) 
1 CONT List of Frankish Kings 
1 CONT Merovingians 
1 CONT History of France 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
