0 @N6000000003495155396@ NOTE
1 CONT Clotaire I (also Chlothar or Chloderic, sometimes called le Vieux or the Old) (497–561), a king of the Franks, was one of the four sons of Clovis. He was born about 497 in Soissons (now in Aisne département, Picardie, France).
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of his father in 511 he received as his share of the kingdom the town of Soissons, which he made his capital, the cities of Laon, Noyon, Cambrai, and Maastricht, and the lower course of the Meuse River. But he was ver
1 CONC y ambitious, and sought to extend his domain.
1 CONT He was the chief instigator of the murder of his brother Chlodomer's children in 524, and his share of the spoils consisted of the cities of Tours and Poitiers. He took part in the various expeditions against Burgundy, and after t
1 CONC he destruction of that kingdom in 534 obtained Grenoble, Die, and some of the neighbouring cities.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Provence was ceded to the Franks by the Ostrogoths, he received the cities of Orange, Carpentras, and Gap. In 531, he marched against the Thuringii with his brother Theuderic I and in 542, with his brother Childebert I agains
1 CONC t the Visigoths of Spain. On the death of his great-nephew Theodebald in 555, Clotaire annexed his territories; and on Childebert's death in 558 he became sole king of the Franks.
1 CONT
1 CONT He also ruled over the greater part of Germany, made expeditions into Saxony, and for some time exacted from the Saxons an annual tribute of 500 cows. The end of his reign was troubled by internal dissensions, his son Chram rising
1 CONC  against him on several occasions. Following Chram into Brittany, where the rebel had taken refuge, Clotaire shut him up with his wife and children in a cottage, to which he set fire. Overwhelmed with remorse, he went to Tours to 
1 CONC implore forgiveness at the tomb of St Martin, and died shortly afterwards.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Family
1 CONT Clotaire's first marriage was to Guntheuc, widow of his own brother Chlodomer, sometime around 524. They had no children.
1 CONT His second marriage occurred around 532, when he and his brother Theuderic defeated Bertachar, king of Thuringia, and Clotaire took his daughter Radegund. She was later canonised. They had no children.
1 CONT His third and most successful marriage was to Ingund, by whom he had five sons and a daughter:
1 CONT • Gunthar, predeceased father
1 CONT • Childeric, predeceased father
1 CONT • Charibert, king of Paris
1 CONT • Guntram, king of Burgundy
1 CONT • Sigebert, king of Austrasia
1 CONT • Chlothsind, married Alboin, king of the Lombards
1 CONT His next marriage was to a sister of Ingund, Aregund, with whom he had one son:
1 CONT • Chilperic, king of Soissons
1 CONT His last wife was Chunsina (or Chunsine), with whom he had one son:
1 CONT • Chram, became his father's enemy and predeceased him
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT • This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
0 @N6000000003495155366@ NOTE
1 CONT Clotaire I, d. 561, Frankish king, son of Clovis I. On his father's death (511) he and his brothers received equal shares of the Frankish kingdom. His capital was at Soissons. In 524 he and his brother Childebert I divided the kin
1 CONC gdom of their deceased brother Clodomir, whose children they murdered. With his brother Theodoric he conquered Thuringia. In 534 Clotaire and Childebert seized and divided the First Kingdom of Burgundy, and in 542 they attacked th
1 CONC e Visigoths of Spain but were repulsed before Zaragoza. The deaths of Theodebald, Theodoric's grandson (555), and of Childebert (558) made Clotaire sole king of the Franks. His sons Chilperic I and Sigebert I inherited Neustria an
1 CONC d Austrasia respectively; his sons Charibert and Guntram divided the remainder of the kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Clotaire I (also Chlothar or Chloderic, sometimes called le Vieux or the Old) (497–561), a king of the Franks, was one of the four sons of Clovis. He was born about 497 in Soissons (now in Aisne département, Picardie, France).
