1 CONC  against him on several occasions. Following Chram into Brittany, where the rebel had taken refuge, Clotaire shut him up with his wife and children in a cottage, to which he set fire. Overwhelmed with remorse, he went to Tours to 
1 CONC implore forgiveness at the tomb of St Martin, and died shortly afterwards.
1 CONT
1 CONT Family
1 CONT Clotaire's first marriage was to Guntheuc, widow of his own brother Chlodomer, sometime around 524. They had no children.
1 CONT His second marriage occurred around 532, when he and his brother Theuderic defeated Bertachar, king of Thuringia, and Clotaire took his daughter Radegund. She was later canonised. They had no children.
1 CONT His third and most successful marriage was to Ingund, by whom he had five sons and a daughter:
1 CONT • Gunthar, predeceased father
1 CONT • Childeric, predeceased father
1 CONT • Charibert, king of Paris
