0 @N6000000002187700825@ NOTE Adalbert, Duke of Alsace
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Adalbert (died 723) was the Duke of Alsace from sometime after 683 and probably before 700 until his death. He was the second duke of the family of the Etichonids and the first to inherit the duchy from his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of Adalrich and Berswinda, Adalbert was created Count of the Sundgau by his father circa 683. It is unknown if Adalbert appointed another count to succeed him after taking over the ducal office, exercised the comital power
1 CONC s himself, or left the office vacant. Under Adalbert, Etichonid control of the offices of the duchy of Alsace and of the monasteries of the region became entrenched.
1 CONT
1 CONT Adalbert seems to have concentrated his power in northern Alsace (the later Nordgau) around the Diocese of Strasbourg. He founded the convent of Saint Stephen at Strasbourg and installed his daughter Attala as its first abbess. In
1 CONC  722 he established a monastery in honour of the Saint Michael the Archangel at Honau on an island in the Rhine north of Strasbourg. This last establishment was co-founded by a group of monks from Ireland led by the first abbot, B
1 CONC enedict. Honau passed to King Theuderic IV on Adalbert's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Adalbert's first wife was Gerlinda (perhaps of Aquitaine); his second wife was Ingina, a wealthy woman of Alsace, and she was the mother of his children. was succeeded by his eldest son, Liutfrid, who in turn made Adalbert's secon
1 CONC d son, Eberhard, a count as early as the 720s. His daughters Eugenia and Gundlinda both entered the nunnery of their aunt Odilia at Hohenburg, where Eugenia eventually succeeded as abbess. Gundlinda was later abbess of Niedermünst
1 CONC er. In 845 the Emperor Lothair I confirmed all the charters which Adalbert had granted to his foundation at Strasbourg.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Hummer, Hans J. Politics and Power in Early Medieval Europe: Alsace and the Frankish Realm 600 – 1000. Cambridge University Press: 2005. See mainly pp 46–55.
0 @N6000000002248190965@ NOTE He is brother of St. Odile, patron saint of Alsace (d 12-0720) said to be born blind and cast out for this reason by her family, adopted by a convent whereshe miraculously recovered her sight-  eventually becoming abess and foundr
1 CONC ess of Hohenburg and of Niedemunster (both under the Benedictine rule).
