0 @N6000000005599087096@ NOTE Afther the death of Sancho IV, King of Navarre, and brother of Ramiroo r Ramirez, Count of Calahorra, Navarre was seized by Sancho Ramieriz, son of Rameriz of Aragon, and he was succeeded by two of his sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT (ref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garcia_VI_of_Navarre) García VI Ram írez (died 21 November 1150, Lorca), called the Restorer (Spanish: elR estaurador), was lord of Monzón, and in 1134 became King of Navarre.H e was born in the 
1 CONC 1110s, the great-grandson of García V of Navarre. Hi s father, Ramiro Sánchez, lord of Monzón, was son of García V's illegi timate son Sancho Garces (distinct from his legitimate half-brother,Ki ng Sancho IV). His mother was Crist
1 CONC ina Rodríguez Díaz de Vivar, daught er of Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar, better known as El Cid Campeador, the rec ent lord of Valencia.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Aragón, which had from 1076 been united to Navarre, lost its warr ior king Alfonso the Battler and fell into a succession crisis (1134) , García managed to wrest Navarre from his likewise illegitimate Arago nese cousins. He w
1 CONC as elected in Pamplona by the bishops and nobles oft he realm against the will of Alfonso. However, by agreement between hi m and Ramiro II, the new king of Aragón, he was deemed the "son" andRa miro the "father" in an attempt to 
1 CONC maintain both the independence of e ach kingdom and the supremacy of the Aragonese one.  García was oblige d to surrender Rioja to Castile in 1136, and to declare himself a vass al of Alfonso VII of Castile. His kingdom was separa
1 CONC ted from Aragón co mpletely when Alfonso entered Zaragoza and rendered the Aragonesehis v assals. In 1137, he allied with Alfonso I of Portugal and confronted A lfonso VII. They confirmed a peace between 1139 and 1140.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1144, he married Urraca, the illegitimate daughter of the king of A lphonso VII of Castile. He was thereafter an ally of Castile in the Re conquista and present at the conquest of Almería in 1147. The marriag e also made him a 
1 CONC brother-in-law of Raymond Berengar IV, count of Barc elona, with whom he confirmed a peace treaty in 1149. The count was pr omised to García's daughter Blanca while already engaged to Petronill a of Aragón, but García died before 
1 CONC the marriage could be carried out.
1 CONT
1 CONT García was utterly incompetent and at various times was dependent upo n the revenues of churches and convents. He died on 12 November 1150 i n Lorca, near Estella, and was succeeded by his more competent son, Sa ncho VI the Wise. 
1 CONC  Sometime after 1130, he married Marguerite de l'Aig le. On 24 June 1144, in León, he married Urraca, illegitimate daughte r of Alfonso VII of Castile and Guntroda. His children were, by Margue rite: 1.) Sancho, called "the Wise,"
1 CONC  king of Navarre, married Sancha ( Beatriz), daughter of Alfonso VII of Castile and Berenguela of Barcelo na; 2.) Blanca (born after 1133), married Sancho III of Castile; 3.) M argaret, married William I of Sicily; 4.) Sancha; 5.)
1 CONC  A boy, Rodrigo,l ater count of Montescaglioso in Southern Italy, was born to queen Marg uerite during the marriage, but he was widely considered not to be aso n of king García and the king never recognised him.  Garcias is  somet
1 CONC  imes numbered García IV because he was the fourth García of his dynast y (the Jiménez).
