0 @N6000000002187750329@ NOTE Ramon Berenguer III, Count of Barcelona
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer III the Great was the count of Barcelona, Girona, and Osona from 1082 (jointly with Berenguer Ramon II and solely from 1097), Besalú from 1111, Cerdagne from 1117, and Provence, in the Holy Roman Empire, from 1112,
1 CONC  all until his death in Barcelona in 1131
1 CONT
1 CONT Born in 1082 in Rodez, he was the son of Ramon Berenguer II. He succeeded his father to co-rule with his uncle Berenguer Ramon II. He became the sole ruler in 1097, when Berenguer Ramon II was forced into exile,
1 CONT
1 CONT During his rule Catalan interests were extended on both sides of the Pyrenees. By marriage or vassalage he incorporated into his realm almost all of the Catalan counties (except those of Urgell and Peralada). He inherited the coun
1 CONC ties of Besalú (1111) and Cerdagne (1117) and in between married Douce, heiress of Provence (1112). His dominions then stretched as far east as Nice.
1 CONT
1 CONT In alliance with the Count of Urgell, Ramon Berenguer conquered Barbastro and Balaguer. In 1118 he captured and rebuilt Tarragona, which became the metropolitan seat of the church in Catalonia (before that, Catalans had depended e
1 CONC cclesiastically on the archbishopric of Narbonne). He also established relations with the Italian maritime republics of Pisa and Genoa and in 1114 and 1115 raided with them the Moorish pirate strongholds of Majorca and Ibiza. They
1 CONC  became his tributaries and many Christian slaves there were recovered and set free. Ramon Berenguer also raided mainland Muslim dependencies with Pisa's help, such as Valencia, Lleida and Tortosa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Toward the end of his life Ramon Berenguer became a Templar. He gave his five Catalonian counties to his eldest son Ramon Berenguer IV and Provence to the younger son Berenguer Ramon.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer's marriages and descendants
1 CONT First wife, María Rodríguez de Vivar, second daughter of Cid, died ca. 1105 
1 CONT María -> married Bernat III, Count of Besalú (d. 1111) 
1 CONT Jimena, a.k.a. Eixemena -> married Roger III, Count of Foix 
1 CONT Second wife, Almodis 
1 CONT Third wife, Douce or Dolça de Gévaudaun, heiress of Provence, d. ca. 11
1 CONT Almodis -> married Ponce de Cervera 
1 CONT Berenguela or Berengaria, b. 1108, d. 1149 -> married Alfonso VII of Castile 
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona, b. 1115, d. 1162 
1 CONT Berenguer Ramon I, Count of Provence, b. ca. 1115, d. 1144 
1 CONT Bernat -> died young 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer II and
1 CONT Berenguer Ramon II Count of Barcelona
1 CONT with Berenguer Ramon II Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer IV 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Douce Count of Provence Succeeded by:
1 CONT Berenguer Ramon I 
