0 @N6000000002240782536@ NOTE On the death of his father, the entire Frankish realm went to brother
1 CONT Dagobert in 629, but he was ceded several territories with a capital at
1 CONT Toulouse, a kingdom called Aquitaine. He and his infant son were assassinated
1 CONT the following year and the kingdom reverted back to brother Dagobert.
0 @N6000000002244390351@ NOTE On the death of his father, the entire Frankish realm went to brother
1 CONT Dagobert in 629, but he was ceded several territories with a capital at
1 CONT Toulouse, a kingdom called Aquitaine. He and his infant son were assassinated
1 CONT the following year and the kingdom reverted back to brother Dagobert.
0 @N6000000003949533309@ NOTE Charibert II (c.608–8 April 632), a son of Clotaire II and his second wife Sichilde, was briefly king of Aquitaine from 629 to his death, with his capital at Toulouse.
1 CONT When his father, Clotaire II, King of the Franks, died in 629, Charibert made a bid for the kingdom of Neustria against his elder half-brother Dagobert I, who had already been king of Austrasia since 623. In the ensuing negotiatio
1 CONC ns, Charibert, a minor, was represented by his uncle Brodulf, the brother of Queen Sichilde. Dagobert had Brodulf killed and ceded the near-independent realm of Aquitaine to Charibert. This agreement was confirmed in 631, when Cha
1 CONC ribert stood godfather to Dagobert's son Sigebert.
1 CONT Charibert's realm included Toulouse, Cahors, Agen, Perigueux, and Saintes, to which he added his possessions in Gascony. Charibert was married to Gisela, the heiress of Amand of Gascony. His fighting force subdued the resistance o
1 CONC f the Basques, until the whole of the Basque Country was under his control.
1 CONT In 632, Charibert died at Blaye, Gironde—possibly assassinated on Dagobert's orders—and soon after that Charibert's infant son Chilperic was also killed. Aquitaine passed again to Dagobert. Both Charibert and his son are buried in
1 CONC  the early Romanesque Basilica of Saint-Romain at Blaye.
