0 @N6000000001503791171@ NOTE Weis' "Ancestral Roots" (190:1) calls him Frankish King of Cologne,
1 CONT
1 CONT living 420, kinsman of Clovis I.
1 CONT
1 CONT kennwalrus@aol.com (Kennwalrus) posted to
1 CONT
1 CONT GEN-MEDIEVAL-L-request@rootsweb.com on 17 Feb 1998
1 CONT
1 CONT Subject: Re: CLOVIS THE RIPARIAN
1 CONT
1 CONT ..."Clovis the Riparian, supposedly King at Koln ca. 420, can be traced
1 CONT
1 CONT back, in English, to a 1940s article by G. Andrews Moriarty in the New
1 CONT
1 CONT England Historical and Genealogical Register. (My papers are in disarray,
1 CONT
1 CONT so I speak -- this is a warning -- from memory.) He, however, was
1 CONT
1 CONT summarizing his understanding of a series of 1920s articles by a scholar
1 CONT
1 CONT named Depoin, published in a French periodical called the Revue Mabillon.
1 CONT
1 CONT What Depoin actually theorized in this article, on the basis of much later
1 CONT
1 CONT and rather confused saints' lives, was that Sigebert the Lame of Cologne
1 CONT
1 CONT (real, fl. prob. ca. 500, give or take a bit) was younger brother of his
1 CONT
1 CONT predecessor, Childebert, and that both were sons and successors of a
1 CONT
1 CONT Clovis who was not the same man as Clovis the Great of the Salic Franks.
1 CONT
1 CONT Moriarty apparently read or remembered this incorrectly, for he made
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigebert the Lame SON of Childebert, and GRANDSON of this 'other
1 CONT
1 CONT Clovis.' Having done so, he guessed fl.' dates for the supposed three
1 CONT
1 CONT generations, at 30 years each.
1 CONT
1 CONT ....Actually, the saint's life in question is a late and shaky document upon
1 CONT
1 CONT which to found a genealogy; while it's barely possible that the genealogy
1 CONT
1 CONT as Depoin extracted it is valid (Moriarty's would be less so, since its
1 CONT
1 CONT alteration from Depoin's version is apparently accidental), nothing even
1 CONT
1 CONT remotely contemporary documents the Riparian kings back of Sigebert of
1 CONT
1 CONT Cologne. Assuming that there were any, it seems likely that they were
1 CONT
1 CONT in some degree akin to CLOWIS THE GREAT, but impossible to specify
1 CONT
1 CONT how. (This is not for want of trying, often of an original and inventive
1 CONT
1 CONT cast, by many hands over many decades; but the original documentation
1 CONT
1 CONT being so very thin, they are unlikely to approximate the historical truth,
1 CONT
1 CONT whatever it was.)"
1 CONT
1 CONT The next day, Kennwalrus, further expanded on his comments:
1 CONT
1 CONT ...". . . fortunately, as I've located the relevant articles. You're right: in
1 CONT
1 CONT *NEHGR* 98 (1944) 303-10, "The Origin of the Carolingians," at 309, G.
1 CONT
1 CONT A. Moriarty writes: "Siegbert the Lame ... was the son of Childebert, King
1 CONT
1 CONT of Cologne, and ... grandson of an earlier Clovis, ... King of Cologne early
1 CONT
1 CONT in the fifth century (Gregory of Tours, *op. cit.*)." There's nothing in
1 CONT
1 CONT Gregory about Siegbert's (or Sigebert's) ancestry, other than the general
1 CONT
1 CONT statement, made somewhere in G of T's *History of the Franks*, that the
1 CONT
1 CONT other Frankish kings (ca. 490? 500?) were (all?) relatives of Clovis the
1 CONT
1 CONT Great, who killed them. Moriarty apparently wrote doubly absent-
1 CONT
1 CONT mindedly at that point, for it isn't just the citation, but the proposed
1 CONT
1 CONT ancestry itself, that's reported incorrectly. Earlier in the same article
1 CONT
1 CONT he cites his source for the WHOLE lineage as M. J. Depoin, "Les Grands
1 CONT
1 CONT [*sic*] Figures Monacales ...," *Revue Mabillon*, 1921-2. The latter
1 CONT
1 CONT article says, "Il s'agit ici ["ici" being the *Legend of St. Goar*] de trois
1 CONT
1 CONT rois de Cologne dont le dernier, Sigebert le Boiteux, apparait comme
1 CONT
1 CONT successeur d'un Childebert anterieur au fils de Clotilde, et fils d'un
1 CONT
1 CONT Clovis qui etablit son pouvoir sur les Ripuaires probablement au temps
1 CONT
1 CONT de l'exil de Childeric Ier." In another article by the same man -- "Etudes
1 CONT
1 CONT merovingiennes" -- for which I can identify neither the date nor the
1 CONT
1 CONT journal (I took bad notes) -- unless it's the *Revue des Etudes
1 CONT
1 CONT Historiques*, 11, pp. 369-85 -- he says, "Le royaume des Franks
1 CONT
1 CONT austrasiens de 450 a 509 a donc eu pour titulaires:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1. Clodovie (Clovis), frere de Merovee.
1 CONT
1 CONT 2. Childibert (Childebert), fils de Clodovie.
1 CONT
1 CONT 3. Sigebert, frere cadet de Childibert.
1 CONT
1 CONT 4. Cloderic, fils de Sigebert."
1 CONT
1 CONT In other words, Depoin analyzed a later medieval legend as meaning that
1 CONT
1 CONT a Frank named Clovis took power at Cologne in the 460s or so; and that a
1 CONT
1 CONT Childebert and a Sigebert ("the Lame") were his sons and successors, the
1 CONT
1 CONT latter being father of Cloderic the Parricide. Whether one accepts this
1 CONT
1 CONT depends on (a) one's assessment of the value of the *Legend of St. Goar*
1 CONT
1 CONT as a source for the fifth century (a subject on which I remain agnostic
1 CONT
1 CONT here), (b) Depoin's analysis of *Goar*; and (c) other sources for the
1 CONT
1 CONT period, which name no Ripuarian kings (they name precious few
1 CONT
1 CONT anybodys, as it was a dreadful period for annalists and historians, as for
1 CONT
1 CONT everybody else), but do seem to indicate that there WERE 'other' Frankish
1 CONT
1 CONT kings -- other than Childeric, fa. of Clovis, that is -- in the middle 400s.
1 CONT
1 CONT Long story to short: Sigebert the Lame's real. He definitely doesn't
1 CONT
1 CONT descend from Clovis the Great. (He was almost certainly older than C
1 CONT
1 CONT the G.) Plausibly, though not provably, he was of royal Frankish descent,
1 CONT
1 CONT and kin, in some unknown degree, to C the G; but the evidence for the
1 CONT
1 CONT existence of Clovis the Ripuarian is debatable, which makes Depoin's
1 CONT
1 CONT analysis of this C's genealogical bearings of secondary relevance; and
1 CONT
1 CONT Moriarty's report of Depoin's analysis is incorrect -- either misread, or
1 CONT
1 CONT (I think more likely) misremembered in the writing. (Which would
1 CONT
1 CONT indicate that Moriarty, at least sometimes, wrote from an unreliable
1 CONT
1 CONT memory, without his sources beside him, and without correcting from
1 CONT
1 CONT those sources later.)"
1 CONT http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~jast/D0005/G0000009.html#I2508
