0 @N6000000002187700852@ NOTE Sigebert III
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigebert III (630?-660) was a Frankish King of Austrasia who began his rule in around 635 and ended it with his death in approximately February 660. He was the eldest son of Dagobert I.
1 CONT
1 CONT To satisfy the Austrasian aristocracy, who exercised a certain autonomy, Sigebert's father gave him the kingdom of Austrasia although it remained part of the larger Frankish realm. On the death of Dagobert, Sigebert ruled Austrasi
1 CONC a independently, and free from any subjection to Neustria.
1 CONT
1 CONT He tried in vain to add Thuringia to his kingdom, but was defeated by Duke Radulph in 640. It was under his reign that the mayor of the palace began to play the most important role in the political life of Austrasia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Grimoald, the son of Pepin I, managed to convince the king to adopt his son Childebert. When Sigebert finally had a son of his own, the future Dagobert II, the mayor of the palace felt threatened, and on the death of Sigebert he e
1 CONC xiled the young Dagobert to Ireland. Sigebert's remains, defiled during the French Revolution, are preserved in the cathedral at Nancy.
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT More from Wikipedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigebert III
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigebert III (c.630-656/660) was the king of Austrasia from 634 to his death on probably 1 February in 656, or maybe as late as 660. He was the eldest son of Dagobert I.
1 CONT
1 CONT To satisfy the Austrasian aristocracy, who exercised a certain autonomy, Sigebert's father gave him the kingdom of Austrasia although it remained part of the larger Frankish realm. On the death of Dagobert, Sigebert ruled Austrasi
1 CONC a independently, and free from any subjection to Neustria.
1 CONT
1 CONT He tried in vain to add Thuringia to his kingdom, but was defeated by Duke Radulph in 640. Though only ten years of age, he was the leader of his army. The Chronicle of Fredegar records that the route left him weeping in his saddl
1 CONC e. From this, we can surmise that, at least in part, the downfall of the Merovingian dynasty was a result of child rule, for both Sigebert and his younger brother Clovis II, who ruled in Neustria, were prepubescent children who co
1 CONC uld not fight on the field and whose regents had their own interests at heart.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was under his reign that the mayor of the palace began to play the most important role in the political life of Austrasia. That mayor, Grimoald, the son of Pepin I, managed to convince the king to adopt his son Childebert. When
1 CONC  Sigebert finally had a son of his own, the future Dagobert II, the mayor of the palace felt threatened, and on the death of Sigebert he exiled the young Dagobert to Ireland. Sigebert's remains, defiled during the French Revolutio
1 CONC n, are preserved in the cathedral at Nancy.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is notable that he is often described as the first roi fainéant—do-nothing king—of the Merovingian dynasty.
