0 @N6000000003495155236@ NOTE
1 CONT Clovis II (also Chlodowech or Chlodwig, modern French "Louis", modern German "Ludwig") (637-November 27, 655 or 658) succeeded his father Dagobert I in 639 as king of Neustria and Burgundy. His brother Sigebert III had been king o
1 CONC f Austrasia since 634. He was initially under the regency of his mother Nanthild until her untimely death in her early thirties in 642. This death allowed him to fall under the influence of the secular magnates, who reduced the ro
1 CONC yal power in their own favour.
1 CONT
1 CONT His wife, Balthild was an Anglo-Saxon aristocrat sold into slavery in France. She bore him three sons who all became kings after his death. The eldest, Clotaire, succeeded him and his second eldest, Childeric, was eventually place
1 CONC d on the Austrasian throne by Ebroin. The youngest, Theuderic, succeeded Childeric in Neustria and eventually became the sole king of the Franks.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a minor for almost the whole of his reign. He is sometimes regarded as king of Austrasia during the interval 656–657 when Childebert the Adopted had usurped the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT --
1 CONT born c. 634
1 CONT died , October or November 657
1 CONT
1 CONT Merovingian Frankish king of Neustria and Burgundy from 639, the son of Dagobert I. He was dominated successively by Aega and by Erchinoald, Neustrian mayors of the palace.
1 CONT
1 CONT MLA style:
1 CONT "Clovis II." Encyclopædia Britannica. 2006. Encyclopædia Britannica Premium Service. 13 May 2006 <http://wwwa.britannica.com/eb/article-9024459>.
0 @N6000000003635701386@ NOTE [495] "Bloodlines...", p 226, rgnd 633-656
