0 @N6000000003098227629@ NOTE Charlemagne, or "Charles the Great, Carolingian King" of the Franks, came to rule over most of Europe and assumed (800) the title of Roman Emperor. He is sometimes regarded as the founder of the Holy Roman Empire. Charlemagne was 
1 CONC probably born in 742 at Aachen. In 768 he and his brother Carloman inherited the Frankish kingdom (most of present-day France and a part of western Germany) from their father Pepin "The Shore". The entire kingdom passed to Charlem
1 CONC agne when Carloman died in 771.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charlemagne inherited great wealth and a strong military organization from his father and brother. He used these assets to double the territory under Carolingian control. In 772 he opened his offensive against the Saxons, and for 
1 CONC more than three decades he pursued a ruthless policy aimed at subjugating them and converting them to Christianity. Almost every year Charlemagne attacked one or another region of Saxon territory. Mass executions-- 4,500 Saxons we
1 CONC re executed on a single day in 782--and deportations were used to discourage the stubborn. The Saxons proved to be a far more difficult enemy than any of the other peoples subjugated by Charlemagne. For example, the Lombards were 
1 CONC conquered in a single extended campaign (773-74), after which Charlemagne assumed the title "King of the Lombards." In 788 he absorbed the duchy of . Bavaria, and soon thereafter he launched an offensive against the Avar empire. T
1 CONC he Avars succumbed within a decade, yielding Charlemagne a vast hoard of gold and silver. After one disastrous campaign (778) against the Muslims in Spain, Charlemagne left the southwestern front to his son Louis.
1 CONT
1 CONT The latter (later Emperor Louis I), with the help of local Christian rulers, conquered Barcelona in 801 and controlled much of Catalonia by 814. On Christmas Day, 800, Charlemagne accepted the title of emperor and was crowned by P
1 CONC ope LEO III. For several years after he regarded the imperial title of being of little value. Moreover, he intended to divide his lands and titles among his sons, as was the Frankish custom. At his death on Jan. 28, 814, however, 
1 CONC only one son, Louis, survived; Louis therefore assumed control of the entire Frankish empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT The internal organization of Charlemagne's empire varied from region to region. In much of what is today France, and especially in the south, the old Roman civitates (fortified cities) served as the focus of most important aspects
1 CONC  of political, military, religious, and social organization. Both the count of the city, appointed by Charlemagne as his representative, and the bishop made their respective headquarters in the civitas. The count or his agent led 
1 CONC the local army, and the walls of the civitates afforded protection for the inhabitants both of the city and the nearby countryside. In those parts of the empire that had not been part of the Roman world, Charlemagne made an effort
1 CONC  to impose a similar system. He divided newly conquered lands into pagi (districts), which were placed under the jurisdiction of counts who exercised the same kind of administrative powers of their counterparts to the west. Charle
1 CONC magne also sought to establish these new pagi as dioceses. In frontier areas, Charlemagne often established districts that were essentially military in their purpose and organization; these were called marks or marches. Local cust
1 CONC oms were everywhere perpetuated by recognition of traditional laws. The laws, some unwritten, of each of the various peoples of the Carolingian empire, such as Salian Franks, Ripuarian Franks, Romans, Saxons, Lombards, Bavarians, 
1 CONC Thuringians, and Jews, were codified and/or modified if local codes already existed, they were recognized.
1 CONT
1 CONT This judicial autonomy enjoyed by the several peoples of the empire indicates the diversity that not only existed but also flourished under Charlemagne. The emperor did, however, legislate to provide a system by which these variou
1 CONC s peoples could interact with each other. The central administration of the empire, like the local administrations, was rudimentary. A palatine court followed Charlemagne on his numerous campaigns; during the later years of his li
1 CONC fe, when he remained at AACHEN, the court stayed there. Charlemagne also sent missi dominici, high-ranking agents of the central government, from the court to see that his orders, often cast in the form of capitularies (ordinances
1 CONC  divided into capitula, or chapters), were enforced. As part of his administrative efforts, Charlemagne sought to standardize weights, measures, and coinage. He also made an attempt to control and develop trade. To these ends he s
1 CONC trongly encouraged the development of Jewish communities.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charlemagne's concern for administration and his interest in seeing the church function effectively led him to encourage a rudimentary educational system based in monasteries. Thus a small group of clerical and lay administrators 
1 CONC attained a useful level of literacy. Charlemagne left the development and implementation of this system largely to Alcuin. The latter's work led to what some scholars have called the Carolingian Renaissance. At Charlemagne's court
1 CONC  a group of scholars was gathered that included men from England, Spain, and Italy, as well as native Franks and probably Jews.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charlemagne has been credited with great political and humanitarian vision and a devout religious bent; as a result, some have been led to think of his military ventures as crusades. In fact, he was a gluttonous and superstitious 
1 CONC illiterate, or semiliterate, who had a considerable capacity for brutality. His accomplishments were due mostly to the energy with which he pursued his military goals and the ruthlessness with which he treated any opponents. Nonet
1 CONC heless, his achievements were considerable, and the effect of his conquests was to spread Roman Christianity across central Europe.
1 CONT
1 CONT Einhard, Charlemagne's biographer, describes him as 6'3.5", short neck, with a slight paunch in his later years. He was strong and towered over other men. He believed in moderation, disliked drunkards, and fasting. Feasting was fi
1 CONC ne in moderation. A natural person, he disliked fuss and chastised his men for hunting in silks instead of leathers. He genuinely liked people and ideas. Charlemagne's empire was inherited by his only surviving son, Louis the Piou
1 CONC s. His grandsons split the territory between them. Louis the German's territory will be the basis of modern Germany. Charles the Bald inherited what will become France. The strip between them which included Italy was inherited by 
1 CONC Lothar. He died first and the French and Germans have fought over control of his section which included Alsace Lorraine into the twentieth century. Many towns in this area including Aachen(Aix la Chapelle) have two names, one Fren
1 CONC ch and one German.
0 @N6000000001351726628@ NOTE Established the system of reckoning in pounds, shillings and pence. He will also be remembered as the white-haired old king in the Song of Roland; but he was neither an economist nor the rather feckless character of the Song, bein
1 CONC g rather one of the ideal examples in European history of the man of action, a type that always spells danger.  In 768 Charlemagne and his brother Carloman succeeded to the joint rule of the Franks, but three years later Carloman 
1 CONC died, and Charlemagne ruled supreme. He was as active as his father in defending and expanding his territories. In 773, when the Lombards were again putting pressure on the Pope, he crossed the Alps with astonishing speed and defe
1 CONC ated the Lombards absolutely, putting their king in a monastery (now a family habit) and assuming the 'Iron' Crown of Lombardy himself. He now began a systematic campaign to conquer the Saxons, and ten years of the most bitter fig
1 CONC hting ensued. The Saxons discovered an able leader in Widukind, and in 782, managed to wipe out a substantial army of Franks. Charlemagne had 4,500 Saxons beheaded at Verden in retribution, and went on to celebrate 'The Nativity o
1 CONC f Our Lord and Easter as he was wont to do,' says Einhard, his biographer. It took nearly three years to find Widukind, and he was then baptized---a clear declaration of submission; the rest of the Saxons gave little trouble in ta
1 CONC king baptism, or obeying their new Frankish masters---they remembered Verden. A feudal vassal of Charlemagne who should have learned a lesson from this was Duke Tassilo of Bavaria, but he preferred to behave as if he were independ
1 CONC ent of his overlord. Charlemagne gave him one chance to reform, but then found that he was plotting with his enemies, so in 788 he too was put into a monastery, and Bavaria was incorporated into the fast growing empire. In Spain h
1 CONC e was not so successful: he had been forced to call off his invasion in 778, for his troops were needed elsewhere, and anyway the Muslims turned out to be not as disunited as he had been told; it was in this retreat that Roland di
1 CONC ed. But in 793 the Muslims attacked over his borders, so he set up an enclave on the southern side of the Pyrenees to guard the area. He now turned his attention to the Avars, relations of the Huns, who lived in the area of the mi
1 CONC ddle Danube, and were phenomenally rich with tribute-money they had wrung from the Byzantine Emperors. Peaceful negotiations had failed to keep them from raiding Charlemagne's lands, and so he set out to conquer them. It was as ha
1 CONC rd a war as that against the Saxons, lasting from 791-9, and Charlemagne was wise to distribute the loot he gained from it to his war-weary people instead of keeping it for himself. Since 476 there had been no Emperor in the West,
1 CONC  and until recently the Popes had looked to the Byzantine Emperors for protection. In 800 the Pope was set upon and deposed, and Charlemagne had to go to Rome to restore him. On Christmas Day of that year he was praying in St. Pet
1 CONC er's when the Pope came up and crowned him as Emperor, taking him 'unawares.' Historians wrangle over the coronation of Charlemagne, and the results of their searches read like detective stories. Suffice it to say that Charlemagne
1 CONC  must have known what was going to happen, but he was rather disturbed about the whole thing afterwards; possibly he was upset at not having the fiat of the Emperor of the East, though a woman was reigning there at the time, possi
1 CONC bly he felt the Pope had arrogated to himself too great a part in the coronation. Certainly he kept a very healthy respect for the Byzantine Empire, though he was not a man to fear another's power: he had good relations with Harou
1 CONC n-al-Rashid, the Caliph of Baghdad, who sent him a white elephant, and arranged protection for pilgrims visiting Jerusalem, in the heart of Muslim territory. In a less exciting area he developed good relations also with the variou
1 CONC s Anglo-Saxons states in England; and the first commercial treaty of which we have a record in English history is a letter from Charlemagne to Offa of Mercia, then the central Anglo-Saxon state, requesting more short cloaks, but n
1 CONC ot as short as the last batch, for when one was forced by the call of nature to get off one's horse, the cloak turned out to be a very draughty affair. Einhard's biography gives us a fine picture of Charlemagne in the prime of his
1 CONC  life: a large pleasant looking man, with rather a weak voice, who loved all forms of exercise, but excelled in swimming. He wore the ordinary dress of his nation, objecting strongly to having to dress in Roman fashion on the two 
1 CONC occasions Popes requested it to impress the citizens of Rome. He ate and drank moderately, but had a passion for roast meat. He loved to hear music and to listen to readings from St. Augustine's City of God; he also delighted in t
1 CONC he old songs of his nation, which his priggish son had destroyed after his death, because they were pagan. He plainly respected learning, and loved to be surrounded by learned people, but he probably didn't get very far in his own
1 CONC  learning; he used to keep a copy-book under his pillow (he suffered from insomnia) but he never really learned to write. His palace at Aachen was the Versailles of the ninth century, beautiful and impressive, though it is a typic
1 CONC ally homely touch that he settled on this site because the swimming was good there, with natural hot springs to warm the water. The pictorial arts flourished under him, especially in the decoration of books, which themselves were 
1 CONC written in the fine minuscule hand which was developed in his reign, and was to form the basis of the Renaissance italic hand. Schools were built up, modelled on the palace school, which was more of a university in that it served 
1 CONC as a place for distinguished scholars to work, and a training ground for the sons of the nobility. Alcuin was called from England, and Peter of Pisa came, along with the best minds of the age. Monasteries built up huge libraries, 
1 CONC and in their scriptoria multiple copies were made. By these means the riches of literature of the ancient world were preserved for the modern, and not even the destructive power of the Norsemen could entirely root out the achievem
1 CONC ent. Although the full effect of this educational revolution was not to be felt until after the death of Charlemagne, when the whole of Europe began to build great edifices of stone, and theologians and philosophers dared to reaso
1 CONC n, this was truly the Carolingian Renaissance, and owed a tremendous debt to the boundless vision and enthusiasm of Charlemagne himself. In fact, the cultural influences of the Carolingian state were to outlast by far the state it
1 CONC self. Having conquered territories, Charlemagne tended to do little but install Frankish counts there, introduce his elementary form of feudalism, and then occasionally add to the legal system such laws as were necessary. He sent 
1 CONC round groups of 'Missi Dominici' to check on the administration of the counts, and held formal assemblies each year, which provided an elementary check on what was happening all over the Empire; but it was only while his dominant 
1 CONC personality and military might were at the head of the system that it could work---the whole Empire was ready to spring apart into fragments when this was removed. It lacked the economic organisation necessary for unity, retaining
1 CONC  the spirit of self-sufficiency which was the hallmark of medieval regionalism. On his death in 814, his son Louis the Pious succeeded, but on his death in 840 civil war broke out between Louis' sons, and in 843 at Verdun the Empi
1 CONC re was divided between the three of them, one taking the western strip, one the eastern and the third taking a central strip right down from the Low Countries to half-way down Italy---Germany was to go a separate way from that of 
1 CONC France, the Low Countries and Burgundy were to aim at separate development, and all were to have interest in what became of the Italian domains. It is possible to place too much emphasis on the decisiveness of this treaty for the 
1 CONC future history of Western Europe, but even so one should remember that the year before it was made when the two leaders of West and East met to make the preliminary arrangements, the one swore his oath in French and the other in G
1 CONC erman so that their followers could understand them. The popular names for the rulers who followed in the wake of Charlemagne spell out for us the decline from greatness, Louis the Pious, Charles the Bald, Louis the Stammerer, Cha
1 CONC rles the Fat, Charles the Simple. Europe was to be divided, with disastrous results; but nonetheless people remembered the achievement of Charlemagne through the long terrible years of war and the terrible attacks from the Norseme
1 CONC n. They created the tradition of the Song of Roland, which was only outdone in popularity by the later re-workings of the predominantly national legends of the Germans and the Celtic lands. Perhaps it was not so bad that Arthur re
1 CONC placed Charlemagne in the end, for his like did not come to Europe again until the days of Napoleon. [Source: Who's Who in the Middle Ages, John Fines, Barnes & Noble Books, New York, 1995]. 
1 CONT ASC
1 CONT 812. This year died the Emperor Charlemagne, after a reign of five and forty winters; and Archbishop Wulfred, accompanied by Wigbert, Bishop of Wessex, undertook a journey to Rome. 
0 @N6000000000437049538@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Holy Roman Emperor, King Of The Franks
1 CONT !NAME: Charlemagne
1 CONT !TITLE: King of the Franks (768-814), Emperor of the West (800-814);"
1 CONT !BIRTH:2 Apr 742, at Ingelheim, Germany
1 CONT !MARRIAGE: ". . .(third) ca. 771, Hildegardeof Swabia,"
1 CONT !DEATH: ". . . 814/4 at Aix-la-Chapelle;" "The grandson of CharlesMartel and the son of Pepin the Short, he was the second Carolingian king of the Franks (768-814) and the first Christiann Emperor of the West (800-814).  One of hi
1 CONC story's great figures, he was destined to found a new Roman empire inthe West.  He regarded himself as the divine agent whose mission it was to revive the civilization, religion, and culture that had been lost in the Dark Ages. . 
1 CONC . . Under the last of the Merovingians the intellectual and cultural of Francia had almost been extinquished. Charlemagne's first concern was to re-educate the clergy. . . . he brought to France learned and renowned teachers froma
1 CONC broad, Alcuin from England, Paulus Diaconus from Italy.  With their help, he founded cathedral and monastic schools, and established the seven liberal arts as the general program of studies.  The result, not fully apparent until h
1 CONC is death, was a veritable renaissance in letters and arts."
1 CONT !REFERENCE-NOTE: ENCYLCOPEDIA AMERICANA - 1964, pp. 310, 311 Vol. 6. Library of Congress Number:64-12146.
0 @N6000000002322269097@ NOTE aka Charles the Great. Had two more mistresses:
1 CONT
1 CONT Adalind, son Theodric (807-818) a cleric.
1 CONT unknown, son Richbod (800-844) abbott of St-Riquier.
1 CONT King of the Lombards 774. Holy Roman Emperor.
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT CHARLEMAGNE (742?-814). "By the sword and the cross," Charlemagne
1 CONT (Charles the Great) became master of Western Europe. It was falling
1 CONT into decay when Charlemagne became joint king of the Franks in 768.
1 CONT Except in the monasteries, people had all but forgotten education and
1 CONT the arts. Boldly Charlemagne conquered barbarians and kings alike. By
1 CONT restoring the roots of learning and order, he preserved many political
1 CONT rights and revived culture.
1 CONT    Charlemagne's grandfather was Charles Martel, the warrior who
1 CONT crushed the Saracens (see Charles Martel). Charlemagne was the elder
1 CONT son of Bertrade ("Bertha Greatfoot") and Pepin the Short, first "mayor
1 CONT of the palace" to become king of the Franks. Although schools had
1 CONT almost disappeared in the 8th century, historians believe that Bertrade
1 CONT gave young Charles some education and that he learned to read. His
1 CONT devotion to the church motivated him throughout life.
1 CONT    Charlemagne was tall, powerful, and tireless. His secretary,
1 CONT Eginhard, wrote that Charlemagne had fair hair and a "face laughing and
1 CONT merry . . . his appearance was always stately and dignified." He had a
1 CONT ready wit, but could be stern. His tastes were simple and moderate. He
1 CONT delighted in hunting, riding, and swimming. He wore the Frankish dress
1 CONT linen shirt and breeches, a silk-fringed tunic, hose wrapped with
1 CONT bands, and, in winter, a tight coat of otter or marten skins. Over all
1 CONT these garments "he flung a blue cloak, and he always had a sword girt
1 CONT about him."
1 CONT    Charlemagne's character was contradictory. In an age when the usual
1 CONT penalty for defeat was death, Charlemagne several times spared the
1 CONT lives of his defeated foes; yet in 782 at Verden, after a Saxon
1 CONT uprising, he ordered 4,500 Saxons beheaded. He compelled the clergy and
1 CONT nobles to reform, but he divorced two of his four wives without any
1 CONT cause. He forced kings and princes to kneel at his feet, yet his mother
1 CONT and his two favorite wives often overruled him in his own household.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charlemagne Begins His Reign
1 CONT
1 CONT    In 768, when Charlemagne was 26, he and his brother Carloman
1 CONT inherited the kingdom of the Franks. In 771 Carloman died, and
1 CONT Charlemagne became sole ruler of the kingdom. At that time the northern
1 CONT half of Europe was still pagan and lawless. In the south, the Roman
1 CONT Catholic church was striving to assert its power against the Lombard
1 CONT kingdom in Italy. In Charlemagne's own realm, the Franks were falling
1 CONT back into barbarian ways, neglecting their education and religion.
1 CONT    Charlemagne was determined to strengthen his realm and to bring
1 CONT order to Europe. In 772 he launched a 30-year campaign that conquered
1 CONT and Christianized the powerful pagan Saxons in the north. He subdued
1 CONT the Avars, a huge Tatar tribe on the Danube. He compelled the
1 CONT rebellious Bavarian dukes to submit to him.
1 CONT    When possible he preferred to settle matters peacefully, however.
1 CONT For example, Charlemagne offered to pay the Lombard king Desiderius for
1 CONT return of lands to the pope, but, when Desiderius refused, Charlemagne
1 CONT seized his kingdom in 773 to 774 and restored the Papal States.
1 CONT    The key to Charlemagne's amazing conquests was his ability to
1 CONT organize. During his reign he sent out more than 50 military
1 CONT expeditions. He rode as commander at the head of at least half of them.
1 CONT He moved his armies over wide reaches of country with unbelievable
1 CONT speed, but every move was planned in advance. Before a campaign he told
1 CONT the counts, princes, and bishops throughout his realm how many men they
1 CONT should bring, what arms they were to carry, and even what to load in
1 CONT the supply wagons. These feats of organization and the swift marches
1 CONT later led Napoleon to study his tactics.
1 CONT    One of Charlemagne's minor campaigns has become the most famous. In
1 CONT 778 he led his army into Spain, where they laid siege to Saragossa.
1 CONT They failed to take the city, and during their retreat a group of
1 CONT Basques ambushed the rear guard at Roncesvalles and killed "Count
1 CONT Roland." Roland became a great hero of medieval songs and romances (see
1 CONT Roland).
1 CONT    By 800 Charlemagne was the undisputed ruler of Western Europe. His
1 CONT vast realm covered what are now France, Switzerland, Belgium, and the
1 CONT Netherlands. It included half of present-day Italy and Germany, part of
1 CONT Austria, and the Spanish March ("border"). The broad March reached to
1 CONT the Ebro River. By thus establishing a central government over Western
1 CONT Europe, Charlemagne restored much of the unity of the old Roman Empire
1 CONT and paved the way for the development of modern Europe.
1 CONT
1 CONT Crowned Emperor
1 CONT
1 CONT    On Christmas Day in 800, while Charlemagne knelt in prayer in St.
1 CONT Peter's in Rome, Pope Leo III seized a golden crown from the altar and
1 CONT placed it on the bowed head of the king. The throng in the church
1 CONT shouted, "To Charles the August, crowned by God, great and pacific
1 CONT emperor, long life and victory!"
1 CONT    Charlemagne is said to have been surprised by the coronation,
1 CONT declaring that he would not have come into the church had he known the
1 CONT pope's plan. However, some historians say the pope would not have dared
1 CONT to act without Charlemagne's knowledge.
1 CONT    The coronation was the foundation of the Holy Roman Empire. Though
1 CONT Charlemagne did not use the title, he is considered the first Holy
1 CONT Roman emperor (see Holy Roman Empire).
1 CONT
1 CONT Reform and Renaissance
1 CONT
1 CONT    Charlemagne had deep sympathy for the peasants and believed that
1 CONT government should be for the benefit of the governed. When he came to
1 CONT the throne, various local governors, called "counts," had become lax
1 CONT and oppressive. To reform them, he expanded the work of investigators,
1 CONT called missi dominici. He prescribed their duties in documents called
1 CONT capitularies and sent them out in teams of two a churchman and a noble.
1 CONT They rode to all parts of the realm, inspecting government,
1 CONT administering justice, and reawakening all citizens to their civil and
1 CONT religious duties.
1 CONT    Twice a year Charlemagne summoned the chief men of the empire to
1 CONT discuss its affairs. In all problems he was the final arbiter, even in
1 CONT church issues, and he largely unified church and state.
1 CONT    Charlemagne was a tireless reformer who tried to improve his
1 CONT people's lot in many ways. He set up money standards to encourage
1 CONT commerce, tried to build a Rhine-Danube canal, and urged better farming
1 CONT methods. He especially worked to spread education and Christianity in
1 CONT every class of people.
1 CONT    He revived the Palace School at Aachen, his capital. He set up other
1 CONT schools, opening them to peasant boys as well as nobles.
1 CONT    Charlemagne never stopped studying. He brought an English monk,
1 CONT Alcuin, and other scholars to his court. He learned to read Latin and
1 CONT some Greek but apparently did not master writing. At meals, instead of
1 CONT having jesters perform, he listened to men reading from learned works.
1 CONT    To revive church music, Charlemagne had monks sent from Rome to
1 CONT train his Frankish singers. To restore some appreciation of art, he
1 CONT brought valuable pieces from Italy. An impressive monument to his
1 CONT religious devotion is the cathedral at Aachen, which he built and where
1 CONT he was buried (see Aachen).
1 CONT    At Charlemagne's death in 814 only one of his three sons, Louis, was
1 CONT living. Louis's weak rule brought on the rise of civil wars and
1 CONT revolts. After his death his three quarreling sons split the empire
1 CONT between them by the Partition of Verdun in 843.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
