1 CONT Charles the Great, with an unbending will to power, a readiness to fight
1 CONT resolutely against external enemies, an ability to increase his domain through
1 CONT a strength of character rooted in an unbroken conviction of being at one with
1 CONT the divine will, did so unite in one superstate nearly all the Christian lands
1 CONT of western Europe. He combined a personal piety with enjoyment of life, a
1 CONT religious sense of mission with a strong will to power, rough manners with
1 CONT intellectual growth, and intransigence against his enemies with rectitude.
0 @N6000000002322291153@ NOTE He was co-king of Franks with elder brother Charlemagne in 754 and King of
1 CONT the East Franks from 768-771. Note about Charlemagne: Besides expanding the
1 CONT political power of his empire, Charles brought a cutural renaissance in his
1 CONT empire. Although this imperium survived its founder by one generation, the
1 CONT medevial kingdoms of France and Gemany derived all their constitutional
1 CONT traditions from Charles's monarchy. Throughout medieval Europe, the person of
1 CONT Charles I was considered the prototype of a Christian king and emperor.
0 @N6000000002187572541@ NOTE Charlemagne
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign King of the Franks: 24 September 768 – 28 January 814;
1 CONT King of the Lombards: 774 – 28 January 814;
1 CONT Emperor: 25 December 800 – 28 January 814 
1 CONT Coronation King of the Franks: c. June 754, St Denis;
1 CONT King of the Lombards: 774;
1 CONT Holy Roman Emperor: 25 December 800, Rome 
1 CONT Titles Patrician of the Romans 
1 CONT Born c.2 April 742/747 
1 CONT Died 28 January 814 
1 CONT  Palace of Aachen 
1 CONT Buried Palatine Chapel in Aachen 
1 CONT Predecessor Pippin the Short 
1 CONT Heirs Apparent Charles the Younger, Pippin of Italy, Louis the Pious 
1 CONT Successor Louis the Pious 
1 CONT Consort Desiderata of Lombardy,
1 CONT Hildegard,
1 CONT Fastrada,
1 CONT Luitgard 
1 CONT Issue Pippin the Hunchback (763-811),
1 CONT Charles the Younger (772 or 773-811),
1 CONT Carloman, renamed Pippin (773 or 777-810), King of Italy,
1 CONT Rotrude (or Hruodrud) (777-810),
1 CONT Louis the Pious (778-840), King of Aquitaine, later King and Emperor of the Franks,
1 CONT Bertha (779-823)
1 CONT Gisela (781-808)
1 CONT Theodrada (b.784), abbess of Argenteuil 
1 CONT Royal House Carolingian Dynasty 
1 CONT Father Pippin the Short 
1 CONT Mother Bertrada of Laon 
1 CONT
1 CONT Charlemagne (En: pronounced /'??rl?me?n/; Fr: pronounced [?a?l?'ma?]; Latin: Carolus Magnus, meaning Charles the Great) (742/747 – 28 January 814) was King of the Franks from 768 to his death. He expanded the Frankish kingdoms int
1 CONC o a Frankish Empire that incorporated much of Western and Central Europe. During his reign, he conquered Italy and was crowned Imperator Augustus by Pope Leo III on 25 December 800, in an attempted revival of the Roman Empire in t
1 CONC he West. His rule is also associated with the Carolingian Renaissance, a revival of art, religion, and culture through the medium of the Catholic Church. Through his foreign conquests and internal reforms, Charlemagne helped defin
1 CONC e both Western Europe and the Middle Ages.
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of King Pippin the Short and Bertrada of Laon, he succeeded his father and co-ruled with his brother Carloman I. The latter got on badly with Charlemagne, but war was prevented by the sudden death of Carloman in 771. Charl
1 CONC emagne continued the policy of his father towards the papacy and became its protector, removing the Lombards from power in Italy, and waging war on the Saracens, who menaced his realm from Spain. It was during one of these campaig
1 CONC ns that Charlemagne experienced the worst defeat of his life, at Roncesvalles (778). He also campaigned against the peoples to his east, especially the Saxons, and after a protracted war subjected them to his rule. By forcibly con
1 CONC verting them to Christianity, he integrated them into his realm and thus paved the way for the later Ottonian dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Today he is not only regarded as the founding father of both French and German monarchies, but as the father of Europe: his empire united most of Western Europe for the first time since the Romans, and the Carolingian renaissance 
1 CONC encouraged the formation of a common European identity.[1] Pierre Riché reflects:
1 CONT
1 CONT “ . . . he enjoyed an exceptional destiny, and by the length of his reign, by his conquests, legislation and legendary stature, he also profoundly marked the history of western Europe.[2] ” 
