0 @N6000000003495151548@ NOTE
1 CONT Long Bios at 
1 CONT http://www.chronique.com/Library/MedHistory/charlemagne.htm
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlemagne
1 CONT
1 CONT  He was so fond of his daughters that he dissuaded them from marriage, saying that he could not bear to be without them. They consoled themselves with unlicensed amours, and bore several illegitimate children. Charlemagne accepted
1 CONC  these accidents with good humor, since he himself, following the custom of his predecessors, had four successive wives and five mistresses or concubines. His abounding vitality made him extremely sensitive to feminine charms; and
1 CONC  his women preferred a share in him to the monopoly of any other man. His harem bore him some eighteen children, of whom eight were legitimate.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was six feet four inches tall, and built to scale.
1 CONT He had beautiful white hair, animated eyes, a powerful nose...
1 CONT a presence "always stately and dignified." He was temperate
1 CONT in eating and drinking, abominated drunkenness, and kept
1 CONT in good health despite every exposure and hardship.
1 CONT —Eginhardt (the King's secretary) describing Charlemagne
1 CONT http://www.lucidcafe.com/library/96apr/charlemagne.html
1 CONT
1 CONT Charlemagne (742 or 747 – 28 January 814) (also Charles the Great[1]; from Latin, Carolus Magnus or Karolus Magnus), son of , was the king of the Franks from 768 to 814 and king of the Lombards from 774 to 814. He was crowned Impe
1 CONC rator Augustus in Rome on Christmas Day, 800 by Pope Leo III and is therefore regarded as the founder of the Holy Roman Empire (as Charles I). Through military conquest and defence, he solidified and expanded his realm to cover mo
1 CONC st of Western Europe and is today regarded as the founding father of both France and Germany and sometimes as the Father of Europe. His was the first truly imperial power in the West since the Fall of the Roman Empire.
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlemagne
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and heirs
1 CONT His first wife was Himiltrude, married in 766. The marriage was never formally annulled. By her he had:
1 CONT • Pippin the Hunchback (767-813)
1 CONT His second wife was Gerperga (often erroneously called Desiderata or Desideria), daughter of Desiderius, king of the Lombards, married in 768, annulled in 771.
1 CONT His third wife was Hildegard of Savoy (757 or 758-783 or 784), married 771, died 784. By her he had:
1 CONT • Charles the Younger (772 or 773-811), king of Neustria from 781
1 CONT • Adelaide (773 or 774-774)
1 CONT • Carloman, baptised Pippin (773 or 777-810), king of Italy from 781
1 CONT • Rotrude (or Hruodrud) (777-810)
1 CONT • Louis (778-840), twin of Lothair, king of Aquitaine from 781 and emperor and king of the Franks from 814
1 CONT • Lothair (778-779 or 780), twin of Louis
1 CONT • Bertha (779-823)
1 CONT • Gisela (781-808)
1 CONT • Hildegarde (782-783)
1 CONT His fourth wife was Fastrada, married 784, died 794. By her he had:
1 CONT • Theodrada (b.784), abbess of Argenteuil
1 CONT • Hiltrude (b.787)
1 CONT His fifth and favourite wife was Luitgard, married 794, died 800 childless.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Concubinages and illegitimate children
1 CONT His first known concubine was Gersuinda. By her he had:
1 CONT • Adaltrude (b.774)
1 CONT His second known concubine was Madelgard. By her he had:
1 CONT • Ruodhaid (775-810), abbess of Faremoutiers
1 CONT His third known concubine was Amaltrud of Vienne. By her he had:
1 CONT • Alpaida (b.794)
1 CONT His fourth known concubine was Regina. By her he had:
1 CONT • Drogo (801-855), bishop of Metz from 823
1 CONT • Hugh (802-844), archchancellor of the Empire
1 CONT His fifth known concubine was Ethelind. By her he had:
1 CONT • Theodoric (b.807)
