1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT        Charlemagne
1 CONT
1 CONT        (French for Carolus Magnus, or Carlus Magnus ("Charles the
1 CONT        Great"); German Karl der Grosse). 
1 CONT
1 CONT        The name given by later generations to Charles, King of the Franks,
1 CONT        first sovereign of the Christian Empire of the West; born 2 April,
1 CONT        742; died at Aachen, 28 January, 814. Note, however, that the place
1 CONT        of his birth (whether Aachen or Liège) has never been fully
1 CONT        ascertained, while the traditional date has been set one or more years
1 CONT        later by recent writers; if Alcuin is to be interpreted literally the year
1 CONT        should be 745. At the time of Charles' birth, his father, Pepin the
1 CONT        Short, Mayor of the Palace, of the line of Arnulf, was, theoretically,
1 CONT        only the first subject of Childeric III, the last Merovinigian King of
1 CONT        the Franks; but this modest title implied that real power, military,
1 CONT        civil, and even ecclesiastical, of which Childeric's crown was only
1 CONT        the symbol. It is not certain that Bertrada (or Bertha), the mother of
1 CONT        Charlemagne, a daughter of Charibert, Count of Laon, was legally
1 CONT        married to Pepin until some years later than either 742 or 745. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        Charlemagne's career led to his acknowledgment by the Holy See as
1 CONT        its chief protector and coadjutor in temporals, by Constantinople as
1 CONT        at least Basileus of the West. This reign, which involved to a greater
1 CONT        degree than that of any other historical personage the organic
1 CONT        development, and still more, the consolidation of Christian Europe,
1 CONT        will be sketched in this article in the successive periods into which it
1 CONT        naturally divides. The period of Charlemagne was also an epoch of
1 CONT        reform for the Church in Gaul, and of foundation for the Church in
1 CONT        Germany, marked, moreover, by an efflorescence of learning which
1 CONT        fructified in the great Christian schools of the twelfth and later
1 CONT        centuries. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        To the Fall of Pavia (742-774) 
1 CONT
1 CONT        In 752, when Charles was a child of not more than ten years, Pepin
1 CONT        the Short had appealed to Pope Zachary to recognize his actual rule
1 CONT        with the kingly title and dignity. The practical effect of this appeal to
1 CONT        the Holy See was the journey of Stephen III across the Alps two
1 CONT        years later, for the purpose of anointing with the oil of kingship not
1 CONT        only Pepin, but also his son Charles and a younger son, Carloman.
1 CONT        The pope then laid upon the Christian Franks a precept, under the
1 CONT        gravest spiritual penalties, never "to choose their kings from any
1 CONT        other family". Primogeniture did not hold in the Frankish law of
1 CONT        succession; the monarchy was elective, though eligibility was
1 CONT        limited to the male members of the one privileged family. Thus,
1 CONT        then, at St. Denis on the Seine, in the Kingdom of Neustria, on the
1 CONT        28th of July, 754, the house of Arnulf was, by a solemn act of the
1 CONT        supreme pontiff established upon the throne until then nominally
1 CONT        occupied by the house of Merowig (Merovingians). 
1 CONT
1 CONT        Charles, anointed to the kingly office while yet a mere child, learned
1 CONT        the rudiments of war while still many years short of manhood,
1 CONT        accompanying his father in several campaigns. This early experience
1 CONT        is worth noting chiefly because it developed in the boy those
1 CONT        military virtues which, joined with his extraordinary physical
1 CONT        strength and intense nationalism, made him a popular hero of the
1 CONT        Franks long before he became their rightful ruler. At length, in
1 CONT        September, 768, Pepin the Short, foreseeing his end, made a
1 CONT        partition of his dominions between his two sons. Not many days
1 CONT        later the old king passed away. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        To better comprehend the effect of the act of partition under which
1 CONT        Charles and Carloman inherited their father's dominions, as well as
1 CONT        the whole subsequent history of Charles' reign, it is to be observed
1 CONT        that those dominions comprised: 
1 CONT
1 CONT           first, Frankland (Frankreich) proper; 
1 CONT           secondly, as many as seven more or less self-governing
1 CONT           dependencies, peopled by races of various origins and obeying
1 CONT           various codes of law. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        Of these two divisions, the former extended, roughly speaking, from
1 CONT        the boundaries of Thuringia, on the east, to what is now the Belgian
1 CONT        and Norman coastline, on the west; it bordered to the north on
1 CONT        Saxony, and included both banks of the Rhine from Cologne (the
1 CONT        ancient Colonia Agrippina) to the North Sea; its southern
1 CONT        neighbours were the Bavarians, the Alemanni, and the Burgundians.
1 CONT        The dependent states were: the fundamentally Gaulish Neustria
1 CONT        (including within its borders Paris), which was, nevertheless, well
1 CONT        leavened with a dominant Frankish element; to the southwest of
1 CONT        Neustria, Brittany, formerly Armorica, with a British and
1 CONT        Gallo-Roman population; to the south of Neustria the Duchy of
1 CONT        Aquitaine, lying, for the most part, between the Loire and the
1 CONT        Garonne, with a decidedly Gallo-Roman population; and east of
1 CONT        Aquitaine, along the valley of the Rhone, the Burgundians, a people
1 CONT        of much the same mixed origin as those of Aquitaine, though with a
1 CONT        large infusion of Teutonic blood. These States, with perhaps the
1 CONT        exception of Brittany, recognized the Theodosian Code as their law.
1 CONT        The German dependencies of the Frankish kingdom were Thuringia,
1 CONT        in the valley of the Main, Bavaria, and Alemannia (corresponding to
1 CONT        what was later known as Swabia). These last, at the time of Pepin's
1 CONT        death, had but recently been won to Christianity, mainly through the
1 CONT        preaching of St. Boniface. The share which fell to Charles consisted
1 CONT        of all Austrasia (the original Frankland), most of Neustria, and all
1 CONT        of Aquitaine except the southeast corner. In this way the possessions
1 CONT        of the elder brother surrounded the younger on two sides, but on the
1 CONT        other hand the distribution of mm under their respective rules was
1 CONT        such as to preclude any risk of discord arising out of the national
1 CONT        sentiments of their various subjects. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        In spite of this provident arrangement, Carloman contrived to
1 CONT        quarrel with his brother. Hunald, formerly Duke of Aquitaine,
1 CONT        vanquished by Pepin the Short, broke from the cloister, where he
1 CONT        had lived as a monk for twenty years, and stirred up a revolt in the
1 CONT        western part of the duchy. By Frankish custom Carloman should
1 CONT        have aided Charles; the younger brother himself held part of
1 CONT        Aquitaine; but he pretended that, as his dominion were unaffected by
1 CONT        this revolt, it was no business of his. Hunald, however, was
1 CONT        vanquished by Charles single-handed; he was betrayed by a nephew
1 CONT        with whom he had sought refuge, was sent to Rome to answer for
1 CONT        the violation of his monastic vows, and at last, after once more
1 CONT        breaking cloister, was stoned to death by the Lombards of Pavia. For
1 CONT        Charles the true importance of this Aquitanian episode was in its
1 CONT        manifestation his brother's unkindly feeling in his regard, and
1 CONT        against this danger he lost no time in taking precautions, chiefly by
1 CONT        winning over to himself the friends whom he judged likely to be
1 CONT        most valuable; first and foremost of these was his mother, Bertha,
1 CONT        who had striven both earnestly and prudently to make peace between
1 CONT        her sons, but who, when it became necessary to take sides with one
1 CONT        or the other could not hesitate in her devotion to the elder. Charles
1 CONT        was an affectionate son; it also appears that, in general, he was
1 CONT        helped to power by his extraordinary gift of personal attractiveness. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        Carloman died soon after this (4 December, 771), and a certain
1 CONT        letter from "the Monk Cathwulph", quoted by Bouquet (Recueil.
1 CONT        hist., V, 634), in enumerating the special blessings for which the
1 CONT        king was in duty bound to be grateful, says, 
1 CONT
1 CONT           Third . . . God has preserved you from the wiles of your
1 CONT           brother . . . . Fifth, and not the least, that God has removed
1 CONT           your brother from this earthly kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT        Carloman may not have been quite so malignant as the enthusiastic
1 CONT        partisans of Charles made him out, but the division of Pepin's
1 CONT        dominions was in itself an impediment to the growth of a strong
1 CONT        Frankish realm such as Charles needed for the unification of the
1 CONT        Christian Continent. Although Carloman had left two sons by his
1 CONT        wife, Gerberga, the Frankish law of inheritance gave no preference
1 CONT        to sons as against brother; left to their own choice, the Frankish
1 CONT        lieges, whether from love of Charles or for the fear which his name
1 CONT        already inspired, gladly accepted him for their king. Gerberga and
1 CONT        her children fled to the Lombard court of Pavia. In the mean while
1 CONT        complications had arisen in Charles' foreign policy which made his
1 CONT        newly established supremacy at home doubly opportune. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        From his father Charles had inherited the title "Patricius Romanus"
1 CONT        which carried with it a special obligation to protect the temporal
1 CONT        rights of the Holy See. The nearest and most menacing neighbour of
1 CONT        St. Peter's Patrimony was Desidarius (Didier), King of the
1 CONT        Lombards, and it was with this potentate that the dowager Bertha
1 CONT        had arranged a matrimonial alliance for her elder son. The pope had
1 CONT        solid temporal reasons for objecting to this arrangement. Moreover,
1 CONT        Charles was already, in foro conscientiae, if not in Frankish law,
1 CONT        wedded to Himiltrude. In defiance of the pope's protest (PL
1 CONT        98:250), Charles married Desiderata, daughter of Desiderius (770),
1 CONT        three years later he repudiated her and married Hildegarde, the
1 CONT        beautiful Swabian. Naturally, Desiderius was furious at this insult,
1 CONT        and the dominions of the Holy See bore the first brunt of his wrath. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        But Charles had to defend his own borders against the heathen as
1 CONT        well as to protect Rome against the Lombard. To the north of
1 CONT        Austrasia lay Frisia, which seems to have been in some equivocal
1 CONT        way a dependency, and to the east of Frisia, from the left bank of the
1 CONT        Ems (about the present Holland-Westphalia frontier), across the
1 CONT        valley of the Weser and Aller, and still eastward to the left bank of
1 CONT        the Elbe, extended the country of the Saxons, who in no fashion
1 CONT        whatever acknowledged any allegiance to the Frankish kings. In 772
1 CONT        these Saxons were a horde of aggressive pagans offering to
1 CONT        Christian missionaries no hope but that of martyrdom; bound
1 CONT        together, normally, by no political organization, and constantly
1 CONT        engaged in predatory incursions into the lands of the Franks. Their
1 CONT        language seems to have been very like that spoken by the Egberts
1 CONT        and Ethelreds of Britain, but the work of their Christian cousin, St.
1 CONT        Boniface, had not affected them as yet; they worshipped the gods of
1 CONT        Walhalla, united in solemn sacrifice -- sometimes human -- to
1 CONT        Irminsul (Igdrasail), the sacred tree which stood at Eresburg, and
1 CONT        were still slaying Christian missionaries when their kinsmen in
1 CONT        Britain were holding church synods and building cathedrals. Charles
1 CONT        could brook neither their predatory habits nor their heathenish
1 CONT        intolerance; it was impossible, moreover, to make permanent peace
1 CONT        with them while they followed the old Teutonic life of free village
1 CONT        communities. He made his first expedition into their country in July,
1 CONT        772, took Eresburg by storm, and burned Irminsul. It was in January
1 CONT        of this same year that Pope Stephen III died, and Adrian I, an
1 CONT        opponent of Desiderius, was elected. The new pope was almost
1 CONT        immediately assailed by the Lombard king, who seized three minor
1 CONT        cities of the Patrimony of St. Peter, threatened Ravenna itself, and
1 CONT        set about organizing a plot within the Curia. Paul Afiarta, the papal
1 CONT        chamberlain, detected acting as the Lombard's secret agent, was
1 CONT        seized and put to death. The Lombard army advanced against Rome,
1 CONT        but quailed before the spiritual weapons of the Church, while
1 CONT        Adrian sent a legate into Gaul to claim the aid of of the Patrician. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        Thus it was that Charles, resting at Thionville after his Saxon
1 CONT        campaign, was urgently reminded of the rough work that awaited his
1 CONT        hand south of the Alps. Desiderius' embassy reached him soon after
1 CONT        Adrian's. He did not take it for granted that the right was all upon
1 CONT        Adrian's side; besides, he may have seen here an opportunity make
1 CONT        some amends for his repudiation of the Lombard princess. Before
1 CONT        taking up arms for the Holy See, therefore, he sent commissioners
1 CONT        into Italy to make enquiries and when Desiderius pretended that the
1 CONT        seizure of the papal cities was in effect only the legal foreclosure of
1 CONT        a mortgage, Charles promptly offered to redeem them by a money
1 CONT        payment. But Desiderius refused the money, and as Charles'
1 CONT        commissioners reported in favour of Adrian, the only course left
1 CONT        was war. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        In the spring of 773 Charles summoned the whole military strength
1 CONT        of the Franks for a great invasion of Lombardy. He was slow to
1 CONT        strike, but he meant to strike hard. Data for any approximate
1 CONT        estimate of his numerical strength are lacking, but it is certain that
1 CONT        the army, in order to make the descent more swiftly, crossed the
1 CONT        Alps by two passes: Mont Cenis and the Great St. Bernard. Einhard,
1 CONT        who accompanied the king over Mont Cenis (the St. Bernard
1 CONT        column was led by Duke Bernhard), speaks feelingly of the marvels
1 CONT        and perils of the passage. The invaders found Desiderius waiting for
1 CONT        them, entrenched at Susa; they turned his flank and put the Lombard
1 CONT        army to utter rout. Leaving all the cities of the plains to their fate,
1 CONT        Desiderius rallied part of his forces in Pavia, his walled capital,
1 CONT        while his son Adalghis, with the rest, occupied Verona. Charles,
1 CONT        having been joined by Duke Bernhard, took the forsaken cities on
1 CONT        his way and then completely invested Pavia (September, 773),
1 CONT        whence Otger, the faithful attendant of Gerberga, could look with
1 CONT        trembling upon the array of his countrymen. Soon after Christmas
1 CONT        Charles withdrew from the siege a portion of the army which he
1 CONT        employed in the capture of Verona. Here he found Gerberga and her
1 CONT        children; as to what became of them, history is silent; they probably
1 CONT        entered the cloister. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        What history does record with vivid eloquence is the first visit of
1 CONT        Charles to the Eternal City. There everything was done to give his
1 CONT        entry as much as possible the air of a triumph in ancient Rome. The
1 CONT        judges met him thirty miles from the city; the militia laid at the feet
1 CONT        of their great patrician the banner of Rome and hailed him as their
1 CONT        imperator. Charles himself forgot pagan Rome and prostrated
1 CONT        himself to kiss the threshold of the Apostles, and then spent seven
1 CONT        days in conference with the successor of Peter. It was then that he
1 CONT        undoubtedly formed many great designs for the glory of God and the
1 CONT        exaltation of Holy Church, which, in spite of human weaknesses
1 CONT        and, still more, ignorance, he afterwards did his best to realize. His
1 CONT        coronation as the successor of Constantine did not take place until
1 CONT        twenty-six years later, but his consecration as first champion of the
1 CONT        Catholic Church took place at Easter, 774. Soon after this (June,
1 CONT        774) Pavia fell, Desiderius was banished, Adalghis became a
1 CONT        fugitive at the Byzantine court, and Charles, assuming the crown of
1 CONT        Lombardy, renewed to Adrian the donation of of territory made by
1 CONT        Pepin the Short after his defeat of Aistulph. (This donation is now
1 CONT        generally admitted, as well as the original gift of Pepin at Kiersy in
1 CONT        752. The so-called "Privilegium Hadriani pro Carolo" granting him
1 CONT        full right to nominate the pope and to invest all bishops is a
1 CONT        forgery.) 
1 CONT
1 CONT        To the Baptism of Wittekind (774-785) 
1 CONT
1 CONT        The next twenty years of Charles' life may be considered as one long
1 CONT        warfare. They are filled with an astounding series of rapid marches
1 CONT        from end to end of a continent intersected by mountains, morasses,
1 CONT        and forests, and scantily provided with roads. It would seem that the
1 CONT        key to his long series of victories, won almost as much by moral
1 CONT        ascendancy as by physical or mental superiority, is to be found in the
1 CONT        inspiration communicated to his Frankish champion by Pope Adrian
1 CONT        I. Weiss (Weltgesch., 11, 549) enumerates fifty-three distinct
1 CONT        campaigns of Charlemagne; of these it is possible to point to only
1 CONT        twelve or fourteen which were not undertaken principally or entirely
1 CONT        in execution of his mission as the soldier and protector of the
1 CONT        Church. In his eighteen campaigns against the Saxons Charles was
1 CONT        more or less actuated by the desire to extinguish what he and his
1 CONT        people regarded as a form of devil-worship, no less odious to them
1 CONT        than the fetishism of Central Africa is to us. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        While he was still in Italy the Saxons, irritated but not subdued by
1 CONT        the fate of Eresburg and of Irminsul had risen in arms, harried the
1 CONT        country of the Hessian Franks, and burned many churches; that of St.
1 CONT        Boniface at Fritzlar, being of stone, had defeated their efforts.
1 CONT        Returning to the north, Charles sent a preliminary column of cavalry
1 CONT        into the enemy's country while he held a council of the realm at
1 CONT        Kiersy (Quercy) in September, 774, at which it was decided that the
1 CONT        Saxons (Westfali, Ostfali, and Angrarii) must be presented with the
1 CONT        alternative of baptism or death. The northeastern campaigns of the
1 CONT        next seven years had for their object a conquest so decisive as to
1 CONT        make the execution of this policy feasible. The year 775 saw the
1 CONT        first of a series of Frankish military colonies, on the ancient Roman
1 CONT        plan established at Sigeburg among the Westfali. Charles next
1 CONT        subdued, temporarily at least, the Ostali, whose chieftain, Hessi,
1 CONT        having accepted baptism, ended his life in the monastery of Fulda
1 CONT        (see BONIFACE, SAINT; FULDA). Then, a Frankish camp at
1 CONT        Lübbecke on the Weser having been surprised by the Saxons, and its
1 CONT        garrison slaughtered, Charles turned again westward, once more
1 CONT        routed the Westfali, and received their oaths of submission. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        At this stage (776) the affairs of Lombardy interrupted the Saxon
1 CONT        crusade. Areghis of Beneventum, son-in-law of the vanquished
1 CONT        Desiderius, had formed a plan with his brother-in-law Adalghis
1 CONT        (Adelchis), then an exile at Constantinople, by which the latter was
1 CONT        to make a descent upon Italy, backed by the Eastern emperor; Adrian
1 CONT        was at the same time involved in a quarrel with the three Lombard
1 CONT        dukes, Reginald of Clusium, Rotgaud of Friuli, and Hildebrand of
1 CONT        Spoleto. The archbishop of Ravenna, who called himself "primate"
1 CONT        and "exarch of Italy", was also attempting to found an independent
1 CONT        principality at the expense of the papal state but was finally subdued
1 CONT        in 776, and his successor compelled to be content with the title of
1 CONT        "Vicar" or representative of the pope. The junction of the aforesaid
1 CONT        powers, all inimical to the pope and the Franks, while Charles was
1 CONT        occupied in Westphalia, was only prevented by the death of
1 CONT        Constantine Copronymus in September, 775 (see BYZANTINE
1 CONT        EMPIRE). After winning over Hildebrand and Reginald by
1 CONT        diplomacy, Charles descended into Lombardy by the Brenner Pass
1 CONT        (spring of 776), defeated Rotgaud, and leaving garrisons and
1 CONT        governors, or counts (comites), as they were termed, in the
1 CONT        reconquered cities of the Duchy of Friuli, hastened back to Saxony.
1 CONT        There the Frankish garrison had been forced to evacuate Eresburg,
1 CONT        while the siege of Sigeburg was so unexpectedly broken up as to
1 CONT        give occasion later to a legend of angelic intervention in favour of
1 CONT        the Christians. As usual, the almost incredible suddenness of the
1 CONT        king's reappearance and the moral effect of his presence quieted the
1 CONT        ragings of the heathen. Charles then divided the Saxon territory into
1 CONT        Missionary districts. At the great spring hosting (champ de Mai) of
1 CONT        Paderborn, in 777, many Saxon converts were baptized; Wittekind
1 CONT        (Widukind), however, already the leader and afterwards the popular
1 CONT        hero of the Saxons, had fled to his brother-in-law, Sigfrid the Dane. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        The episode of the invasion of Spain comes next in chronological
1 CONT        order. The condition of the venerable Iberian Church, still suffering
1 CONT        under Moslem domination, appealed strongly to the king's sympathy.
1 CONT        In 777 there came to Paderborn three Moorish emirs, enemies of the
1 CONT        Ommeyad Abderrahman, the Moorish King of Cordova. These emirs
1 CONT        did homage to Charles and proposed to him an invasion of Northern
1 CONT        Spain; one of the, Ibn-el-Arabi, promised to bring to the invaders'
1 CONT        assistance a force of Berber auxiliaries from Africa; the other two
1 CONT        promised to exert their powerful influence at Barcelona and
1 CONT        elsewhere north of the Ebro. Accordingly, in the spring of 778,
1 CONT        Charles, with a host of crusaders, speaking many tongues, and which
1 CONT        numbered among its constituents even a quota of Lombards, moved
1 CONT        towards the Pyrenees. His trusted lieutenant, Duke Bernhard. with
1 CONT        one division, entered Spain by the coast. Charles himself marched
1 CONT        through the mountain passes straight to Pampelona. But
1 CONT        Ibn-el-Arabi, who had prematurely brought on his army of Berbers,
1 CONT        was assassinated by the emissary of Abderrahman, and though
1 CONT        Pampelona was razed, and Barcelona and other cities fell, Saragossa
1 CONT        held out. Apart from the moral effect of this campaign upon the
1 CONT        Moslem rulers of Spain, its result was insignificant, though the
1 CONT        famous ambuscade in which perished Roland, the great Paladin, at
1 CONT        the Pass of Roncesvalles, furnished to the medieval world the
1 CONT        material for its most glorious and influential epic, the "Chanson de
1 CONT        Roland". 
1 CONT
1 CONT        Much more important to posterity were the next succeeding events
1 CONT        which continued and decided the long struggle in Saxony. During
1 CONT        the Spanish crusade Wittekind had returned from his exile, bringing
1 CONT        with him Danish allies, and was now ravaging Hesse; the Rhine
1 CONT        valley from Deutz to Andenach was a prey to the Saxon
1 CONT        "devil-worshipers"; the Christian missionaries were scattered or in
1 CONT        hiding. Charles gathered his hosts at Düren, in June, 779, and
1 CONT        stormed Wittekind's entrenched camp at Bocholt, after which
1 CONT        campaign he seems to have considered Saxony a fairly subdued
1 CONT        country. At any rate, the "Saxon Capitulary" (see CAPITULARIES)
1 CONT        of 781 obliged all Saxons not only to accept baptism (and this on
1 CONT        the pain of death) but also to pay tithes, as the Franks did for the
1 CONT        support of the Church; moreover it confiscated a large amount of
1 CONT        property for the benefit of the missions. This was Wittekind's last
1 CONT        opportunity to restore the national independence and paganism; his
1 CONT        people, exasperated against the Franks and their God, eagerly rushed
1 CONT        to arms. At Suntal on the Weser, Charles being absent, they defeated
1 CONT        a Frankish army killing two royal legates and five Counts. But
1 CONT        Wittekind committed the error of enlisting as allies the
1 CONT        non-Teutonic Sorbs from beyond the Saale; race-antagonism soon
1 CONT        weakened his forces, and the Saxon hosts melted away. Of the
1 CONT        so-called "Massacre of Verdun" (783) it is fair to say that the 4500
1 CONT        Saxons who perished were not prisoners of war; legally, they were
1 CONT        ringleaders in a rebellion, selected as such from a number of their
1 CONT        fellow rebels. Wittekind himself escaped beyond the Elbe. It was not
1 CONT        until after another defeat of the Saxons at Detmold, and again at
1 CONT        Osnabrück, on the "Hill of Slaughter", that Wittekind acknowledged
1 CONT        the God of Charles the stronger than Odin. In 785 Wittekind
1 CONT        received baptism at Attigny, and Charles stood godfather. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        Last Steps to the Imperial Throne (785-800) 
1 CONT
1 CONT        The summer of 783 began a new period in the life of Charles, in
1 CONT        which signs begin to appear of his less amiable traits. It was in this
1 CONT        year, signalized, according to the chroniclers, by unexampled heat
1 CONT        and a pestilence, that the two queens died, Bertha, the king's mother,
1 CONT        and Hildegarde, his second (or his third) wife. Both of these women,
1 CONT        the former in particular, had exercised over him a strong influence
1 CONT        for good. Within a few months the king married Fastrada, daughter
1 CONT        of an Austrasian count. The succeeding years were, comparatively
1 CONT        speaking, years of harvest after the stupendous period of ploughing
1 CONT        and sowing that had gone before; and Charles' nature was of a type
1 CONT        that appears to best advantage in storm and stress. What was to be
1 CONT        the Western Empire of the Middle Ages was already hewn out in the
1 CONT        rough when Wittekind received baptism. From that date until the
1 CONT        coronation of Charles at Rome, in 800, his military work was
1 CONT        chiefly in suppressing risings of the newly conquered or quelling the
1 CONT        discontents of jealous subject princes. Thrice in these fifteen years
1 CONT        did the Saxons rise, only to be defeated. Tassilo, Duke of Bavaria,
1 CONT        had been a more or less rebellious vassal ever since the beginning of
1 CONT        his reign, and Charles now made use of the pope's influence,
1 CONT        exercised through the powerful bishops of Freising, Salzburg, and
1 CONT        Regensburg (Ratisbon), to bring him to terms. In 786 a Thuringian
1 CONT        revolt was quelled by the timely death, blinding, and banishment of
1 CONT        its leaders. Next year the Lombard prince, Areghis, having fortified
1 CONT        himself at Salerno, had actually been crowned King of the Lombards
1 CONT        when Charles descended upon him at Beneventum, received his
1 CONT        submission, and took his son Grimwald as a hostage, after which,
1 CONT        finding that Tassilo had been secretly associated with the conspiracy
1 CONT        of the Lombards, he invaded Bavaria from three sides with three
1 CONT        armies drawn from at least five nationalities. Once more the
1 CONT        influence of the Holy See settled the Bavarian question in Charles'
1 CONT        favour; Adrian threatened Tassilo with excommunication if he
1 CONT        persisted in rebellion, and as the Duke's own subjects refused to
1 CONT        follow him to the field, he personally made submission, did homage,
1 CONT        and in return received from Charles a new lease of his duchy
1 CONT        (October, 787). 
1 CONT
1 CONT        During this period the national discontent with Fastrada culminated
1 CONT        in a plot in which Pepin the Hunchback, Charles' son by Himiltrude,
1 CONT        was implicated, and though his life was spared through his father's
1 CONT        intercession, Pepin spent what remained of his days in a monastery.
1 CONT        Another son of Charles (Carloman, afterwards called Pepin, and
1 CONT        crowned King of Lombardy at Rome in 781, on the occasion of an
1 CONT        Easter visit by the king, at which time also his brother Louis was
1 CONT        crowned King of Aquitaine) served his father in dealing with the
1 CONT        Avars, a pagan danger on the frontier, compared with which the
1 CONT        invasion of Septimania by the Saracens (793) was but an
1 CONT        insignificant incident of border warfare. These Avars, probably of
1 CONT        Turanian blood, occupied the territories north of the Save and west
1 CONT        of the Theiss. Tassilo had invited their assistance against his
1 CONT        overlord; and after the Duke's final submission Charles invaded
1 CONT        their country and conquered it as far as the Raab (791). By the
1 CONT        capture of the famous "Ring" of the Avars, with its nine concentric
1 CONT        circles, Charles came into possession of vast quantities of gold and
1 CONT        silver, parts of the plunder which these barbarians had been
1 CONT        accumulating for two centuries. In this campaign King Pepin of
1 CONT        Lombardy cooperated with his father, with forces drawn from Italy;
1 CONT        the later stages of this war (which may be considered the last of
1 CONT        Charles' great wars) were left in the hands of the younger king. 
1 CONT
1 CONT        The last stages by which the story of Charles' career is brought to its
1 CONT        climax touch upon the exclusive spiritual domain of the Church. He
1 CONT
0 @N6000000003949643870@ NOTE  Charlemagne, or Charles the Great, CAROLINGIAN king of the FRANKS, came to rule over most of Europe and assumed (800) the title of Roman emperor. He is sometimes regarded as the founder of the HOLY ROMAN EMPIRE. In 768 he and his
1 CONC  brother Carloman inherited the Frankish kingdom (most of present-day France and a part of western Germany) from their father PEPIN THE SHORT. The entire kingdom passed to Charlemagne when Carloman died in 771. He inherited great 
1 CONC wealth and a strong military organization from his father and brother. He used these assets to double the territory under Carolingian control. In 772 he opened his offensive against the SAXONS, and for more than three decades he p
1 CONC ursued a ruthless policy aimed at subjugating them and converting them to Christianity. Almost every year Charlemagne attacked one or another region of Saxon territory. --4,500 Saxons were executed on a single day in 782--and depo
1 CONC rtations were used to discourage the stubborn. The Saxons proved to be a far more difficult enemy than any of the other peoples subjugated by Charlemagne. For example, the LOMBARDS were conquered in a single extended campaign 773-
1 CONC 74), after which Charlemagne assumed the title "king of the Lombards." In 788 he absorbed the duchy of Bavaria, and soon thereafter he launched an offensive against the AVAR empire. The Avars succumbed within a decade, yielding Ch
1 CONC arlemagne a vast hoard of gold and silver. After one disastrous campaign (778) against the Muslims in Spain, Charlemagne left the southwestern front to his son Louis, (later Emperor LOUIS I) who, with the help of local Christian r
1 CONC ulers, conquered Barcelona in 801 and controlled much of Catalonia by 814. On Christmas Day, 800, Charlemagne accepted the title of emperor and was crowned by Pope LEO III. For several years after he regarded the imperial title of
1 CONC  being of little value. Moreover, he intended to divide his lands and titles among his sons, as was the Frankish custom. At his death on Jan. 28, 814, however, only one son, Louis, survived; Louis therefore assumed control of the 
1 CONC entire Frankish empire.
0 @N6000000004058923334@ NOTE Charlemagne Emperor Of The HOLY ROMAN EMPIRE; CHARLEMAGNE (742?-814). (Note: "By the sword and the cross," Charlemagne (Charles the Great) became master of Western Europe. It was falling into decay when Charlemagne became joint ki
1 CONC ng of the Franks in 768. Except in the monasteries, people had all but forgotten education and the arts. Boldly Charlemagne conquered barbarians and kings alike. By restoring the roots of learning and order, he preserved many poli
1 CONC tical rights and revived culture). He was buried at Notre Dame D'aix La Chapelle, Austrasia. He was born on 4/2/742 at Aix La Chapelle, Austrasia. He married Hildegard Empress Of The HOLY ROMAN EMPIRE circa 772 at Aachen, Rhinelan
1 CONC d, Prussia. He died on 1/28/814 at Aix La Chapelle, Austrasia, at age 71.
1 CONT
