0 @N6000000002187755152@ NOTE Henry, Count of Portugal
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry of Burgundy (1066–1112) was Count of Portugal from 1093 to his death. He was the son of Henry of Burgundy, heir of Robert I, Duke of Burgundy, and brother of Eudes I, Duke of Burgundy. His name is Henri in modern French, Hen
1 CONC ricus in Latin, Enrique in modern Spanish and Henrique in modern Portuguese.
1 CONT
1 CONT Main article: The Establishment of the Monarchy in Portugal. 
1 CONT As a younger son, Henry had little chances of acquiring fortune and titles by inheritance, thus he joined the Reconquista against the Moors in the Iberian Peninsula. He helped king Alfonso VI of Castile conquer modern Galicia and 
1 CONC the north of Portugal and in reward he married his daughter Teresa of León in 1093. With the marriage, Henry also became Count of Portugal, then a fiefdom of the Kingdom of León.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Teresa, Henry had several sons. The only one to survive childhood was Afonso Henriques, who became the second Count of Portugal in 1112. However, the young man Afonso was energetic and expanded his dominions at the expense of
1 CONC  Muslims. In 1139, he declared himself King of Portugal after reneging the subjugation to Leon, in open confrontation with his mother.
