0 @N6000000002187750956@ NOTE Alfonso IX of Leon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso IX of León (August 15, 1171 – September 23 or 24, 1230; ruled from 1188–1230), first cousin of Alfonso VIII of Castile, and numbered next to him as being a junior member of the family, is said by Ibn Khaldun to have been c
1 CONC alled the Baboso or "Slobberer", because he was subject to fits of rage during which he foamed at the mouth.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso was the only son of King Ferdinand II of Leon and Urraca of Portugal. Though he took a part in the work of the reconquest, this king is chiefly remembered by the difficulties into which his successive marriages led him wit
1 CONC h the pope. He was first married in 1191 to his cousin Teresa of Portugal, who bore him two daughters, and a son who died young.
1 CONT
1 CONT The marriage was declared null by the pope, to whom Alfonso paid no attention till he was presumably tired of his wife. It cannot have been his conscience which constrained him to leave Teresa, for his next step was to marry Beren
1 CONC guela of Castile in 1197, who was his second cousin. For this act of contumacy the king and kingdom were placed under interdict.
1 CONT
1 CONT The pope was, however, compelled to modify his measures by the threat that if the people could not obtain the services of religion they would not support the clergy, and that heresy would spread. The king was left under interdict 
1 CONC personally, but to that he showed himself indifferent, and he had the support of his clergy. Berenguela left him after the birth of five children, and the king then returned to Teresa, to whose daughters he left his kingdom by wil
1 CONC l.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso's children by Teresa of Portugal were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancha (c. 1192-1270) 
1 CONT Dulce, also called Aldonza (c. 1195-c.1243) 
1 CONT Fernando (1204-August 1214) 
1 CONT His eldest daughter, Sancha, was engaged to her cousin King Henry I of Castile, but Henry died in 1217 before the marriage could be solemnized. Wanting to disinherit his eldest son, Fernando, King Alfonso invited John of Brienne t
1 CONC o marry his daughter Sancha and thus inherit the Leonese throne. However, Queen Berenguela convinced John of Brienne to marry one of her daughters instead. Though she was the nominal heiress on her father's death in 1230, Sancha w
1 CONC as easily set aside by Berenguela and Fernando. Sancha became a nun at Cozollos, where she died in 1270; she was later beatified. Her sister Dulce-Aldonza spent her life with their mother in Portugal.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso's children by Berenguela of Castile were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Leonor (1198/99-October 31, 1210) 
1 CONT King Fernando III (1200-1252) 
1 CONT Berenguela (1201-1237), married John of Brienne 
1 CONT Alfonso, Lord of Molina (1203-1272) 
1 CONT Constanza (1205-September 7, 1242), became a nun at Las Huelgas 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Florez, Enrique. Reinas Catolicas, 1761 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ferdinand II King of Leon
1 CONT 1188–1230 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ferdinand III 
