0 @N6000000005598882376@ NOTE William is styled as handome, courteous, complex and violatile.  Addit ionally, he has been described as 'intelligent, gifted, artistic, idea listic with an instaiable thirst for sensual passion and adventurer' .  He is also descr
1 CONC ibed as an'priapic old aristocrat who used to disqu ise himself as a death mute to seduce the wives of absent nobles in lo cal inns'.  He is regarded as the first of the 'troubadours' and a poe t of considerable skill.  His roggis
1 CONC h behavior offended the sensibilit ies of the church and he was subsequently excommunicated.  Because of her violent mood swings, Guillaume had no great difficulity in convinc ing the church that his marriage to Ermengarde should 
1 CONC be annulled.
1 CONT
1 CONT In about 1095, Guillaume considered taking the Cross and making a crus ade to Palestine, but came to think better of the idea.  In 1095, Guil laume marched on Toulouse.  At the time, he mortgaged the area to his wife Philippa's co
1 CONC usin Bertrand.  He soon left Philippa as his regent in Poitiers and took up the Cross and headed east.  He was a Crusader in 1101 who was in company with Stephen of Burgandy, Robert of Flander s, Robert of Normandie and Stephen of
1 CONC  Blois.  He witnessed his army be ing cut to pieces by the Turks at Heraclea.  After the massacre, he li ngered in Antioch and Jerusalem on his way home, thrilled by Arab cult ure and translations of Greek and Roman classics.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon his arrrival back in Toulouse, he took up poetry and is also kno w as 'the first of the troubadours', one of the first known singer-poe ts in the vernacular.  Guillaume's court soon gained a reputation for literature.  Guilla
1 CONC ume maintained a castle at Niort.  As his wife Phil ippa became obsessed with the Abbey at Fontevrault, he began to turn h is passionate desires elsewhere.  By 1115, he had developed a a violen t passion for the wife of his vassel
1 CONC  Aimery I de Rochefoucauld, the Vi scount of Châtellerault.  Her name was Dangerosa de L' Isle Bouchard .  Guillaume passed on his domains in Toulouse to his son Guillaume, b ut the ducal authority that was attached to them had be
1 CONC en undermined.
