0 @N6000000003949636299@ NOTE King of (Transjurane) Burgundy from his election in 888 until his death.
1 CONT     Rudolph belonged to the Welf family and was the son of Conrad, count of Auxerre, from whom he inherited the lay abbacy of St Maurice en Valais, making him the most powerful magnate in Upper Burgundy - present-day western Switz
1 CONC erland and the Franche Comté.
1 CONT     After the deposition and death of Charles the Fat, the nobles and leading clergy of Upper Burgundy met at St Maurice and elected Rudolph as king. Apparently on the basis of this election, Rudolph claimed the whole of Lotharing
1 CONC ia, taking much of modern Lorraine and Alsace - but his claim was contested by Arnulf of Carinthia, the new king of East Francia or Germany, who rapidly forced Rudolph to abandon Lotharingia in return for recognition as king of Bu
1 CONC rgundy. However , hostilities between Rudolph and Arnulf seem to have continued intermittently until 894.
1 CONT     Rudolph's relationships with his other neighbours were friendlier. His sister Adelaide married Richard the Justiciar , duke of Burgundy (the present day Burgundy, part of west Francia), and his daughter, another Adelaide, marr
1 CONC ied Louis the Blind of Provence (Lower Burgundy).    Rudolph was succeeded as king of Burgundy by his son, Rudol ph II.
