0 @N6000000007329715179@ NOTE Kilde: Wurts, John S., Magna Charta: The  Pedigrees of the Barons,
1 CONT Philadelphia, PA: Brookfield Publishing Co,   1942.
1 CONT Kilde: date: [Ref: Wurts p428], parents:   [Ref:  Wurts p428]
1 CONT Kilde: names: [Ref: Wurts p428]  Kilde: date: [Ref: Wurts p428]  //
1 CONT [Alan B. Wilson, <abwilson -at- uclink2.berkeley.edu>] : 'The   descent
1 CONT from Charlemagne through Hugues 'l'Abbe'  appears in Turton, The
1 CONT Plantagenet Ancestry, p. 6.  [Turton  cites Louis de Mas Latrie,   Tresor
1 CONT le  chronologie, 1889]. It also is outlined in Michel Call's Pedigree
1 CONT Chart  No. 502 and Family Group Sheet No. 935.  Call cites Turton and
1 CONT Collett's  collection of unpublished pedigree charts.'
1 CONT [Nat Taylor, <ntaylor -at- fas.harvard.edu>] : ' Turton cannot be   cited
1 CONT as an authority; his source, the 'TrÚsor de chronologie', must also   be
1 CONT incorrect if it indeed identifies Hugh the Abbot with Hugh,
1 CONT illegitimate  son of Charlemagne.  Hugh the Abbot (d. 886) was, as Cam
1 CONT Dyck said correctly, son of   Conrad,  count of Paris and Auxerre (d. ca.
1 CONT 862), of the Welf family (see  Europaische Stammtafeln III:736) and
1 CONT Adelaide, daughter of Hugh,   count of  Tours (Adelaid married second
1 CONT Robert the Strong, and was mother of   the  first Robertine kings Odo and
1 CONT Robert I, who were thus Hugh the   Abbot's  half-brothers.  Hugh of Tours
1 CONT was probably of the Etichonid family   (see  Vollmer, 'Die Etichonen' in
1 CONT Studien und Vorarbeiten zur Geschichte   des  grossfrankischen Adels, ed.
1 CONT Gerd Tellenbach (1957), especially the   chart  at p. 183). It is through
1 CONT this Hugh of Tours (d. 826) that the given   name  'Hugh' passed both to
1 CONT the Capetians, and (through the Welf brother of   Hugh  the Abbot) to the
1 CONT early Burgundian dynasty (where it appeared in the  person of Hugh 'the
1 CONT Black', Duke of Burgundy, d. 952), and also,   through  Adelaide's
1 CONT sister's marriage to Emperor Lothar I, to the Carolingians   and  their
1 CONT successors in Italy and Savoy. The Welf Hugh the Abbot was   generally
1 CONT believed to have died s.p..  His identification as the father of
1 CONT Petronilla, wife of Tertullus, ancestor of the Angevins, is based on   a
1 CONT mistaken reading of the medieval text 'Gesta consulum andegavensis',
1 CONT edited by Louis Halphen and Rene Poupardin (Paris, 1913).  The text
1 CONT actually says that she was a relative of Hugh the Abbot and doesn't   say
1 CONT he  was her father.  Bernard Bachrach discusses the accuracy of this text
1 CONT in  his article 'Some Origins of the Angevin Dynasty,'  Medieval
1 CONT Prosopography  10/2 (1989), 1-23.  He corrects it by drawing a dotted
1 CONT line  between Hugh the Abbot and Petronilla in his  genealogical table of
1 CONT the Angevins.  But at any rate Hugh was not the   son  of Charlemagne.
1 CONT That was another Hugh, abbot of Saint-Quentin, who   died  in battle on
1 CONT June 14, 844 (see Bernard W. Scholz,  Carolingian   Chronicles  (Ann
1 CONT Arbor, 1970), p. 207, note 7). But this is all nitpicking as the
1 CONT original point of your message, to demonstrate multiple descents of   the
1 CONT earliest Anglo-Norman kings of England from Charlemagne, is perfectly
1 CONT correct.  The problem is, of  course, that mistaken identifications made
1 CONT by Turton and others   continue  to make the rounds in people's databases
1 CONT and on their pedigree charts   and  on the usenet even when contradicted
1 CONT by perfectly clear, solid   scholarly  work.  //
